"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_mathematics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_mathematics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_mathematics_en"][4]={"university_mathematics_en:M4-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M4-L1 – Compulsory elective: transformer mathematics, attention and language models<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Choice of study: AI, computer science and specialised fields<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> University studies (elective /\nspecialisation)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L5<\/a> (matrices), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L7<\/a> (probabilities), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L10&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L10<\/a> (information theory), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L11&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L11<\/a> (neural networks), and,\nwhere appropriate, <a href=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L6<\/a>\n(optimisation)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Advanced study or compulsory elective, from\nadvanced Bachelor’s to Master’s level.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it about?<\/h2>\n<p>A language model does not process strings of characters directly as\nsentences. Instead, it uses discrete units called tokens and converts\nthem into numerical vectors. A transformer can relate different\npositions in a sequence to one another. Its central mechanism is a\nweighted combination of information: <strong>attention<\/strong>. The\nmathematical foundations are matrix multiplication, the dot product,\nnormalisation and differentiable optimisation. This lesson explains what\nis actually computed – and why neither automatic truth nor human\nunderstanding of language follows from this process.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and intuition<\/h2>\n<p>In the sentence ‘The bank is by the river’, the word ‘river’ helps\ndetermine which meaning of ‘bank’ is plausible during processing.\nTechnically, this is not decided by a built-in dictionary. A model\nconstructs vectors and learns which positions receive greater weight in\nrelation to other positions. Attention is a <strong>computational\nmechanism for weighting information<\/strong>, not a guarantee of\ngrammatical correctness or factual reliability.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols – read before the formulae<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Read aloud<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Distinction<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{i}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘x sub i’<\/td>\n<td>Vector representation of the token at position i<\/td>\n<td>Not the original word<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_{i}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘q sub i’<\/td>\n<td>Query vector for a position: what information is sought?<\/td>\n<td>Not a browser search query<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_{j}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘k sub j’<\/td>\n<td>Key vector for a position: how can it be identified?<\/td>\n<td>Not an encryption key<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{j}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘v sub j’<\/td>\n<td>Value vector: information to be combined<\/td>\n<td>Not a truth value<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>·<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘dot product’<\/td>\n<td>Measures a bilinear similarity in this calculation<\/td>\n<td>Not necessarily cosine similarity<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_{k}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘d sub k’<\/td>\n<td>Number of components in a key or query vector<\/td>\n<td>Not the length of the token sequence<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_{ij}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘s sub i j’<\/td>\n<td>Normalised comparison score between query i and key j<\/td>\n<td>Not yet a probability<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exp<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘exponential function’<\/td>\n<td>Converts real-valued comparison scores into positive values<\/td>\n<td>Not percentage arithmetic<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha _{ij}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘alpha sub i j’<\/td>\n<td>Attention weight between positions i and j<\/td>\n<td>Not a causal influence<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Σ<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘sum’<\/td>\n<td>Adds the contributions from all permitted positions<\/td>\n<td>Not an integral over continuous time<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">softmax<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{softmax}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘softmax’<\/td>\n<td>Converts finitely many numbers into positive weights that sum to\none<\/td>\n<td>Not general statistical calibration<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q,K,V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘Q, K, V’<\/td>\n<td>Matrices of all queries, keys and values<\/td>\n<td>Q/K/V have a different meaning in M3-L12<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>⊤<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘transpose’<\/td>\n<td>Interchanges rows and columns<\/td>\n<td>Not the matrix inverse<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘n’<\/td>\n<td>Number of token positions under consideration<\/td>\n<td>Not the number of training examples<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From tokens to vectors<\/h2>\n<p>A tokeniser divides text into units. A token is not always a word,\nnor is it always a single character. Each token index is first assigned\na learned numerical vector, the <strong>embedding<\/strong>. The model\nalso needs information about order and position, because without such\ninformation attention alone does not interpret the positions as an\nordered sentence.<\/p>\n<p>For an input with n positions, we obtain a matrix X: each row\ncontains an embedding. Three new representations are generated from X\nusing learned matrices:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>K<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/msub><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=XW_Q,\\qquad K=XW_K,\\qquad V=XW_V\\text{.}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: ‘The query matrix is the input matrix multiplied by a\ntrained query weight matrix; the key and value matrices are obtained in\nthe same way.’ The matrices\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>K<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare parameters that the model changes during training. Their numbers of\ncolumns are an architectural choice.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scaled dot-product attention<\/h2>\n<p>For position i and the position j under consideration, the\ntransformer first computes:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_{ij}=\\frac{q_i\\cdot k_j}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\text{.}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: ‘Compare query i and key j using the dot product and\ndivide by the square root of the key dimension.’ The scaling factor\nhelps control the magnitude of the comparison scores when there are many\nvector components. It is not a universal guarantee against numerical\nproblems.<\/p>\n<p>The scores for a query are converted into weights using softmax:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><\/munder><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha_{ij}=\\frac{\\exp(s_{ij})}{\\sum_m\\exp(s_{im})}\\text{.}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: ‘Exponentiate the score for j and divide by the sum of\nthe exponentiated scores for all positions m that this query is allowed\nto attend to.’ For <strong>unmasked, permitted positions with finite\nscores<\/strong>, it follows that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha _{ij} &gt; 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nover all permitted positions,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_j\\alpha_{ij}=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith <strong>causal masking<\/strong> in autoregressive language models,\npositions may attend only to permitted context positions that are\nalready available at the current generation step. Softmax is therefore\nnormalised over permitted positions only; forbidden positions have\n<strong>zero weight<\/strong>, rather than a strictly positive weight. At\nleast one position must be permitted.<\/p>\n<p>The output vector is obtained by weighting the values:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>o<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_i=\\sum_j\\alpha_{ij}v_j\\text{.}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: ‘Add the value vectors, multiplying each by its attention\nweight.’ The result is a <strong>weighted mixture of vectors<\/strong>,\nnot simply the selected token with the highest score.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Fully worked example with two positions<\/h2>\n<p>We deliberately use just <strong>one component<\/strong> per query and\nkey:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_{k} = 1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{d_k}=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe query is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q = \\ln (2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(the natural logarithm of two). The two keys are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_{1} = 1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_{2} = 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTheir values are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{1} = 6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{2} = 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-mathematics-en/media/img/000058_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Two attention scores, ln two and zero, yield softmax weights of two thirds and one third and an output of four.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two attention scores, ln two and zero,\nyield softmax weights of two thirds and one third and an output of\nfour.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Normalised attention:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>2<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac23\\cdot6+\\frac13\\cdot0=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><mn>1<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><mn>1<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>2<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>o<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>2<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\ns_1&amp;=\\frac{q\\cdot k_1}{1}=\\ln(2),\\\\\ns_2&amp;=\\frac{q\\cdot k_2}{1}=0\\text{.}\\\\\n\\exp(s_1)&amp;=2,\\quad\\exp(s_2)=1,\\\\\n\\alpha_1&amp;=\\frac2{2+1}=\\frac23,\\\\\n\\alpha_2&amp;=\\frac1{2+1}=\\frac13\\text{.}\\\\\no&amp;=\\frac23\\cdot6+\\frac13\\cdot0=4\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: ‘The first position receives two thirds of the weight,\nthe second one third. The weighted sum of the transmitted values is\nfour.’ Here the values are one-dimensional; in real models, they are\nusually vectors with many components. The numbers have been chosen for\nteaching purposes and are not taken from a real language model.<\/p>\n<p>In matrix notation, the same mechanism for many positions is\nexpressed as follows:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Attention<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">softmax<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><msup><mi>K<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Attention}(Q,K,V)=\\operatorname{softmax}\\left(\\frac{QK^{\\mathrm{T}}}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right)V\\text{.}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Softmax acts <strong>row by row<\/strong> on the comparison scores;\nany masks are applied before softmax. The dimensions must be compatible:\nQ and K use the same comparison dimension, and K and V have the same\nnumber of rows.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Countercheck: a causal mask changes the normalised\nmixture<\/h3>\n<p>Suppose a single query has <strong>three<\/strong> keys with\ncomparison scores\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=(\\ln (2), 0, \\ln (3))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand one-dimensional values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=(6,0,100)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWithout a mask, the exponential values are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,1,3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\ntheir sum is 6. The output vector would therefore be:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mn>2<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mfrac><mn>3<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>o<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>2<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>3<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>52<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\alpha_{\\mathrm{unmasked}}&amp;=\\left(\\frac26,\\frac16,\\frac36\\right),\\\\\no_{\\mathrm{unmasked}}&amp;=\\frac26\\cdot6+\\frac16\\cdot0+\\frac36\\cdot100=52\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If the third position lies <strong>in the future<\/strong> relative to\nthe query under consideration, it must not be taken into account in\nautoregressive causal self-attention. This means that the third score is\nexcluded before softmax, formally, for example, by masking it with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe remaining weights are <strong>renormalised<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mn>2<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>o<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>2<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\alpha_{\\mathrm{masked}}&amp;=\\left(\\frac2{2+1},\\frac1{2+1},0\\right)\n=\\left(\\frac23,\\frac13,0\\right),\\\\\no_{\\mathrm{masked}}&amp;=\\frac23\\cdot6+\\frac13\\cdot0+0\\cdot100=4\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: ‘The future position receives not merely a smaller\nweight, but exactly zero; the permitted positions redistribute the\nentire weight among themselves.’ A mask value of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis idealised mathematical notation; actual implementations need a\ncorrect masking procedure and at least one permitted position per\nquery.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Numerical countercheck: computing softmax without\noverflow<\/h3>\n<p>Softmax is unchanged if <strong>the same value is subtracted from all\npermitted scores<\/strong>. For the maximum value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=\\max (s_j)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>ℓ<\/mi><\/munder><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>ℓ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha_j=\\frac{\\exp(s_j-m)}{\\sum_{\\ell}\\exp(s_{\\ell}-m)}\\text{.}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This avoids unnecessarily large exponential values. Consider an\nexample with scores\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>999<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=(1000,999)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>m<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>e<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>α<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>e<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mi>e<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.731059<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0.268941<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nm&amp;=1000,\\\\\n\\exp(s-m)&amp;=(\\exp(0),\\exp(-1))=\\left(1,\\frac1e\\right),\\\\\n\\alpha&amp;=\\left(\\frac e{e+1},\\frac1{e+1}\\right)\\approx(0.731059;0.268941)\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: ‘Instead of generating enormous exponential values, we\ncalculate the same distribution using zero and minus one.’ This is an\nessential practical measure for numerically stable attention, but it\ndoes not provide a complete guarantee against rounding, masking or\ntraining problems.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Multi-head attention, residual connections and training<\/h2>\n<p>Instead of computing a single attention mapping, a transformer often\ncomputes several <strong>heads<\/strong>, each with its own trained\nprojections. The results are combined and transformed linearly once\nmore. Different heads <em>can<\/em> learn different relationships; each\nindividual head is not guaranteed to have a fixed linguistic meaning.\nOther components include feedforward layers, normalisation and\n<strong>residual connections<\/strong> (adding the input information to a\nrepresentation that has undergone further processing).<\/p>\n<p>During training, a loss function is optimised, for example, the\nnegative log-probability of the next token in language modelling.\nParameter updates are determined from the derivatives of the loss\nfunction. The model produces a distribution over possible next tokens,\nbut it does not automatically produce a verified statement about the\nworld. Even a statistically plausible answer can be factually\nincorrect.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Computational costs, long contexts and limits of\napplicability<\/h2>\n<p>With dense attention, the comparison matrix for n positions contains\napproximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n^{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nentries. Doubling n quadruples this number in the simple model. This\ndescribes <strong>classical dense attention<\/strong>, not all modern\nvariants: sparse or blockwise attention, memory-optimised\nimplementations and other architectures change actual memory\nrequirements and running time.<\/p>\n<p>Attention weights <strong>cannot reliably be interpreted as causes of\na model decision<\/strong>. Tokenisation, context length, training data,\nnumerical precision and the training objective also limit what can be\ninferred. The number of model parameters alone predicts neither truth\nnor reliability.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>The formulae explain why text generation, image and text processing,\nvector search and some multimodal AI systems require large matrix\nmultiplications. They also help place concepts such as prompt context,\ncausal masking, model quantisation and the computational costs of long\ninputs in context. Implementation involves additional topics: secure\nexecution, tokeniser conventions, training, memory layout and evaluation\nmethodology.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to the next specialist lesson<\/h2>\n<p>M4-L1 describes learned statistical relationships. <strong>M4-L2\nexplicitly asks when cause and effect can actually be inferred from such\nrelationships.<\/strong> M2-L11 remains the foundation for neural\nnetworks; M4-L1 does not replace it.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately for this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_mathematics_en:M4-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M4-L2 – Compulsory elective: Causal inference and probabilistic models<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Choice of study: AI, computer science and specialised fields<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> University studies (elective /\nspecialisation)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L15&amp;syslang=de&amp;doclang=de&amp;theme=light\">M2-L15 – School\nmathematics (in German)<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L7<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L4<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L5<\/a>; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L1<\/a> for the connection to\nAI<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Elective/specialist course, advanced\nBachelor’s to Master’s level.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>Statistical data show which events occur together. Anyone seeking to\nchange a system deliberately also needs to know <strong>what would\nhappen under an intervention<\/strong>. A correlation is not always\nsufficient for this purpose. Causal inference combines probability,\ngraphical models and testable assumptions to investigate the\nconsequences of possible interventions. This perspective is important in\nmedicine, economics, product development and the control of autonomous\nsystems.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and intuition<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose a robot works faster on dry days and also reports fewer\nsensor errors on those days. A mere association between faster operation\nand fewer errors does not prove that higher speed prevents errors. The\nweather may influence both quantities. A measure that changes the speed\ndoes not then automatically have the same effect as the difference\nbetween naturally fast and slow days.<\/p>\n<p>To describe such situations, we use <strong>directed acyclic\ngraphs<\/strong> (DAGs). In the causal model under consideration, an\narrow represents an assumed direction of direct influence; its presence\nmust be justified on subject-specific grounds. The graph is a modelling\nassumption, not a causal relationship that follows from the data\nalone.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols – read these first<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Read aloud<\/th>\n<th>Meaning in this lesson<\/th>\n<th>Distinction<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“zed”<\/td>\n<td>Observed background factor, such as the operating environment<\/td>\n<td>Ordinary random value<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“ex”<\/td>\n<td>Quantity that can be manipulated, for example an intervention or\ntreatment<\/td>\n<td>Input vector from M4-L1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“why”<\/td>\n<td>Outcome variable, such as performance<\/td>\n<td>Not automatically a cause of X<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z \\to  X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“Z influences X”<\/td>\n<td>Directed edge in the postulated causal model<\/td>\n<td>Mere chronological order<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(X=1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“Probability that X equals one”<\/td>\n<td>Probability of an event in the observational model<\/td>\n<td>Intervention probability<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(Y\\vert X,Z)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“Y given X and Z”<\/td>\n<td>Conditional distribution from observational data<\/td>\n<td>Intervention on X<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[Y]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“Expected value of Y”<\/td>\n<td>Probability-weighted mean<\/td>\n<td>Sample mean without a model<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">do<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{do}(X=1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“do: X is set to one”<\/td>\n<td>Intervention that fixes X in the model<\/td>\n<td>Selection of cases with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">do<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[Y\\vert\\operatorname{do}(X=1)]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“Expected value of Y after an intervention setting X to one”<\/td>\n<td>Expected outcome of an intervention<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[Y\\vert X=1]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“Sum over all values of Z”<\/td>\n<td>Averaging over background groups<\/td>\n<td>Integral for continuous Z<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>⊥<\/code><\/td>\n<td>“Independent of”<\/td>\n<td>Statistical independence<\/td>\n<td>Perpendicularity in geometry<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Observation and intervention are two different\nquestions<\/h2>\n<p>The conditional expectation<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-mathematics-en/media/img/000113_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Causal graph with a common cause and a possible direct effect\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Causal graph with a common cause and a\npossible direct effect<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[Y\\mid X=1]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>is read as: “What mean value of Y do we observe among the cases in\nwhich X equals one?” This is a purely observational question.<\/p>\n<p>A different question is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">do<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[Y\\mid\\operatorname{do}(X=1)]\\text{.}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: “What mean value of Y do we expect if we actively set X\nto one?” In the causal model, the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">do<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{do}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\noperator denotes the intervention in which the original causes of X are\ndisconnected when its value is set. This distinction is the starting\npoint of <strong>intervention calculus<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>A simple causal diagram illustrates possible\n<strong>confounding<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<p>Formula, sketch or calculation scheme · original notation<\/p>\n<pre><code>Z → X → Y\n \\      ↑\n  └──────┘<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Read aloud: “Z influences X and Y; X also influences Y.” This means\nthat a comparison of X and Y may capture both the effect of X and the\ninfluence of Z. We need additional assumptions or a suitable study\ndesign to distinguish the effects.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Fully worked example: apparent effect and actual\nintervention<\/h2>\n<p>We consider an explicitly constructed model with three\nquantities:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis either 0 or 1. Both values are equally frequent:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(Z=1)=P(Z=0)=0{,}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/li>\n<li>The probability of a measure\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndepends on Z:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(X=1|Z=1)=0{,}8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(X=1|Z=0)=0{,}2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/li>\n<li>Let the outcome be deterministic:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>Z<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y = X + 2Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus, actively setting X to one increases the outcome by exactly 1 for a\nfixed Z.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>First, we consider only the data. Bayes’ theorem and the law of total\nprobability give:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nP(X=1)&amp;=0{,}8\\cdot0{,}5+0{,}2\\cdot0{,}5=0{,}5\\text{.}\\\\\nP(Z=1\\mid X=1)&amp;=\\frac{0{,}8\\cdot0{,}5}{0{,}5}=0{,}8\\text{.}\\\\\nP(Z=1\\mid X=0)&amp;=\\frac{0{,}2\\cdot0{,}5}{0{,}5}=0{,}2\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: “Among cases with X equal to one, 80 per cent are in the\ngroup Z equal to one. Among cases with X equal to zero, only 20 per cent\nare.” These groups therefore differ <strong>not only in X<\/strong>, but\nalso in their background factor Z.<\/p>\n<p>For the observed mean, we obtain:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Observed difference<\/mtext><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y\\mid X=1]&amp;=1+2\\cdot0{,}8=2{,}6\\text{.}\\\\\nE[Y\\mid X=0]&amp;=0+2\\cdot0{,}2=0{,}4\\text{.}\\\\\n\\text{Observed difference}&amp;=2{,}6-0{,}4=2{,}2\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: “Simply comparing the observed groups gives a difference\nof 2.2 units.” This value <strong>overestimates<\/strong> the causal\neffect of X in our constructed model.<\/p>\n<p>Under the intervention, however, the original probabilities of the\nbackground groups remain unchanged. Because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>Z<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=X+2Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">do<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">do<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Average causal effect<\/mtext><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y\\mid\\operatorname{do}(X=1)]&amp;=1+2\\cdot0{,}5=2\\text{.}\\\\\nE[Y\\mid\\operatorname{do}(X=0)]&amp;=0+2\\cdot0{,}5=1\\text{.}\\\\\n\\text{Average causal effect}&amp;=2-1=1\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: “Actively changing X from zero to one increases the mean\noutcome by one, not by 2.2.” The example shows how the same data\nsituation can produce a different observed difference from the causal\ndifference.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The adjustment formula<\/h2>\n<p>If the background factor Z is <strong>sufficient to control all\nrelevant back-door paths<\/strong>, is present before the measure in the\nappropriate causal model, and treatment is possible under all the\nconditions considered, the interventional distribution can be obtained\nfrom observational data:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">do<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><\/munder><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(Y=y\\mid\\operatorname{do}(X=x))=\\sum_z P(Y=y\\mid X=x,Z=z)\\cdot P(Z=z)\\text{.}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: “For each background group z, we consider the observed\nproportion with the outcome at a fixed X and average these proportions\nusing the share of that background group in the entire original\npopulation.” For continuous Z, the sum is replaced by an integral.<\/p>\n<p>In particular, this requires <strong>exchangeability without\nunobserved confounding<\/strong> conditional on Z,\n<strong>positivity<\/strong> (each treatment being compared must be\nobservable for the relevant Z groups) and a meaningful\n<strong>consistency assumption<\/strong>: the observed treatment\ncorresponds to the intervention described. The formula does <strong>not\napply merely because<\/strong> Z appears in the data. In more complex\ndiagrams, choosing suitable control variables is itself part of the\nproblem.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why not every control variable is helpful<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>collider<\/strong> is a variable into which two arrows\npoint:<\/p>\n<p>Formula, sketch or calculation scheme · original notation<\/p>\n<pre><code>A → C ← B.<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Even when A and B are initially independent, this may change after\nselection or conditioning on C. “Controlling for all variables just in\ncase” is therefore not a valid general rule. Likewise, a variable\naffected by the measure itself must not be treated as an ordinary\nbackground factor without a precise research question. Dynamic causal\nmodels are required for multiple points in time.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Countercheck: A collider can create an association\nin the first place<\/h3>\n<p>Consider two <strong>independent<\/strong> coin tosses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\neach taking the values 0 and 1 with a probability of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor each value. The selection variable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis 1 precisely when <strong>at least one<\/strong> of the two tosses\nproduces 1. This is the collider\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo>←<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A \\to  C \\leftarrow  B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nA and B influence the selection without influencing each other.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">each with <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mrow><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\"> holds for <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">each conditionally with <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n(A,B)&amp;=(0,0),(0,1),(1,0),(1,1),\\quad\\text{each with }P=\\frac14\\text{.}\\\\\nC=1&amp;\\text{ holds for }(0,1),(1,0),(1,1),\\quad\\text{each conditionally with }P=\\frac13\\text{.}\\\\\nP(B=1\\mid A=1,C=1)&amp;=\\frac{1/3}{2/3}=\\frac12\\text{.}\\\\\nP(B=1\\mid A=0,C=1)&amp;=\\frac{1/3}{1/3}=1\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: “Among the selected cases with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nB is <strong>always one when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>,\nbut only in half the cases when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.”\nBefore selection, the independence of the tosses gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(B=1|A=0)=P(B=1|A=1)=0{,}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOnly the selection\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncreates the statistical dependence. It is <strong>not evidence of a\ncausal effect<\/strong> of A on B. The example assumes that the coin\ntosses are independent and uniformly distributed and that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis determined as described.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Counterfactual questions and probabilistic models<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>counterfactual<\/strong> question might be: “What would the\noutcome for the same machine have been if, instead of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe had chosen the measure\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?”\nThis is a stronger question than asking for a mean across different\nmachines. Modelling such questions requires additional assumptions about\nshared hidden influences and structural equations, rather than merely\nthe observed joint distribution.<\/p>\n<p>A Bayesian network describes a factorised probability distribution\nusing directed edges. For the simple graph Z→X, Z→Y and X→Y, we\nhave:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(Z,X,Y)=P(Z)\\cdot P(X\\mid Z)\\cdot P(Y\\mid X,Z)\\text{.}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: “The joint distribution is decomposed into the\ndistribution of the background, the treatment conditional on that\nbackground, and the outcome conditional on both quantities.” A Bayesian\nnetwork can also be understood in a <strong>purely\nprobabilistic<\/strong> sense. Interpreting the arrows as actual causes\nrequires additional structural assumptions.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Countercheck: Identical observational data,\ndifferent intervention outcomes<\/h3>\n<p>An even more fundamental problem is that the same observed\ndistribution can arise from different causal mechanisms. We assume an\nunobserved, fair binary random variable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand consider two explicitly <strong>alternative<\/strong> structural\nmodels:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Model 1:<\/mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mi>X<\/mi><mo>:=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>:=<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Model 2:<\/mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mi>X<\/mi><mo>:=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>:=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Model 1:}\\quad&amp;X:=U,\\quad Y:=X\\text{.}\\\\\n\\text{Model 2:}\\quad&amp;X:=U,\\quad Y:=U\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In both models,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalways holds in the observational data: only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\noccur, each with probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn intervention, however, replaces the equation for X, not the equation\nfor Y:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Model 1:<\/mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">do<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Model 2:<\/mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">do<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Model 1:}\\quad E[Y\\mid\\operatorname{do}(X=1)]&amp;=1\\text{.}\\\\\n\\text{Model 2:}\\quad E[Y\\mid\\operatorname{do}(X=1)]&amp;=E[U]=\\frac12\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: “Although we see exactly the same joint observational\ndistribution, the models yield different expected outcomes under\nintervention.” Without additional causal assumptions or intervention\ndata, the observational distribution cannot resolve this difference. In\nthis constructed example, there is also no overlap of the two X values\nwithin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nnaive adjustment for U is therefore not valid.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>Causal models help to assess A/B tests, diagnostic algorithms,\nrecommendations, medical studies and technical interventions. In\nrobotics and AI, it is important to know whether a prediction remains\nvalid after a change in the environment or strategy. Observational data\nalone cannot in general prove this.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The example is artificial; its distributions and causal structure are\nfully specified. In practice, unmeasured factors, selection bias,\nmeasurement errors, unclear definitions of interventions and feedback\nover time can prevent identification. A mathematically computable number\nis not necessarily a reliable causal estimate. Interventions involving\nhumans or safety-critical equipment require separate professional and\nethical assessment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to the next specialist lesson<\/h2>\n<p><strong>M4-L3<\/strong> considers dependencies within networks and\nuses graph structures for machine learning. However, a graph in M4-L3\ndoes not automatically represent a causal graph; the meanings of the\nedges differ.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are separately listed for this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_mathematics_en:M4-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M4-L3 – Compulsory elective: Graph neural networks and spectral learning<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Choice of study: AI, computer science and specialised fields<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> University studies (elective /\nspecialisation)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a> (graph theory), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L5<\/a>/<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L6<\/a> (matrices and\neigenvalues), <a href=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L11&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L11<\/a> (neural\nnetworks), <a href=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L7<\/a> (numerical\nmethods)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Specialisation between advanced Bachelor’s\nand Master’s level.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>Many types of data consist not of a single long list of numbers, but\nof objects with relationships: social networks, roads, molecules, power\ngrids, software dependencies or sensor networks. Such structures are\ncalled <strong>graphs<\/strong>. A graph neural network (GNN) computes a\nrepresentation for each node that incorporates information from\nconnected nodes. The mathematics combines graph theory, matrix\ncalculations, weighted sums, symmetry and spectral methods.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and intuition<\/h2>\n<p>In a computer network, each server has its own properties, such as\nworkload and error rate. Its behaviour also depends on neighbouring\nservers. Rather than classifying each server in isolation, a method can\ntake the properties of its neighbours into account. It must make no\ndifference whether the servers happen to be called ‘A’, ‘B’ and ‘C’ or\n‘3’, ‘9’ and ‘12’. <strong>The relationships should matter, not the\narbitrary order of the names.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols – read these first<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Name / pronunciation<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other uses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G=(V,E)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘Graph G of vertices V and edges E’<\/td>\n<td>Network model<\/td>\n<td>G as a function in other lessons<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i,j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘Indices i, j’<\/td>\n<td>Node numbers<\/td>\n<td>Arbitrary physical coordinates<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N(i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘Neighbourhood of i’<\/td>\n<td>Nodes directly connected to node i<\/td>\n<td>Entire connected component<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{i}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘Feature vector of node i’<\/td>\n<td>Original node features<\/td>\n<td>A spatial coordinate<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_{i}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘h i’<\/td>\n<td>Learned or computed node representation<\/td>\n<td>Measurement without transformation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘Adjacency matrix’<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_{ij}=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif connected, otherwise 0<\/td>\n<td>Matrix A in control theory, M3-L12<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘Degree matrix’<\/td>\n<td>Diagonal matrix of node degrees<\/td>\n<td>Differentiation operator<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=D-A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘Laplacian matrix’<\/td>\n<td>Graph Laplacian of an undirected graph<\/td>\n<td>Continuous Laplace operator from M2-L8<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Σ<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘Sum’<\/td>\n<td>Aggregated neighbour information<\/td>\n<td>Product<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mean<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{mean}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘Mean’<\/td>\n<td>Average neighbour information<\/td>\n<td>Weighted sum without normalisation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘Sigma’<\/td>\n<td>Activation function, such as ReLU<\/td>\n<td>Standard deviation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin statistics<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>W<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘Weight matrix’<\/td>\n<td>Trained linear mapping of features<\/td>\n<td>Set of a probability distribution<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>⊤<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘Transposed’<\/td>\n<td>Interchange columns and rows of a matrix<\/td>\n<td>Inverse<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘Lambda’<\/td>\n<td>Eigenvalue of the graph Laplacian<\/td>\n<td>Regularisation parameter in M3-L11<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Graphs as matrices<\/h2>\n<p>For an undirected graph with three nodes A, B, C and edges A–B and\nB–C, the adjacency matrix is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>A<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Column order: A, B, C<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nA&amp;=\\begin{bmatrix}0&amp;1&amp;0\\\\1&amp;0&amp;1\\\\0&amp;1&amp;0\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\\text{Column order: A, B, C}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In words: ‘A is connected to B, B to A and C, and C to B.’ The number\nof neighbours of a node is called its <strong>degree<\/strong>. The\ndegree matrix has entries only on its diagonal:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">diag<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=\\operatorname{diag}(1,2,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>To describe a distribution of node properties mathematically, we use\nthe <strong>graph Laplacian<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>L<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nL&amp;=D-A\\\\\n&amp;=\\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;-1&amp;0\\\\-1&amp;2&amp;-1\\\\0&amp;-1&amp;1\\end{bmatrix}\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In words: ‘The Laplacian matrix is the degree matrix minus the\nadjacency matrix.’ For undirected graphs with non-negative edge weights,\nL is symmetric and positive semidefinite. The constant vector consisting\nentirely of ones satisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L\\cdot 1=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand has eigenvalue zero. The number of connected components equals the\ndimension of its null space. For directed graphs, these statements do\nnot automatically hold in this form.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A complete mini-example of neighbourhood aggregation<\/h2>\n<p>We assign exactly <strong>one<\/strong> feature value to each\nnode:<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-mathematics-en/media/img/000114_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Message-passing principle of a graph neural network\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Message-passing principle of a graph\nneural network<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_A=1,\\qquad x_B=2,\\qquad x_C=4\\text{.}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Our simple learning model calculates the mean of a node’s own value\nand the values of its immediate neighbours. It is a pedagogically\nsimplified message-passing model <strong>without trained\nweights<\/strong> and without non-linear activation.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>7<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.333<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nh_A&amp;=\\frac{x_A+x_B}{2}=\\frac{1+2}{2}=1.5\\text{.}\\\\\nh_B&amp;=\\frac{x_A+x_B+x_C}{3}=\\frac{1+2+4}{3}=\\frac73\\approx2.333\\text{.}\\\\\nh_C&amp;=\\frac{x_B+x_C}{2}=\\frac{2+4}{2}=3\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In words: ‘Node A combines its value with B and obtains 1.5. Node B\ncombines all three nodes and obtains seven thirds. Node C combines B and\nC and obtains three.’ After <strong>one<\/strong> step, each node knows\nonly its immediate neighbourhood. Only several successive steps allow\ninformation from more distant nodes to contribute.<\/p>\n<p>A common abstract notation is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mean<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>:<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∪<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_i(\\mathrm{new})=\\sigma\\left(W\\cdot\\operatorname{mean}\\left(\\{h_j(\\mathrm{old}):j\\in N(i)\\cup\\{i\\}\\}\\right)\\right)\\text{.}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In words: ‘Compute the mean of the old representations of all\nneighbours, including the node itself, multiply by a weight matrix and\napply an activation function.’ This is <strong>one possible<\/strong> GNN\narchitecture, not the only valid one. Depending on the method,\nneighbours are summed, weighted, distinguished by edge type or combined\nusing attention.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Graph convolution, normalisation and eigenvectors<\/h2>\n<p>A widespread form of graph convolution on an undirected graph with\nadded self-loops uses:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mover><mi>A<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mover><mi>D<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mover><mi>A<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>σ<\/mi><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mover><mi>D<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mover><mi>A<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><msup><mover><mi>D<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>W<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\tilde A&amp;=A+I,\\\\\n\\tilde D_{ii}&amp;=\\sum_j\\tilde A_{ij},\\\\\nH_{\\mathrm{new}}&amp;=\\sigma\\!\\left(\\tilde D^{-1/2}\\tilde A\\tilde D^{-1/2}H_{\\mathrm{old}}W\\right)\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In words: ‘We add a self-loop to each node, form the new degree\nmatrix, normalise the adjacency matrix on both sides using the inverse\nsquare roots of the node degrees, multiply by the feature and weight\nmatrices and apply an activation function.’ The formula assumes positive\nnode degrees after adding the self-loops. This symmetric normalisation\nis different from the simple arithmetic mean in our example.<\/p>\n<p>The eigenvectors of the Laplacian describe different patterns of\nvariation on the graph. For the feature values in our example\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x={(1,2,4)}^\\top<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>L<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nx^\\top Lx&amp;={(x_A-x_B)}^2+{(x_B-x_C)}^2\\\\\n&amp;={(1-2)}^2+{(2-4)}^2\\\\\n&amp;=1+4=5\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In words: ‘The quadratic form involving the Laplacian matrix is the\nsum of the squared differences along both edges in this case.’ Small\nvalues mean that neighbouring nodes have similar features. The identity\nholds in this form for the <strong>undirected graph with unit edge\nweights<\/strong>; for weighted edges, the edge weights are included.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Cross-check: Eigenvectors and their meaning in the\nexample graph<\/h3>\n<p>The eigenvalues of the Laplacian matrix of the path graph A–B–C given\nabove can be calculated directly. Alongside the constant vector, there\nare two distinct patterns of variation:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>L<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>L<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>L<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Eigenvalues of <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>L<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>:<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nv_0&amp;={(1,1,1)}^\\top,\\quad Lv_0=0\\cdot v_0\\text{.}\\\\\nv_1&amp;={(1,0,-1)}^\\top,\\quad Lv_1=1\\cdot v_1\\text{.}\\\\\nv_3&amp;={(1,-2,1)}^\\top,\\quad Lv_3=3\\cdot v_3\\text{.}\\\\\n\\text{Eigenvalues of }L&amp;:\\quad 0,1,3\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In words: ‘The constant vector does not change along any edge. The\nsecond vector distinguishes the endpoints; the third varies particularly\nstrongly between the middle node and the end nodes.’ All three vectors\nare mutually orthogonal and form a basis of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{R}^3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe eigenvalues are not merely assertions: each of the three\nmatrix-vector multiplications can be checked row by row. For a different\ngraph, the spectrum changes; for weighted edges, the appropriate\nweighted Laplacian matrix must be used.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Cross-check: Symmetric normalisation is not an\nordinary mean<\/h3>\n<p>For the same example graph, adding self-loops gives the degrees\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2,3,2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the graph convolution, we take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe identity activation and the features\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x={(1,2,4)}^\\top<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the first node, the symmetrically normalised variant yields:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mn>6<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.3165<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">mean<\/mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nh_A(\\mathrm{GCN})&amp;=\\frac12\\cdot1+\\frac1{\\sqrt{2\\cdot3}}\\cdot2\\\\\n&amp;=\\frac12+\\frac2{\\sqrt6}\\approx1.3165\\text{.}\\\\\nh_A(\\text{mean})&amp;=\\frac{1+2}{2}=1.5\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In words: ‘Symmetric normalisation accounts for the node degrees at\nboth ends of an edge. Therefore, even with an identical graph and the\nsame features, it is <strong>not the same operation<\/strong> as the\nsimple neighbourhood mean.’ This statement applies to the layer defined\nhere, not to all GNN architectures.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why node numbering must not determine the result<\/h2>\n<p>If a graph is merely relabelled, the outputs should undergo the same\nrelabelling. This property is called <strong>permutation\nequivariance<\/strong>. In node classification, the result for the same\nnode should not depend on its position in a database table. When\nclassifying an entire graph as a single object, <strong>permutation\ninvariance<\/strong> is often required: the overall result should not\nchange at all under relabelling.<\/p>\n<p>Mathematically, a permutation matrix P is involved. If node numbering\nchanges, both the adjacency matrix and all node features must be\nreordered accordingly:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_{\\mathrm{new}}=PAP^{\\mathrm{T}},\\qquad X_{\\mathrm{new}}=PX\\text{.}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In words: ‘We interchange rows and columns of the connectivity\ninformation consistently and reorder the feature rows in the same way.’\nA suitable GNN layer produces correspondingly renumbered node\nrepresentations. This property is an important architectural\nrequirement, not an automatic advantage of arbitrary neural\nnetworks.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Cross-check: Renumbering the three-node graph<\/h3>\n<p>We interchange only the names of the end nodes A and C. The order of\nthe values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x={(1,2,4)}^\\top<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbecomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{\\mathrm{new}}={(4,2,1)}^\\top<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the rows <strong>and<\/strong> columns of the adjacency matrix are\npermuted accordingly. The simple mean from Section 2 now yields:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mn>3<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mfrac><mn>7<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mfrac><mn>7<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mfrac><mn>3<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nh_{\\mathrm{old}}&amp;=\\left(\\frac32,\\frac73,3\\right)^\\top\\text{.}\\\\\nh_{\\mathrm{new}}&amp;=\\left(3,\\frac73,\\frac32\\right)^\\top=Ph_{\\mathrm{old}}\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In words: ‘Renumbering interchanges exactly the positions of the\nresults for the two end nodes. The computed node results remain\nassociated with their original objects.’ The demonstration assumes a\nconsistent permutation of the edges as well; merely interchanging input\nrows without adjusting A would produce a different data structure. A\nglobal, symmetric sum of the node outputs would additionally be\npermutation <strong>invariant<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Multiple layers and practical limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Many aggregation steps can make node representations increasingly\nsimilar: this is called <strong>oversmoothing<\/strong>. Another problem\nis <strong>oversquashing<\/strong>: a great deal of information from\nlarge regions of the network must be transmitted through a small\nbottleneck. Some networks are <strong>heterophilous<\/strong>, meaning\nthat neighbouring nodes have dissimilar properties; indiscriminate\nsmoothing can then be harmful.<\/p>\n<p>With real data, there are also risks from erroneous edges, outdated\nnetworks and <strong>data leakage<\/strong>: if information from the\nsubsequent test task becomes available during model training through the\ngraph structure, the measurement of model quality can be misleading. The\ngraph and its edges should therefore be examined as critically as the\ntarget values.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>Graph neural networks can model relationships between atoms in\nmolecules, software components, documents, supply chains or sensors.\nSpectral graph methods help with clustering, smoothing and the analysis\nof network structures. In robotics, for example, relationships between\nobjects or joints can be modelled. Computational scaling depends heavily\non graph size, the number of edges, batching methods and model\narchitecture.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The example graph is small, undirected and has equally weighted\nedges. In practice, a graph can be directed, dynamic, heterogeneous or\nprobabilistic. <strong>An edge initially describes an observed or\ndefined relationship, not a proven cause.<\/strong> For questions about\ncausal interventions, see M4-L2; for attention mechanisms, see M4-L1.\nMoving from a mathematically plausible GNN to a reliably tested system\nrequires separate validation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to the other compulsory elective modules<\/h2>\n<p>M4-L4 introduces formal proof techniques for programs, M4-L6\ndiscusses learning-based decisions and M4-L11 deals with probabilistic\nsensor networks. These topics can be combined with graph models, but\nhave different mathematical objectives.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately for this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_mathematics_en:M4-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M4-L4 – Compulsory elective: Formal verification, type theory and proof assistants<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Choice of study: AI, computer science and specialised fields<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> University studies (elective /\nspecialisation)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L1<\/a> (logic and induction), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L13&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L13<\/a> (formal languages and\ncomputability), <a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=de&amp;doclang=de&amp;theme=light\">M1-L3\n– School Mathematics (German-language course)<\/a> (equivalence\ntransformations)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Computer science specialisation, from\nadvanced Bachelor’s to Master’s level.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>A program can behave correctly in thousands of tests and still\ncontain an error affecting a case that has not yet been tested.\n<strong>Formal verification<\/strong> therefore attempts to prove certain\nproperties of a program mathematically. The aim is not to prove every\nconceivable property of software: first we must specify precisely what\nshould hold, under which assumptions and for which program or machine\nmodel. Type theory helps distinguish meaningful propositions and program\nexpressions. Finally, proof assistants can check whether the stated\nproof steps actually follow from the rules.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea: From a test case to a general contract<\/h2>\n<p>A test might check the input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfor example. A mathematical program contract, by contrast, asserts: ‘For\n<strong>every<\/strong> integer that is at least zero, this program\nreturns a value that is at least one.’ The contract is stronger when its\nconditions are satisfied exactly. However, it also depends on the\nprogram under investigation and its semantics being modelled\ncorrectly.<\/p>\n<p>The most important distinction is: <strong>test<\/strong> = selected\nconcrete execution; <strong>proof<\/strong> = deduction for all cases\ncovered by the assumptions; <strong>runtime monitoring<\/strong> =\nchecking during execution. These three approaches complement one\nanother.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols – before the formulas<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>How to read it<\/th>\n<th>Meaning in this chapter<\/th>\n<th>Do not confuse with<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘P’, ‘Q’<\/td>\n<td>Propositions about a program state<\/td>\n<td>Probability P from M1-L7<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{P\\} C \\{Q\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘Under precondition P, Q holds after program C’<\/td>\n<td>Hoare triple for a terminating execution<\/td>\n<td>Merely observing an example<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘Program C’<\/td>\n<td>Program block to be executed<\/td>\n<td>Set C from set theory<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>:=<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘is assigned’<\/td>\n<td>Change in the state of a variable<\/td>\n<td>Mathematical equality <code>=<\/code><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>⇒<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Rightarrow<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘implies’<\/td>\n<td>If the left-hand side holds, the right-hand side holds<\/td>\n<td>Temporal sequence of program execution<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∀<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\forall<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘for all’<\/td>\n<td>General statement about inputs<\/td>\n<td>A sample<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>∧<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\land<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘and’<\/td>\n<td>Both propositions hold<\/td>\n<td>The word ‘or’<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘invariant I’<\/td>\n<td>Proposition preserved in each loop iteration<\/td>\n<td>Imaginary unit i from M2-L5<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">wp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{wp}(C,Q)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘weakest precondition of C for Q’<\/td>\n<td>Condition sufficient before C to ensure Q<\/td>\n<td>The postcondition itself<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>←<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q[x\\leftarrow E]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘in Q, replace free x with E’<\/td>\n<td>Substitution of the assignment expression E into the\npostcondition<\/td>\n<td>Uncontrolled replacement of bound variables<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>⊢<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vdash<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘is derivable’<\/td>\n<td>Formal derivation in a system of rules<\/td>\n<td>Truth in every interpretation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>⊨<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\models<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A \\to  B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘A implies B’, for types ‘function from A to B’<\/td>\n<td>Implication or function type<\/td>\n<td>Cause-and-effect arrow from M4-L2<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Nat<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘type of natural numbers’<\/td>\n<td>Non-negative integers in Lean<\/td>\n<td>Fixed-width machine integer with overflow<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{AG}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{EF}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘always on all paths’, ‘there exists a path on which\neventually’<\/td>\n<td>CTL operators for state-space verification<\/td>\n<td>Ordinary numerical multiplication<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Preconditions and postconditions: A complete proof<\/h2>\n<p>We consider a <strong>mathematical<\/strong> program with an integer\nvariable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand exact arithmetic:<\/p>\n<p>Formula, sketch or calculation scheme · Original notation<\/p>\n<pre><code>C:  x := x + 1\nP:  x ≥ 0\nQ:  x ≥ 1\n\n{ x ≥ 0 }  x := x + 1  { x ≥ 1 }<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Read aloud: ‘If the input value x is at least zero, then x is at\nleast one after the assignment.’ Why? Let the value before execution be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{\\mathrm{alt}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen after the assignment,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{\\mathrm{new}}=x_{\\mathrm{alt}}+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nholds. From\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{\\mathrm{alt}}\\ge 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit follows that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{\\mathrm{new}}\\ge 1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is not an enumeration of test cases, but a general calculation.<\/p>\n<p>An example: for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{\\mathrm{alt}}=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe result is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{\\mathrm{new}}=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA boundary case: for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{\\mathrm{alt}}=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe result is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{\\mathrm{new}}=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth are covered by the proof, as are all other non-negative\nintegers.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Important assumption:<\/strong> With bounded machine integers,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmay overflow. For a signed 32-bit integer with a range from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2147483648<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2147483648<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2147483647<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2147483647<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe must take possible overflow semantics into account and, for example,\nadd\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>2147483646<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0 \\le  x \\le  2147483646<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas an input bound. Only then does the mathematical postcondition remain\nwithin this range. Whether overflow is undefined in the actual language,\nraises an exception or is handled according to fixed rules depends on\nits semantics. A proof about unbounded mathematical integers does not\nautomatically apply to arbitrary machine code.<\/p>\n<p>In Hoare logic, the triple conventionally denotes <strong>partial\ncorrectness<\/strong>: if execution terminates, the postcondition holds.\nFor <strong>total correctness<\/strong>, termination must also be\nguaranteed. The particular assignment terminates in our simplified\nmodel.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Weakest precondition: Reasoning backwards<\/h2>\n<p>We want to execute the instruction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>:=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x := x + 2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand guarantee the condition\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x \\ge  5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafterwards. What minimum value must hold\n<strong>beforehand<\/strong>?<\/p>\n<p>Formula, sketch or calculation scheme · Original notation<\/p>\n<pre><code>Q: x ≥ 5\nC: x := x + 2\n\nwp(C,Q): x + 2 ≥ 5\n         x ≥ 3.<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Read aloud: ‘We substitute the right-hand side of the assignment into\nthe desired postcondition; this requires x plus two to be at least five.\nSo x being at least three is sufficient.’ For a simple assignment, the\nfollowing holds in general:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">wp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>:=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>←<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{wp}(x\\mathrel{:=}E,Q)=Q[x\\leftarrow E]\\text{.}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis an expression, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a proposition about the state after execution. The notation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>←<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q[x\\leftarrow E]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non the right means <strong>substitute E for the free occurrences of x in\nQ<\/strong>; when quantifiers and bound variables are involved,\nsubstitution must be carried out correctly, without accidental variable\ncapture. ‘Weak’ means that the input requirement is no stronger than\nnecessary. With side effects, exceptions, randomness and more complex\nprograms, such simple substitution rules are not automatically\nsufficient.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Loop invariants and termination<\/h2>\n<p>A common error occurs not in a single instruction, but in a loop. We\nwant to add the numbers from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nlet\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe a fixed natural number.<\/p>\n<p>Formula, sketch or calculation scheme · Original notation<\/p>\n<pre><code>i := 0\nsum := 0\nwhile i &lt; n:\n    i := i + 1\n    sum := sum + i<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The appropriate <strong>loop invariant<\/strong> is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">and<\/mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I:\\quad0\\leq i\\leq n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\mathrm{sum}=\\frac{i(i+1)}2\\text{.}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: ‘At every loop state reached, i is between zero and\nn. The sum equals i times i plus one, divided by two.’ We check the\nthree crucial steps:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Initialisation:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{sum}=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{i(i+1)}2=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe invariant holds.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Preservation:<\/strong> Suppose the invariant is true before\nanother iteration and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i&lt;n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAfter the increment,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_{\\mathrm{new}}=i+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe new sum is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{i(i+1)}2+(i+1)=\\frac{(i+1)(i+2)}2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nexactly the required formula for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_{\\mathrm{new}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Exit:<\/strong> When the loop stops,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i\\ge n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nholds. Together with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i\\le n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis implies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i=n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{sum}=\\frac{n(n+1)}2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>To establish termination, the non-negative integer\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n-i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nserves as a <strong>loop variant<\/strong>: it decreases by one in each\niteration. An invariant shows a preserved property; a variant provides\nthe termination argument here.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Numerical example:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nproduces\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{sum}=1+2+3+4=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe formula gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{4\\cdot5}2=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe calculation merely illustrates the result that has already been\nestablished in general.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Type theory: Bringing programs and propositions\ntogether<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>type<\/strong> describes the kind of object expected: a\nnatural number, a list of natural numbers or a function that computes\none number from another. In logic, from the <strong>Curry–Howard\nperspective<\/strong>, a type often corresponds to a proposition and a\nvalue of that type to a proof of the proposition.<\/p>\n<p>An elementary example is the proposition ‘From A and the implication\nfrom A to B, B follows’:<\/p>\n<p>Formula, sketch or calculation scheme · Original notation<\/p>\n<pre><code>A    : Prop           a proposition A\nB    : Prop           a proposition B\nhA   : A              a proof of A\nhAB  : A → B          a mapping from proofs of A to proofs of B\nhAB hA : B            a proof of B<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Read aloud: ‘Apply the function that turns a proof of A into a proof\nof B to the existing proof of A.’ This perspective is useful for\ntreating proofs as checkable objects. It does not automatically make\nevery arbitrary program a proof of safety.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Dependent types<\/strong> can also incorporate values into the\ndescription of a type. A vector ‘exactly five elements long’ may have a\ndifferent type from a vector ‘exactly four elements long’. Certain\ndimensional errors can thus be ruled out during type checking. However,\nthis does not automatically protect against incorrect measurement data,\ninsecure access rights or side effects.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A tiny Lean proof<\/h2>\n<p>The following <strong>Lean 4 example<\/strong> demonstrates modus\nponens. It uses only propositional logic and the built-in proof\nfacilities. For reference: <a\nhref=\"https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/Propositions-and-Proofs/\">Theorem\nProving in Lean 4, chapter on propositions and proofs<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>.<\/p>\n<pre><code>example (A B : Prop) (hA : A) (hAB : A → B) : B := by\n  exact hAB hA<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Line by line: <code>A B : Prop<\/code> introduces two propositions.\n<code>hA : A<\/code> means that A is assumed to have been proved.\n<code>hAB : A → B<\/code> is a proof of the implication. To the right of\nthe colon after the last parenthesis is the goal <code>B<\/code>. The\ninstruction <code>exact hAB hA<\/code> presents the checker with\nprecisely the proof constructed from the two assumptions.<\/p>\n<p>A proof assistant checks such proof objects against a small logical\nkernel. A successful check makes a strong statement about the\n<strong>formalised goal in the chosen system<\/strong>, but does not\nestablish that our informal problem formulation perfectly captures the\nreal requirement. The source code here is for illustration only; the\noffline reader does not require a Lean installation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model checking: Examining finitely many states\nsystematically<\/h2>\n<p>In <strong>model checking<\/strong>, the possible states and\ntransitions of a modelled system are systematically examined. Example:\nan automaton has the states <code>Waiting<\/code>, <code>Working<\/code>\nand <code>Error<\/code>. A condition might be: ‘In no reachable state is\nmotor power active while the protective cover is open.’ Rather than\nlooking at individual test runs, the method checks the reachable\ntransitions of the model.<\/p>\n<p>In the branching-time logic <strong>CTL<\/strong>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{AG} p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans: ‘On all possible paths, the proposition p holds in every state.’\nBy contrast,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{EF} q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans: ‘There is at least one path on which q eventually holds.’ Here,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the universal path quantifier,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe existential path quantifier,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindicates that a proposition holds globally and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthat it eventually holds.<\/p>\n<p>The two statements are fundamentally different. ‘A safe state is\neventually reachable’\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{EF} \\mathrm{safe}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\ndoes not imply that <strong>all<\/strong> executions remain safe at all\ntimes\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{AG} \\mathrm{safe}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).\nWith a large or infinite state space, there is a risk of <strong>state\nexplosion<\/strong>; abstraction and symbolic methods are then\nnecessary.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Countercheck: The existence of a safe execution\ndoes not imply general safety<\/h3>\n<p>We make the CTL statements checkable using a minimal illustrative\ntransition system: there is an <strong>initial state W<\/strong>\n(Waiting, safe) and an <strong>error state F<\/strong> (unsafe). The\ntransitions are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W\\to W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W\\to F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F\\to F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe proposition <code>safe<\/code> holds only in W, and the proposition\n<code>error<\/code> only in F. Each state has at least one successor, so\ninfinite paths are also defined in the model.<\/p>\n<p>Formula, sketch or calculation scheme · Original notation<\/p>\n<pre><code>Start: W.\nPath 1: W → W → W → …        always safe.\nPath 2: W → F → F → …        unsafe from F onwards.\n\nEF safe    is true in W: W itself is already safe.\nAG safe    is false in W: Path 2 reaches F.\nEF error   is true in W: Path 2 reaches F.\nAG ¬error  is false in W: Path 2 reaches F.<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Read aloud: ‘One possible safe execution does not prove that all\nexecutions are safe.’ Here,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>¬<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans ‘not’. The trace\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W\\to F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a <strong>concrete counterexample<\/strong> to the asserted universal\npath property\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{AG} \\mathrm{safe}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n– within the model. Whether a real automaton can reach the same state\nmust be examined separately.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Countercheck: A proof is tied to its number\nmodel<\/h3>\n<p>For the contract\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>:=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{x\\ge 0\\} x:=x+1 \\{x\\ge 1\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom Section 1, the precondition is sufficient for a mathematically\nunbounded integer. For the finite signed 32-bit representation, by\ncontrast, the stronger condition\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>2147483646<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0 \\le  x \\le  2147483646<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis necessary if we want to guarantee that the addition remains within\nthe representable range <strong>without overflow<\/strong>. With the\nboundary value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2147483646<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2147483646<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2147483647<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2147483647<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nan admissible value. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2147483647<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2147483647<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfalls outside the range. This demonstrates the limitation of the\noriginal model specification without assuming any particular programming\nlanguage or runtime.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>Formal verification is particularly relevant to the safety properties\nof control systems, access logic, cryptographic protocols, database\ntransactions and critical software components. Type checkers prevent\ncertain classes of errors at an early stage. Model checking can produce\ncounterexamples in the form of sequences of states when a specified\nproperty is violated in the model. Proof assistants support detailed\nmathematical arguments, including foundations of AI and\noptimisation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>A mathematically correct model may fail to capture reality. Crucial\nfactors include <strong>the quality of the specification<\/strong>,\nactual machine arithmetic, external libraries, timing behaviour,\nhardware, concurrency and trust in the compiler and runtime environment.\nUndecidability from M2-L13 explains why there cannot be a general\nalgorithm that automatically guarantees a complete decision for every\npossible program and every non-trivial program property. Nevertheless,\nparticular program properties can be proved.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to other compulsory electives and sources<\/h2>\n<p>M4-L5 examines the security that cryptographic methods provide under\nassumptions about mathematical hardness. M4-L6 covers learning-based\ndecision methods whose performance is <strong>not<\/strong> formally\nguaranteed without additional assumptions. M4-L9 later examines the\nreliability of AI systems in more depth.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Subject orientation (public; accessed 4 November\n2026):<\/strong> Lean 4 Reference Manual, especially the sections on\npropositional logic and natural numbers: <a\nhref=\"https://lean-lang.org/doc/reference/latest/\">https://lean-lang.org/doc/reference/latest/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n. The reading version and all proofs in this lesson can be understood\noffline; the link merely offers optional further study.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>External subject sources and further links mentioned in the\nlesson:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/Propositions-and-Proofs/\">Theorem\nProving in Lean 4, chapter on propositions and proofs<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://lean-lang.org/doc/reference/latest/\">https://lean-lang.org/doc/reference/latest/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_mathematics_en:M4-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M4-L5 – Compulsory elective: Modern cryptography and post-quantum methods<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Choice of study: AI, computer science and specialised fields<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> University studies (elective /\nspecialisation)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L3<\/a> (groups, fields, modular\narithmetic), <a href=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L13&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L13<\/a>\n(algorithms and computability), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L7<\/a> (probability), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L10&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L10<\/a> (bits, information and\ncoding)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Computer science / mathematics\nspecialisation, from advanced Bachelor’s to Master’s level.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>Cryptography protects messages, keys and identities against certain\ntypes of attack. Security does not arise from keeping a calculation\nsecret, but from well-understood mathematical properties, carefully\ndesigned protocols and suitable secrets. Classical asymmetric methods\noften rely on assumptions about the difficulty of factorisation or\ndiscrete logarithms. Large fault-tolerant quantum computers could\nthreaten some of these foundations. <strong>Post-quantum cryptography\n(PQC)<\/strong> develops algorithms intended to remain secure even\nagainst known quantum attacks – without claiming that every conceivable\nfuture attack is ruled out.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea: three different security objectives<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Confidentiality:<\/strong> Unauthorised parties should not be\nable to read a message.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Integrity:<\/strong> Tampering should be detected.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Authenticity:<\/strong> The receiving party should be able to\nverify the claimed communication partner or signer.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>A <strong>digital signature<\/strong> does not encrypt a message. A\n<strong>key encapsulation mechanism (KEM)<\/strong> does not simply\ntransmit a message of arbitrary length; it enables the joint derivation\nof a secret key, which can then be used, for example, for authenticated\nsymmetric encryption. These functions must be combined correctly within\nthe overall system.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols – before using them<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Read aloud<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Other uses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“em”<\/td>\n<td>Plaintext or message represented as a number in the school-level\nexample<\/td>\n<td>Mass in physics<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“see”<\/td>\n<td>Ciphertext, encrypted numerical value<\/td>\n<td>Speed c<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“kay”<\/td>\n<td>Secret key material<\/td>\n<td>Index in sums<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>⊕<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\oplus<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“exclusive or”, “XOR”<\/td>\n<td>Bitwise addition without carry<\/td>\n<td>Logical XOR operation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bmod  n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“modulo n”<\/td>\n<td>Residue class arithmetic with modulus n<\/td>\n<td>Modulo is not ordinary division<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p,q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“pee, cue”<\/td>\n<td>Prime numbers in the RSA teaching example<\/td>\n<td>Probabilities in other lessons<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=pq<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“n equals p times q”<\/td>\n<td>RSA modulus<\/td>\n<td>Sample size n<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi (n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“phi of n”<\/td>\n<td>Euler’s totient function<\/td>\n<td>Phi as an angle<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>e<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e,d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“ee, dee”<\/td>\n<td>Public and private RSA exponents<\/td>\n<td>Euler’s number e in analysis<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">gcd<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gcd (a,b)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“greatest common divisor”<\/td>\n<td>Largest number that divides both numbers<\/td>\n<td>Not a sum<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a^{-1}\\bmod n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“multiplicative inverse of a modulo n”<\/td>\n<td>Number that gives remainder 1 when multiplied by a<\/td>\n<td>Reciprocal of real numbers<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“ess”<\/td>\n<td>Secret lattice / LWE signal in the illustrative model<\/td>\n<td>State variable in RL<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>η<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“epsilon”, “eta”<\/td>\n<td>Small error / noise term<\/td>\n<td>Convergence bound in analysis<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\cdot s+\\eta  \\bmod  q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“A times s plus noise, modulo q”<\/td>\n<td>Simplified Learning with Errors relationship<\/td>\n<td>Not a complete KEM protocol<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“hash of x”<\/td>\n<td>Cryptographic one-way processing function<\/td>\n<td>Entropy function H in M2-L10<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Symmetric encryption: a transparent bit-level example<\/h2>\n<p>In <strong>symmetric encryption<\/strong>, the sender and receiver\nshare a secret key. XOR illustrates the idea using a single bit pattern\nand a key of the same length:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Message <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>m<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1010<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Key <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>k<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0110<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Ciphertext <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>c<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1100<\/mn><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(bitwise XOR)<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Decryption: <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mn>1100<\/mn><mo>⊕<\/mo><mn>0110<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1010<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Message }m&amp;=1010\\\\\n\\text{Key }k&amp;=0110\\\\\n\\text{Ciphertext }c&amp;=1100\\quad\\text{(bitwise XOR)}\\\\\n\\text{Decryption: }\\quad 1100\\oplus0110&amp;=1010\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: “XOR gives one precisely when the two input bits differ.\nApplying the same key again recovers the original message.” A\n<strong>one-time pad<\/strong> would require a key that is as long as the\nmessage, truly random, secret and used <strong>exactly once<\/strong>.\nUnder these conditions, information-theoretic secrecy is achieved.\nReusing keys destroys this guarantee.<\/p>\n<p>Practical systems do not use hand-generated XOR patterns, but\nwell-tested methods for <strong>authenticated encryption<\/strong>, such\nas AES-GCM or ChaCha20-Poly1305, together with correct key and nonce\nmanagement. A nonce is a value selected in a controlled manner for each\nencryption context; improper reuse can severely undermine security. One\nshould not invent one’s own “lightweight” cryptography for production\ndata.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Asymmetric mathematics: RSA solely as an illustrative\ncalculation<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>public<\/strong> key pair consists of a part that may be\npublished and a secret part. In the classical school-level RSA example,\nwe choose <strong>tiny, deliberately insecure<\/strong> prime\nnumbers:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>p<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>n<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>55<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>e<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">since <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">gcd<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>d<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>27<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">since <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>27<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>81<\/mn><mo>≡<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>40<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\np&amp;=5,\\quad q=11\\text{.}\\\\\nn&amp;=p\\cdot q=55\\text{.}\\\\\n\\varphi(n)&amp;=(p-1)(q-1)=4\\cdot10=40\\text{.}\\\\\ne&amp;=3,\\quad\\text{since }\\gcd(3,40)=1\\text{.}\\\\\nd&amp;=27,\\quad\\text{since }3\\cdot27=81\\equiv1\\pmod{40}\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: “The product of five and eleven is fifty-five. The\ntotient function gives forty. Three and forty are coprime. The private\nexponent twenty-seven is chosen so that three times twenty-seven leaves\nremainder one when divided by forty.”<\/p>\n<p>We encode an admissible small message as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand use the bare mathematical RSA exponentiation for illustration:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Encryption: <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>c<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msup><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>7<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>55<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>343<\/mn><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>55<\/mn><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>13<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Decryption: <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>m<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msup><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>13<\/mn><mn>27<\/mn><\/msup><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>55<\/mn><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Encryption: }\\quad c&amp;=m^e\\bmod n=7^3\\bmod55\\\\\n&amp;=343\\bmod55=13\\text{.}\\\\\n\\text{Decryption: }\\quad m&amp;=c^d\\bmod n=13^{27}\\bmod55=7\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: “Residue class thirteen represents the ciphertext in the\nmodel; exponentiation with the private exponent returns seven.” Euler’s\ncongruence arithmetic explains why this works. <strong>This school-level\nRSA is essentially completely insecure in practice<\/strong>: the modulus\nis tiny, and secure padding / encapsulation concepts, randomness and\nprotection against side-channel attacks are absent. Large RSA keys are\nnot resistant to quantum attacks either. The example serves solely to\nexplain residue classes and public and private keys.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Cross-check using the Chinese remainder theorem:<\/strong>\nInstead of calculating\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>13<\/mn><mn>27<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">13^{27}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas an enormous number, we verify the decryption separately modulo 5 and\nmodulo 11:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msup><mn>13<\/mn><mn>27<\/mn><\/msup><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>≡<\/mo><msup><mn>3<\/mn><mn>27<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≡<\/mo><msup><mn>3<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>27<\/mn><mo>≡<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msup><mn>13<\/mn><mn>27<\/mn><\/msup><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>≡<\/mo><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>27<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≡<\/mo><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>7<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>128<\/mn><mo>≡<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>11<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">The number <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mn>7<\/mn><mrow><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\"> satisfies both conditions:<\/mtext><\/mrow><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mn>7<\/mn><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">and<\/mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mn>7<\/mn><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>11<\/mn><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Since <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mrow><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\"> and <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mn>11<\/mn><mrow><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\"> are coprime,<\/mtext><\/mrow><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">the solution modulo <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mn>55<\/mn><mrow><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\"> is unique: <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n13^{27}&amp;\\equiv3^{27}\\equiv3^3=27\\equiv2\\pmod{5},\\\\\n13^{27}&amp;\\equiv2^{27}\\equiv2^7=128\\equiv7\\pmod{11}\\text{.}\\\\\n&amp;\\text{The number }7\\text{ satisfies both conditions:}\\\\\n7\\bmod 5&amp;=2\\quad\\text{and}\\quad 7\\bmod 11=7\\text{.}\\\\\n&amp;\\text{Since }5\\text{ and }11\\text{ are coprime,}\\\\\n&amp;\\text{the solution modulo }55\\text{ is unique: }m=7\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here we used\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>3<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≡<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3^4\\equiv1\\pmod5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>10<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≡<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>11<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^{10}\\equiv1\\pmod{11}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe check explains the calculation, <strong>not<\/strong> the security of\nthe school-level method.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why quantum computers matter<\/h2>\n<p>A powerful error-corrected quantum computer could use <strong>Shor’s\nalgorithm<\/strong> to solve factorisation and discrete logarithms more\nefficiently than known classical methods. This threatens, among other\nthings, typical RSA and elliptic-curve protocols. The existence of a\nsufficiently powerful quantum computer of this kind is not a\nprerequisite for starting a long-term migration today: intercepted\nconfidential data could potentially be stored and decrypted later\n(“harvest now, decrypt later”).<\/p>\n<p>For many symmetric primitives, the situation is different:\n<strong>Grover’s algorithm<\/strong> changes the theoretical search for\nkeys, but does not simply break all symmetric cryptography. Parameter\nselection, the security model and the protocol remain important.\n<strong>Post-quantum<\/strong> here means algorithms running on ordinary\ncomputers that are designed to resist currently known quantum attacks –\nnot necessarily “quantum communication”.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Lattices and Learning with Errors – what the formula\nmeans<\/h2>\n<p>A mathematical <strong>lattice<\/strong> consists of integer\ncombinations of given basis vectors. Many lattice problems ask for very\nshort vectors or good approximations. In <strong>Learning with Errors\n(LWE)<\/strong>, linear relationships are deliberately perturbed by small\nerrors.<\/p>\n<p>In the simplest one-dimensional <em>teaching model<\/em>, we choose\nthe secret element\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe modulus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>17<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=17<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand two known coefficients\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_{1}=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_{2}=7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>17<\/mn><\/mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">First sample: <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>17<\/mn><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Second sample: <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>17<\/mn><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>34<\/mn><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>17<\/mn><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nb_i&amp;=(a_i\\cdot s+\\eta_i)\\bmod17\\text{.}\\\\\n\\text{First sample: }\\quad a_1&amp;=3,\\quad \\eta_1=1\\\\\nb_1&amp;=(3\\cdot5+1)\\bmod17=16\\text{.}\\\\\n\\text{Second sample: }\\quad a_2&amp;=7,\\quad \\eta_2=-1\\\\\nb_2&amp;=(7\\cdot5-1)\\bmod17=34\\bmod17=0\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: “We multiply a public coefficient by the secret value,\nadd a small error and take the remainder modulo seventeen.” Without\nerrors, these would be ordinary linear equations in a finite residue\nclass structure. With suitably distributed errors, sufficiently large\ndimension and carefully specified parameters, a different problem arises\nthat is difficult for attackers.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limitation of the example:<\/strong> The two tiny scalar\nequations can easily be solved by trial and error and do <strong>not\nconstitute a secure LWE system<\/strong>. Modern\n<strong>Module-LWE<\/strong> methods operate on much larger structured\nobjects with specific error distributions, encapsulation / decapsulation\nmechanisms and protection against tampering or side-channel attacks. No\nsecurity guarantee may be deduced from the illustrative example.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Cross-check – two samples do not determine the secret\nuniquely here:<\/strong> Let us explicitly assume that the errors may be\ndrawn from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{-1,0,1\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn addition to the original solution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta _{1}=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta _{2}=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta _{1}=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta _{2}=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalso fit <strong>exactly the same public observations<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">With <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>:<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>17<\/mn><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>17<\/mn><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">With <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>:<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>17<\/mn><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>17<\/mn><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{With }s=5:\\quad &amp;(3\\cdot5+1)\\bmod17=16;\\\\\n&amp;(7\\cdot5-1)\\bmod17=0\\text{.}\\\\\n\\text{With }s=0:\\quad &amp;(3\\cdot0-1)\\bmod17=16;\\\\\n&amp;(7\\cdot0+0)\\bmod17=0\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Under this error assumption, the two equations therefore admit at\nleast two different secrets. Conversely, the tiny search space would be\neasy to attack by exhaustive trial. <strong>Ambiguity in a miniature\ncalculation is not a security proof for LWE<\/strong>; its actual\nhardness depends on dimension, error distribution and parameters.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Current post-quantum standards: three different tasks<\/h2>\n<p>The US standards institute <strong>NIST<\/strong> published three\ncentral final PQC standards in August 2024. The decisive technical\ndifference between them is their <strong>purpose<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Standard<\/th>\n<th>Algorithm<\/th>\n<th>Purpose<\/th>\n<th>Underlying mathematical idea<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>FIPS 203<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>ML-KEM<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Establish a shared secret by key encapsulation<\/td>\n<td>Module-LWE / lattice assumptions<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>FIPS 204<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>ML-DSA<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Create and verify digital signatures<\/td>\n<td>Module-lattice methods<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>FIPS 205<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>SLH-DSA<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Create and verify digital signatures<\/td>\n<td>Stateless hash-based construction<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Read aloud: “ML-KEM is intended for establishing a secret key; ML-DSA\nand SLH-DSA are intended for signatures, that is, for verifying origin\nand integrity.” A KEM does not automatically protect against an active\nattacker impersonating the other party. Authentication and a correctly\ndesigned protocol context are required. Likewise, a digital signature is\nnot a substitute for encrypting confidential data.<\/p>\n<p>The NIST project develops and evaluates further methods. <strong>The\ncurrent state and recommendations may change<\/strong>; the following\nofficial links are therefore documented with their access date. However,\nthe three FIPS listed above are published standards, not mere research\nproposals. <strong>Documentation status (4 November 2026):<\/strong>\nNotices concerning possible editorial or technical errata have been\npublished on the official NIST pages for FIPS 203 and FIPS 204. For\nactual implementations, the <strong>current version of the relevant\nFIPS, its errata and implementation profiles<\/strong> must therefore be\nchecked; the existence of a standard alone does not imply an error-free\nlibrary.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From mathematical hardness to genuinely secure software<\/h2>\n<p>Cryptographic security is a <strong>conditional<\/strong> mathematical\nstatement: it depends on a hardness assumption, the adversary model,\ncorrect implementations and the particular composition of the protocol.\nTypical security objectives are defined as games: an attacker attempts\nto distinguish a message, forge a signature or determine a secret key.\n“Secure” then means, for example, that the probability of success is\nsufficiently small given the specified resources, provided the\nassumptions hold.<\/p>\n<p>When migrating older systems, existing cryptographic dependencies\nmust be identified, new methods integrated into <strong>standardised and\ntested libraries<\/strong>, keys and certificates managed, and device and\nprotocol limitations taken into account. A hybrid method combines\nclassical and post-quantum-oriented components to mitigate certain\nmigration risks. Whether and how such a combination provides security\ndepends on the <strong>correct design of the combination<\/strong>;\nmerely concatenating two secrets proves nothing.<\/p>\n<p>Even large mathematical keys do not protect against compromised\nendpoints, stolen keys, misconfigurations or side channels. Software\nchecks from M4-L4 and reliable system architecture therefore remain\nimportant.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>Cryptography protects communication channels, software updates,\nfirmware signatures, digital identities, cloud storage and networks of\nautonomous devices. Post-quantum methods are particularly relevant for\nlong-lived technical systems, because today’s devices may need to\ncommunicate securely for many years. A basic mathematical understanding\nhelps us avoid treating <strong>key exchange<\/strong>,\n<strong>signatures<\/strong>, <strong>hash functions<\/strong> and\n<strong>encryption<\/strong> as interchangeable terms.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Our numerical examples are <strong>not implementation\ntemplates<\/strong>. The statements about LWE describe the principle, not\nthe full security proofs of the standardised constructions. Quantum\nresistance is a security assessment based on the current state of known\nattacks, not a proof of the impossibility of all future attacks. Even a\ncorrectly understood standard can be undermined by poor key storage,\nrandom number sources, protocol errors or implementations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connections and sources (accessed 4 November 2026)<\/h2>\n<p>M4-L4 provides the language for verifiable software properties; M4-L7\nwill deal with quantified privacy, and M4-L10 with distributed\nauthenticity and consensus. Relevant <strong>official NIST primary\nsources<\/strong>, for optional external verification; not required for\noffline reading:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>FIPS 203, ML-KEM: <a\nhref=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/203/final\">https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/203/final<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>FIPS 204, ML-DSA: <a\nhref=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/204/final\">https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/204/final<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>FIPS 205, SLH-DSA: <a\nhref=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/205/final\">https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/205/final<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>NIST Post-Quantum Cryptography Project: <a\nhref=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/Projects/Post-Quantum-Cryptography\">https://csrc.nist.gov/Projects/Post-Quantum-Cryptography<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The distinction between a KEM and a signature drawn in this chapter\ncorresponds to the respective descriptions of their purposes in the\nthree FIPS.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>External specialist sources and further reading links mentioned in\nthe teaching text:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/203/final\">https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/203/final<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/204/final\">https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/204/final<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/205/final\">https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/205/final<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/Projects/Post-Quantum-Cryptography\">https://csrc.nist.gov/Projects/Post-Quantum-Cryptography<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_mathematics_en:M4-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M4-L6 – Compulsory elective: Reinforcement learning and decision processes<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Choice of study: AI, computer science and specialised fields<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> University studies (elective /\nspecialisation)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L7<\/a> (probability), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L4<\/a> (Markov chains), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L6<\/a> (optimisation), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L11&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L11<\/a> (neural networks), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L12&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L12<\/a> (control)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Computer science / AI specialisation, from\nadvanced Bachelor’s to Master’s level.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>In ordinary supervised learning, a model receives inputs and\ncorresponding target outputs. In <strong>reinforcement learning<\/strong>\n(RL), by contrast, a learning system makes <strong>decisions about\nactions<\/strong>. Its actions change the environment and influence which\nrewards and states can occur later. A locally good decision can be poor\nin the long term. Reinforcement learning therefore describes\nmathematically the problem of finding a strategy for many successive\ndecisions through trial, knowledge of the model and evaluation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea: decisions have consequences<\/h2>\n<p>A warehouse robot chooses between travelling quickly but riskily\nthrough a narrow passage and taking a slower, safer detour. A single\nimmediate score is insufficient for evaluation: an accident may prevent\nfuture tasks, while a detour may consume battery power. RL considers\n<strong>states<\/strong>, <strong>actions<\/strong>, <strong>transition\nprobabilities<\/strong> and <strong>rewards<\/strong> together. The\ncentral concept is the <strong>policy<\/strong> – a rule specifying which\naction is chosen in a situation.<\/p>\n<p>A sequence of decisions in RL is not the same as a classical control\nloop from M3-L12: in control, a physical model may be given; RL can\nlearn strategies from data. Hybrid methods combine both. For real\nrobots, safety constraints must additionally be monitored.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols – before the first formula<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Read aloud<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Do not confuse with<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“state s”<\/td>\n<td>relevant description of the environment<\/td>\n<td>distance s in physics<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“action a”<\/td>\n<td>possible decision<\/td>\n<td>constant a in algebra<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“reward r”<\/td>\n<td>numerical feedback<\/td>\n<td>distance or radius r<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“time index t”<\/td>\n<td>number of the decision step<\/td>\n<td>continuous time in differential equations<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_{t}, a_{t}, r_{t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“state/action/reward at time t”<\/td>\n<td>quantities ordered in time<\/td>\n<td>independent samples<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(s&#39;\\mid s,a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“probability of s prime given s and a”<\/td>\n<td>transition to the next state s' given the current state and\naction<\/td>\n<td>causal intervention from M4-L2<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(s,a,s&#39;)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“reward function R”<\/td>\n<td>assumed mean immediate reward of a transition<\/td>\n<td>actual physical safety<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi (a\\mid s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“pi of a given s”<\/td>\n<td>probability of choosing action a under policy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>the mathematical constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“gamma”<\/td>\n<td>discount factor, between zero and one here<\/td>\n<td>an angle symbol in geometry<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Σ<\/code><\/td>\n<td>“sum”<\/td>\n<td>aggregated rewards or probabilities<\/td>\n<td>a continuous integral<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>⋅<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[\\cdot ]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“expected value”<\/td>\n<td>average over possible random trajectories<\/td>\n<td>directly observed result<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_\\pi(s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“state value under pi”<\/td>\n<td>expected discounted total future return<\/td>\n<td>single-step reward<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_\\pi(s,a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“action value under pi”<\/td>\n<td>expected return after initial action a and subsequent policy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>query from M4-L1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V^*(s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“optimal state value”<\/td>\n<td>greatest achievable expected value in the model<\/td>\n<td>guaranteed reward of every execution<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\max<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“maximum”<\/td>\n<td>selection of the greatest action value<\/td>\n<td>random choice<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“alpha”<\/td>\n<td>step size in Q-learning<\/td>\n<td>attention\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom M4-L1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“epsilon”<\/td>\n<td>probability of deliberate exploration<\/td>\n<td>accuracy bound in analysis<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The Markov decision model (MDP)<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>Markov decision process<\/strong>, or MDP, extends the\nMarkov chain from M3-L4 to include actions that can be chosen. Its\nclassical description comprises a state set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nan action set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na transition law\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na reward\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a discount factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the Markov model, information about the current state together with\nthe action should suffice to predict the distribution of the next state.\nThere is thus no need to know all past states in addition.<\/p>\n<p>The transition law in textual notation is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">probability of the next state <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">after action <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\"> in the current state <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">For each admissible combination <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">:<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">all <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><\/munder><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nP(s&#39;\\mid s,a)&amp;=\\text{probability of the next state }s&#39;\\\\\n&amp;\\quad\\text{after action }a\\text{ in the current state }s\\text{.}\\\\\n&amp;\\text{For each admissible combination }(s,a)\\text{:}\\\\\n\\sum_{\\text{all }s&#39;}P(s&#39;\\mid s,a)&amp;=1\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: “For a given state and a given action, the probabilities\nof all possible next states add up to one.” This statement applies to a\ncorrectly defined model. A physical robot may nevertheless encounter\nunfamiliar situations that are missing from the state space.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why long-term reward differs from immediate reward<\/h2>\n<p>A decision-maker receives a reward at each step. Later rewards are\noften given less weight by a factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le \\gamma &lt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand bounded rewards, the infinite discounted expected value is\nfinite:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>γ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>γ<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>∣<\/mo><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">initial state <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\"> actions according to policy <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nG_t&amp;=r_t+\\gamma r_{t+1}+\\gamma^2r_{t+2}+\\gamma^3r_{t+3}+\\ldots\\\\\nV_\\pi(s)&amp;=E[G_0\\mid\\text{initial state }s,\\text{ actions according to policy }\\pi]\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: “The total return begins with the immediate reward,\nfollowed by gamma times the reward at the next step, gamma squared times\nthe subsequent reward, and so on.” With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma =0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nonly the first step counts. When\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis close to one, later rewards receive greater weight. In this\nmathematical model,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a valuation parameter – not automatically the actual probability that\na system continues to operate.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Counter-check at the boundary\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma =1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/strong>\nIf a system receives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npoints indefinitely by taking the action “always wait”, the undiscounted\nseries\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}5+0{,}5+0{,}5+\\ldots<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis unbounded. Equation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=0{,}5+V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas no finite solution. The general statements about finiteness and\ncontraction above rely on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma &lt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand bounded rewards. With finite episodes or other additional\nconditions,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma =1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan also be meaningfully defined, <strong>but not under the same blanket\njustification<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>With a constant reward\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=0{,}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma =0{,}8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe geometric series yields:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>G<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nG&amp;=0{,}5+0{,}8\\cdot0{,}5+{(0{,}8)}^2\\cdot0{,}5+\\ldots\\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{0{,}5}{1-0{,}8}\\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{0{,}5}{0{,}2}=2{,}5\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: “Someone who receives half a reward point per step\nindefinitely values this sequence at two point five in total when gamma\nequals zero point eight.” This is a mathematical discounted value, not a\nstatement about actual service life.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The Bellman equation: breaking a large future into many\nsmall steps<\/h2>\n<p>The <strong>Bellman equation<\/strong> uses the structure “one step\nnow, then the rest”. For a fixed policy and a finite state and action\nspace, we have:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><\/munder><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><\/munder><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nV_\\pi(s)&amp;=\\sum_a\\pi(a\\mid s)\\cdot\\sum_{s&#39;}P(s&#39;\\mid s,a)\\cdot\\\\\n&amp;\\qquad\\bigl[R(s,a,s&#39;)+\\gamma V_\\pi(s&#39;)\\bigr]\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: “For each possible first action, we weight all subsequent\nstates by their probabilities. We add the mean immediate reward and the\nfuture value discounted by gamma; we then average over the decisions of\nthe policy.” A possibly random immediate reward is captured here by the\nconditional expected value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(s,a,s&#39;)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>For the <strong>optimal<\/strong> discounted value of a finite, fully\nobservable MDP, the Bellman optimality equation is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msup><mi>V<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/munder><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><\/munder><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><msup><mi>V<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nV^*(s)&amp;=\\max_a\\sum_{s&#39;}P(s&#39;\\mid s,a)\\cdot\\\\\n&amp;\\qquad\\bigl[R(s,a,s&#39;)+\\gamma V^*(s&#39;)\\bigr]\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: “We choose the action for which the expected immediate\nreward plus the discounted optimal future value is greatest.” An optimal\npolicy therefore maximises a <strong>modelled expected value<\/strong>,\nnot automatically a guarantee of safety. For finite sets and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le \\gamma &lt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe Bellman operator is a contraction with respect to the maximum norm;\nthe equation consequently has a unique bounded solution.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Complete worked example: wait or start an attempt?<\/h2>\n<p>A simplified device is in an <strong>active state S<\/strong>. There\nare two actions:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Wait:<\/strong> immediate reward\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthen back to state S with probability one.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Attempt:<\/strong> with probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>75<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}75<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe task succeeds and immediately yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nwith probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit fails and yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn both cases, the episode then ends in the <strong>terminal state\nT<\/strong>, whose future value is zero.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>We choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma =0{,}8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the action “attempt”, the expected immediate reward is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Attempt<\/mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>75<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nE[r\\mid\\text{Attempt}]\n&amp;=0{,}75\\cdot8+0{,}25\\cdot(-4)\\\\\n&amp;=6-1=5\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: “In three out of four cases, one receives eight points;\notherwise one loses four. The average is five.” For “wait”, we obtain\nthe value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mi>V<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}5+0{,}8\\cdot V^*(S)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nfor “attempt”, the value remains five. The optimal Bellman equation is\ntherefore:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mi>V<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V^*(S)=\\max\\left(0{,}5+0{,}8\\cdot V^*(S),\\,5\\right)\\text{.}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Let us check the candidate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V^*(S)=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Wait<\/mtext><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Attempt<\/mtext><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Wait}&amp;=0{,}5+0{,}8\\cdot5=4{,}5\\text{.}\\\\\n\\text{Attempt}&amp;=5\\text{.}\\\\\n\\max(4{,}5;5)&amp;=5\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Thus, in this small model, <strong>attempt<\/strong> is optimal.\nSimply choosing “always wait” would yield only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2{,}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npoints according to the geometric series. “Wait once and then attempt”\nalso yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}5+0{,}8\\cdot 5=4{,}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npoints and is therefore worse than attempting immediately. These values\nare <strong>expected values<\/strong>: a particular attempt may still\nyield\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFurthermore, the decision assumes that the specified transition\nprobabilities and rewards are correct.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Q-values and learning without a fully known model<\/h2>\n<p>When transition probabilities are not known exactly, methods such as\n<strong>tabular Q-learning<\/strong> can update action values using\nobserved transitions. A Q-value estimates how good a first action is\nwhen followed by a favourable strategy. The update uses a learning rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nQ_{\\mathrm{new}}(s,a)&amp;=Q_{\\mathrm{old}}(s,a)+\\alpha\\cdot\\\\\n&amp;\\qquad\\bigl[r+\\gamma\\max_bQ_{\\mathrm{old}}(s&#39;,b)-Q_{\\mathrm{old}}(s,a)\\bigr]\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: “The new action value is the old one plus the learning\nrate times the correction error. The correction error is the observed\nreward plus the discounted best estimated value in the next state, minus\nthe current estimate itself.” In the terminal state, the future\ncomponent is zero.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_{\\mathrm{old}}=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha =0{,}2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma =0{,}9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\max_b Q_{\\mathrm{old}}(s&#39;,b)=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><strong>Important regarding the state boundary:<\/strong> The\nfollowing numerical value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>4<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbelongs to a <strong>non-terminal<\/strong> next state with admissible\nsubsequent actions. If the episode ends with the transition, the term\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma\\max_b Q(s&#39;,b)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndisappears entirely; a maximum must not be taken over non-existent\nterminal-state actions.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Target value<\/mtext><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Difference<\/mtext><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>52<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Target value}&amp;=2+0{,}9\\cdot4=5{,}6\\text{.}\\\\\n\\text{Difference}&amp;=5{,}6-3=2{,}6\\text{.}\\\\\nQ_{\\mathrm{new}}&amp;=3+0{,}2\\cdot2{,}6=3{,}52\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: “The current estimate of three becomes a new value of\nthree point five two through a correction of zero point two times two\npoint six.” This example is a <strong>single tabular update<\/strong> and\ndoes not yet guarantee that an optimal strategy has been learnt.\nConvergence statements for tabular Q-learning require, among other\nthings, suitable step sizes, sufficiently frequent visits to\nstate–action pairs and an appropriate stationary MDP description. These\nsimple guarantees do not automatically apply to deep Q-learning with\nneural networks.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Terminal counter-calculation:<\/strong> Suppose the same old\nQ-value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>3<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the same step size\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napply, but the observed transition leads directly to a terminal state\nafter a reward\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen the target value is simply\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nQ_{\\mathrm{new}}&amp;=3+0{,}2\\cdot[-4-3]\\\\\n&amp;=3-1{,}4\\\\\n&amp;=1{,}6\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The result differs from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>52<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3{,}52<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\neven though both calculations use the same update rule: in the terminal\nstate, there is no further future return.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exploration versus exploitation<\/h2>\n<p>An RL system must <strong>exploit<\/strong> known good decisions\n(<em>exploitation<\/em>) and <strong>explore<\/strong> potentially better,\nunfamiliar possibilities (<em>exploration<\/em>). In a simple\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-greedy\nstrategy, the system chooses a currently best action with probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand chooses randomly among all actions with probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf two actions are possible and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon =0{,}1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthen, for a uniquely best current action, we have:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">best action<\/mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>95<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">other action<\/mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>05<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nP(\\text{best action})&amp;=0{,}9+\\frac{0{,}1}{2}=0{,}95\\text{.}\\\\\nP(\\text{other action})&amp;=\\frac{0{,}1}{2}=0{,}05\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: “Ninety per cent of the time, the best known action is\nchosen deliberately. In the remaining ten per cent, the choice is\nrandom; half of these choices again select the best action.” In real\nsystems, however, exploration is <strong>not a licence for dangerous\ntests<\/strong>. Safe exploration must take constraints into account or\ntake place in a simulation beforehand.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Bandits, partial observability, offline RL and language\nmodels<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>multi-armed bandit<\/strong> deals with decisions involving\nunknown rewards, but without the full state-dependent long-term dynamics\nof an MDP. A <strong>partially observable<\/strong> Markov decision\nprocess (<em>POMDP<\/em>) distinguishes the true state from an uncertain\nobservation. The decision-maker then often needs a distribution over\npossible states, known as the <em>belief state<\/em>. This is\nparticularly important for sensors in robotics.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Offline reinforcement learning<\/strong> uses existing\ncollected data instead of freely trying new actions in the environment\nduring learning. It can be safer and more cost-effective, but suffers\nfrom <strong>distribution shift<\/strong>: actions that rarely occur in\nthe data are often difficult to evaluate. Not every offline RL strategy\nis therefore suitable for deployment.<\/p>\n<p>In language models, RL is sometimes used to adapt the model towards\npreferred responses. However, the use of preference data, reward models\nor other optimisation methods is <strong>not identical<\/strong> to the\nsmall classical MDP example. In particular, a high expected reward in a\ntraining procedure is not proof of truth and does not guarantee safe\nresponses.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>Reinforcement learning is used for planning actions in games,\nsimulated environments, technical resource management and certain\nrobotics applications. The Bellman structure helps to weigh short-term\ncosts against long-term returns. It connects <strong>Markov models from\nM3-L4<\/strong>, <strong>optimisation from M3-L6<\/strong>,\n<strong>control from M3-L12<\/strong> and <strong>neural networks from\nM2-L11<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>In the example, the mathematical model assumes fully observed states,\nknown transitions, a fixed reward model and finite state and action\nsets. In reality, sensors may be incomplete, data outdated and rewards\npoorly designed. <strong>Reward hacking<\/strong> occurs when a system\nincreases the defined score without achieving the actual intended goal.\nFurthermore, extreme random events can be unacceptable despite a good\nexpected value. Constraints, safety checks, evaluation outside the\ntraining data and, where appropriate, formal methods from M4-L4 are\ntherefore important.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to the next compulsory elective lesson<\/h2>\n<p>M4-L7 will cover mathematically quantified privacy for datasets and\nlearning methods. M4-L9 will later address calibration, robustness and\nmeasurement of uncertainty in AI models. M4-L11 is also particularly\nrelevant to robotics and observational uncertainty.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p>No individual sources are listed separately in this lesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_mathematics_en:M4-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M4-L7 – Compulsory elective: Differential privacy and federated learning<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Choice of study: AI, computer science and specialised fields<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> University studies (elective /\nspecialisation)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L7<\/a> (probability), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L5<\/a> (statistics), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L6<\/a> (optimisation), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L11&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L11<\/a> (AI mathematics), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L5<\/a> (security objectives)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Master’s-oriented specialisation in\ncomputer science and AI; also accessible to advanced Bachelor’s students\nwith a background in statistics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>Even when a dataset is published only in the form of an average, a\nfrequency or a trained AI model, information about individuals may be\nrevealed. <strong>Differential privacy (DP)<\/strong> therefore asks more\nthan whether names have been removed. Instead, it formulates a\n<strong>mathematical guarantee about the random behaviour of an output\nprocedure<\/strong>: the statistical change in an outcome should be\nlimited in a controlled way when one person’s data is absent or\nreplaced. <strong>Federated learning<\/strong> takes a different\napproach: training data remains on devices or within institutions, while\nselected model updates are combined. The two approaches can be combined,\nbut they are not the same.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea and intuition<\/h2>\n<p>A research team wants to count how many users have activated a\nfeature. Publishing an exact number could reveal one individual’s\nparticipation by comparing two datasets. The difference between the two\ndatasets here is just one vote. If the team returns a <strong>randomly\nperturbed number<\/strong>, the two possible underlying situations become\nharder to distinguish. However, the random perturbation must be\ncalibrated to the maximum sensitivity of the query, and each additional\nquery may reveal further information.<\/p>\n<p>A second team wants to improve a predictive model on many phones.\nDevices can calculate gradients locally instead of sending raw data to a\ncentral computer. However, <strong>gradients can also contain private\ninformation<\/strong>. Federated learning alone therefore provides no\ndifferential privacy guarantee. This requires a specifically justified\nand correctly implemented DP mechanism.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols – before the first formula<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Read aloud<\/th>\n<th>Meaning in this chapter<\/th>\n<th>Do not confuse with<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mi>D<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D, D&#39;<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“dataset D and D prime”<\/td>\n<td>two <strong>neighbouring<\/strong> datasets that differ in exactly\none contribution under the chosen definition<\/td>\n<td>derivative D from other chapters<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M(D)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“mechanism M applied to D”<\/td>\n<td><strong>random<\/strong> output of an evaluation method<\/td>\n<td>deterministic evaluation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“output set S”<\/td>\n<td>any set of possible outputs<\/td>\n<td>an individual measurement<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Pr<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Pr [E]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“probability of event E”<\/td>\n<td>probability of an event<\/td>\n<td>guarantee for a particular run<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“epsilon”<\/td>\n<td>non-negative privacy parameter; a smaller value means a stricter\nratio bound, all else being equal<\/td>\n<td>learning rate or epsilon in a limit<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>δ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“delta”<\/td>\n<td>non-negative additive exception or relaxation term in the DP\ninequality<\/td>\n<td>gradient direction<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mi>ε<\/mi><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“e to the power of epsilon”<\/td>\n<td>upper multiplicative factor for probabilities<\/td>\n<td>error rate of the model<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta _{1} f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“delta one of f”<\/td>\n<td>maximum change in a numerical query between neighbouring datasets,\nexpressed as an absolute difference<\/td>\n<td>gradient of a multivariate function<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Lap<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Lap}(0,b)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“Laplace distribution centred at zero with scale b”<\/td>\n<td>symmetric noise distribution<\/td>\n<td>Gaussian distribution<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“zed”<\/td>\n<td>sampled random number or added noise<\/td>\n<td>confidential raw value<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“theta”<\/td>\n<td>parameter vector of a model<\/td>\n<td>personal identifier<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_{i}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“g sub i”<\/td>\n<td>local gradient or model update from participant i<\/td>\n<td>geometric vector in space<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_{i}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“n sub i”<\/td>\n<td>number of examples used by participant i<\/td>\n<td>sample size of the entire population<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“C”<\/td>\n<td>upper bound on the norm of a bounded update (“clipping”)<\/td>\n<td>constant of integration<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Vert g\\Vert _{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“Euclidean norm of g”<\/td>\n<td>length of a vector calculated from the squares of its\ncomponents<\/td>\n<td>absolute value of a single number<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Σ<\/code><\/td>\n<td>“sum”<\/td>\n<td>addition of several contributions<\/td>\n<td>integral of a continuous rate<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Defining privacy mathematically<\/h2>\n<p>For two <strong>neighbouring<\/strong> datasets\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>D<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D&#39;<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand every set of possible outputs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na random mechanism\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis called\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\varepsilon,\\delta)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-differentially\nprivate<\/strong> if:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Pr<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∈<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mi>ε<\/mi><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Pr<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∈<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Pr[M(D)\\in S]\\leq e^{\\varepsilon}\\cdot\\Pr[M(D\\prime)\\in S]+\\delta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read in full:<\/strong> The probability that the mechanism\nproduces an output from set S when using dataset D is at most e to the\npower of epsilon times the corresponding probability for the\nneighbouring dataset D prime, plus delta. The inequality must hold for\n<strong>every<\/strong> permitted neighbouring pair and\n<strong>every<\/strong> output set. Since it also applies to the pair in\nreverse order, the comparison is controlled in both directions.<\/p>\n<p>When\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta  = 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe speak of <em>pure epsilon-DP<\/em>. A smaller\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nlimits distinguishability more strongly; however, the numerical values\ncan only be interpreted in conjunction with the adjacency model, data\nflow, mechanism, publication frequency and adversary model.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>δ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis <strong>not<\/strong> simply the probability that “all data is\ndisclosed”. That would be an incorrect simplification of the\ndefinition.<\/p>\n<p>There are two important adjacency models: <strong>adding/removing one\nperson<\/strong> (<em>add/remove<\/em>) and <strong>replacing one\ncontribution<\/strong> (<em>replace-one<\/em>). They can lead to different\nsensitivities. Before performing a calculation, we must establish which\nmodel applies.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sensitivity and the Laplace mechanism<\/h2>\n<p>A numerical function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(D)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis to be published. Its <strong>global absolute sensitivity<\/strong>\nis:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sup<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta_1f=\\sup_{D,D\\prime\\;\\mathrm{neighbouring}}\\lvert f(D)-f(D\\prime)\\rvert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This means: how much can the number change in the worst permitted\nneighbouring case?\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sup<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sup<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>\nis read as “supremum”: the least upper bound of all possible changes. In\nfinite examples, it is often actually a maximum; in more general ranges,\nthis maximum need not be attained. When counting individual\ncontributions, each equal to either zero or one, the sensitivity for the\nreplacement case used here is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta _{1} f = 1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>A classical mechanism adds Laplace noise:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>Z<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>∼<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Lap<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">with <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">for <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nM(D)&amp;=f(D)+Z\\\\\nZ&amp;\\sim\\operatorname{Lap}(0,b),\\quad\\text{with }b=\\frac{\\Delta_1 f}{\\varepsilon}\n\\quad\\text{for }\\varepsilon&gt;0\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: the output consists of the exact count plus a randomly\nsampled Laplace value. The scale b is the maximum change divided by the\nprivacy parameter epsilon. The Laplace density is symmetric about zero;\nlarge deviations become exponentially less frequent as their magnitude\nincreases. Under the stated conditions, this mechanism satisfies pure\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-DP.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Complete numerical example:<\/strong> Twelve people have\nactivated a feature. The neighbouring dataset contains eleven such\nactivations. The published count has a sensitivity of one. For\ndemonstration, choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>693<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon  = \\ln (2) \\approx  0{,}693<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>443<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=\\frac1{\\ln(2)}\\approx1{,}443<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe maximum factor in the DP inequality is now\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mi>ε<\/mi><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^\\varepsilon=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis does <strong>not<\/strong> mean that each individual published count\ncan deviate by at most two; Laplace noise is unbounded in both\ndirections. It means that there is a precise bound on\n<strong>probability ratios for output sets<\/strong>. If the sampled\nnoise value were\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z = -1{,}2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe published number would be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12 - 1{,}2 = 10{,}8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nPublishing a value rounded to a whole number would constitute\npost-processing; it does not weaken the DP guarantee for the same\npublication.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Assumption:<\/strong> The count is the only protected release;\nother releases, participant selection and internal access must be\nconsidered separately. Revealing the exact number of participants may\nalso violate the privacy assumptions if that number is itself\nprivate.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Exact ratio cross-check:<\/strong> For two neighbouring counts\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(D)=12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>D<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(D&#39;)=11<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe Laplace mechanism with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=\\frac1{\\ln(2)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas the following two densities at a possible real-valued output\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>12<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>b<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>11<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>b<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>12<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>11<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>b<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\np_{12}(x)&amp;=\\frac1{2b}\\cdot\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{|x-12|}{b}\\right)\\\\\np_{11}(x)&amp;=\\frac1{2b}\\cdot\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{|x-11|}{b}\\right)\\\\\n\\frac{p_{12}(x)}{p_{11}(x)}&amp;=\\exp\\!\\left(\\frac{|x-11|-|x-12|}{b}\\right)\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe ratio is exactly\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>b<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exp\\left(\\frac1b\\right)=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=11<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhowever, it is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>b<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exp\\left(-\\frac1b\\right)=\\frac12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor <strong>every<\/strong> real\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe triangle inequality\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">||x-11|-|x-12|| \\le  1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nshows that the ratio lies between\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIntegrating over arbitrary output sets establishes the two-sided pure\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln (2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-DP\nbound for this neighbouring pair. <strong>Important:<\/strong> A density\nvalue is not the probability of exactly one real-valued output point.\nThe privacy definition concerns <strong>sets<\/strong> of outputs, not\nisolated density values.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Means, norm bounds and post-processing<\/h2>\n<p>The sensitivity of a mean can only be bounded straightforwardly if\nthe range of values and the adjacency rule are established. Example:\nthere are <strong>exactly ten fixed<\/strong> contributions, and each\nvalue is guaranteed to lie between zero and one. When one contribution\nis <strong>replaced<\/strong>, the mean changes by at most\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>10<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac1{10}=0{,}1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the number of participants is secret or variable, however, this small\nformula is <strong>not<\/strong> sufficient without further\nspecifications.<\/p>\n<p>One fundamental principle is known as <em>post-processing<\/em>: if a\nDP output alone is processed further, without accessing the secret\noriginal data again, this subsequent processing does not weaken the\nmathematically guaranteed DP bound. A chart derived from noisy numbers\nis protected in this sense; renewed access to precise raw data is not\nautomatically protected.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Privacy budget and repeated queries<\/h2>\n<p>Multiple outputs may complement one another. Under simple\n<strong>sequential composition<\/strong> of two DP mechanisms on the same\nprotected data, we obtain the following general upper bound:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>δ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\"> combined with <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>δ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo>→<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>δ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>δ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;(\\varepsilon_1,\\delta_1)\\text{ combined with }(\\varepsilon_2,\\delta_2)\\\\\n&amp;\\qquad\\longrightarrow(\\varepsilon_1+\\varepsilon_2,\\delta_1+\\delta_2)\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: the two epsilon bounds are added, as are the two delta\nbounds. This is a conservative bound that also applies when later\nqueries are selected adaptively in an appropriate manner. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon _{1} = 0{,}4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon _{2} = 0{,}3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe composed epsilon value is at most\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nif both mechanisms satisfy pure DP, delta remains zero. More advanced\nmethods can provide better accounting, <strong>but not every additional\nquery is free<\/strong>. In operation, budget accounting, the mechanism\nversion and the specification of a protected unit are required.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Federated learning as a different building block<\/h2>\n<p>In federated learning, several devices have local datasets. Each\ntrains a model to some extent and sends <strong>model updates<\/strong>\nfor aggregation. A simple weighted average is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_{\\mathrm{new}}=\\frac{n_1\\cdot\\theta_1+n_2\\cdot\\theta_2}{n_1+n_2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: the new shared parameter vector is the sum of the two\nlocal parameter vectors, each weighted by the number of local examples,\ndivided by the total number of examples. This is a simplified\n<em>federated averaging<\/em> principle; real systems synchronise\nmultiple local training steps and repeated rounds.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:<\/strong> Device A has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_{1} = 20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nexamples and reports the vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_1=(1;2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDevice B has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_{2} = 30<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nexamples and reports\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_2=(3;0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nComponent by component, we obtain:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>50<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>90<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>50<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>110<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>50<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\theta_{\\mathrm{new}}&amp;=\\frac{20\\cdot(1;2)+30\\cdot(3;0)}{50}\\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{(20;40)+(90;0)}{50}\\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{(110;40)}{50}\\\\\n&amp;=(2{,}2;0{,}8)\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The shared parameter vector is therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2{,}2;0{,}8)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe calculation proves <strong>no privacy guarantee<\/strong>. The\nparameters could reveal information about individual training\nexamples.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Clipping, secure aggregation and DP training<\/h2>\n<p>Before applying a DP mechanism, update norms are often bounded so\nthat individual participants have a controllable influence:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mfrac><mi>C<\/mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_{\\mathrm{clipped}}=g\\cdot\\min\\left(1,\\frac C{\\lVert g\\rVert_2}\\right)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The formula means: if the vector is shorter than the bound C, it\nremains unchanged. Otherwise, it is shortened to length C. Example:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=(3;4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas a norm of five. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C = 2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mn>2<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_{\\mathrm{clipped}}=(3;4)\\cdot\\left(\\frac25\\right)=(1{,}2;1{,}6)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhose norm is exactly two. Clipping alone is <strong>not yet a DP\nmechanism<\/strong>: it lacks the correctly calibrated random\nperturbation of the published aggregation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Secure aggregation<\/strong>, in turn, aims to prevent the\nserver from seeing individual local updates in plaintext; it uses\ncryptography and addresses a different risk from differential privacy.\n<em>Client-level DP<\/em> protects the participation of an entire device\nor person; <em>example-level DP<\/em> instead protects individual\ntraining examples. This choice changes sensitivity, sampling, budget\naccounting and the guarantee that must be justified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Caution:<\/strong> A formula for Laplace noise applied to a\nsimple count cannot be transferred unchanged to a complex, multi-stage\ntraining process. In DP-SGD, per-example gradients are typically\nclipped, aggregated and perturbed with appropriately calibrated noise,\nwhile a privacy accountant tracks the multiple training steps. A fully\ncorrect guarantee and implementation require additional assumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>AI training with sensitive data:<\/strong> quantify privacy\nbounds for participation or individual training examples instead of\nmerely claiming that data has been “anonymised”.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Smartphones and distributed devices:<\/strong> train local\nmodels without storing all raw data centrally; combine with DP and\nsecure aggregation when needed.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Official statistics:<\/strong> publish noisy frequencies and\nmeans, provided that adjacency, value ranges and the overall budget are\ncorrectly specified.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Security analysis:<\/strong> examine whether private\ninformation also leaks through logs, metadata, variable participant\nnumbers or faulty implementations.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>DP is a statement about a specified random\nmechanism<\/strong>, not about the ethical quality or general legal\ncompliance of the data processing. Consent, data minimisation and secure\nstorage remain relevant even with DP.<\/li>\n<li>Epsilon values are hardly comparable without the adjacency unit,\ndelta, sampling, composition and mechanism. Small epsilon values improve\nthe defined bound but may reduce the usefulness of the data.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Federated learning is no substitute for DP.<\/strong>\nUpdates, timing, participant lists and metadata can reveal information;\nsecure aggregation covers other risks.<\/li>\n<li>Clipping may introduce bias into data and models. With unbounded\ndata, a formally claimed sensitivity may be incorrect.<\/li>\n<li>Implementation errors, unprotected intermediate results and\nsubsequent releases can render a theoretically sound calculation\nineffective in practice.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to the next lesson<\/h2>\n<p><strong>M4-L8<\/strong> explains how generative models produce\nstructured outputs from noise. Despite the similar word <em>noise<\/em>,\nthe objective is different: M4-L7 uses randomness to limit information\nabout individual records; M4-L8 uses randomness as part of a learning\nand generation process. Randomly generated images are\n<strong>not<\/strong> private merely for that reason.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source check:<\/strong> 4 November 2026<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>NIST, <em>Guidelines for Evaluating Differential Privacy\nGuarantees<\/em>, SP 800-226, March 2025: <a\nhref=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/226/final\">https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/226/final<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n(checked on 4 November 2026).<\/li>\n<li>Dwork / Roth, <em>The Algorithmic Foundations of Differential\nPrivacy<\/em>, 2014: <a\nhref=\"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/algorithmic-foundations-differential-privacy/\">https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/algorithmic-foundations-differential-privacy/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n(checked on 4 November 2026).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_mathematics_en:M4-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M4-L8 – Compulsory elective: Generative models, diffusion and optimal transport<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Choice of study: AI, computer science and specialised fields<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> University studies (elective /\nspecialisation)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L7<\/a> (random variables), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L7<\/a> (densities and\nintegration), <a href=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L9&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L9<\/a>/<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L10&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L10<\/a> (Fourier analysis and\nfunction spaces as an outlook), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L6<\/a> (optimisation), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L11&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L11<\/a>/<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L1<\/a> (neural models)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Master’s-oriented advanced study in AI and\ndata science.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>A classifier assigns an existing input to a category. A\n<strong>generative model<\/strong>, by contrast, is intended to produce\nnew examples with characteristics of a learned distribution: images,\nsounds or geometric shapes. One modern approach begins with easily\ngenerated <strong>random noise<\/strong> and works towards structured\ndata step by step. Such <strong>diffusion models<\/strong> use\nprobability distributions, stochastic processes and neural networks.\n<strong>Optimal transport<\/strong> addresses a related but different\nquestion: how can probability mass be moved from one distribution to\nanother at the lowest possible cost? Both fields connect statistics,\nanalysis and optimisation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea: noise is a starting point here, not a privacy\nguarantee<\/h2>\n<p>A photograph contains many interrelated pixels. If controlled noise\nis repeatedly added, details become less distinct and the state\neventually resembles a simple noise distribution. A model can be trained\nto learn an appropriate <strong>reverse process<\/strong> towards\nplausible data. This reversal is not simply the algebraic undoing of\nchanges to a particular image: after substantial noise has been added,\ninformation has been destroyed and several reconstructions are\npossible.<\/p>\n<p>In optimal transport, imagine two piles of probability mass, such as\nmeasurements from an old and a new type of sensor. Every transport route\nhas a cost. We seek a <strong>coupling<\/strong> that respects all the\nsource and target masses and has the lowest possible total cost. The two\napproaches are mathematically related, but must not be treated as\nidentical algorithms.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols – before the first formula<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Read aloud<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Do not confuse with<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{0}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“x zero”<\/td>\n<td>original data vector, for example pixel values<\/td>\n<td>fixed geographical coordinate system<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“x at step t”<\/td>\n<td>randomly noised state at time step t<\/td>\n<td>deterministic position of a particle<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“t”<\/td>\n<td>index of a noise or reversal step<\/td>\n<td>necessarily physical time<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta _{t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“beta at step t”<\/td>\n<td>planned additional noise variance in the discrete forward process,\nusually between zero and one<\/td>\n<td>beta distribution in statistics<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha _{t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“alpha at step t”<\/td>\n<td>remaining signal fraction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1 - \\beta _{t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>step size in optimisation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar{a}_{t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“alpha bar at step t”<\/td>\n<td>product of all alpha values up to this step<\/td>\n<td>average of many measurements<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“epsilon”<\/td>\n<td>here, <strong>standard normally distributed noise<\/strong>, not the\nprivacy epsilon from M4-L7<\/td>\n<td>privacy budget<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N(\\mu ,\\sigma ^{2})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“normal distribution with mu and variance sigma squared”<\/td>\n<td>Gaussian distribution with mean\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>the natural number N of data points<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_{t}(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“density p at time t at x”<\/td>\n<td>probability <em>per unit interval of values<\/em> at location x<\/td>\n<td>probability of an individual real value<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(x_{t} | x_{0})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“q of x t given x zero”<\/td>\n<td>conditional forward distribution of a noised point<\/td>\n<td>trained reverse direction<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon_\\theta(x_t,t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“epsilon hat with parameters theta”<\/td>\n<td>noise estimated by the neural network<\/td>\n<td>actual sampled noise ε<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>⋅<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[\\cdot ]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“expected value”<\/td>\n<td>average over random training and noise events<\/td>\n<td>individual observation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">{\\Vert v\\Vert}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“length of v squared”<\/td>\n<td>sum of the squared vector components<\/td>\n<td>matrix determinant<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_x \\log  p(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“gradient with respect to x of the logarithm of density p”<\/td>\n<td><em>score<\/em>: direction of increasing log-density<\/td>\n<td>score assigned by a classifier<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_{i}, b_{j}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“a sub i and b sub j”<\/td>\n<td>probability masses at source and target points, respectively<\/td>\n<td>free optimisation variables<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi _{ij}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“pi sub i j”<\/td>\n<td>mass transported from source i to target j<\/td>\n<td>mathematical constant pi<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_{ij}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“c sub i j”<\/td>\n<td>cost of this transport<\/td>\n<td>normalisation factor<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Σ<\/code><\/td>\n<td>“sum”<\/td>\n<td>addition over all possible transport routes<\/td>\n<td>integral of a continuous density<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The forward process: adding controlled noise to data<\/h2>\n<p>In the standard discrete model, a further amount of noise is\nintroduced at each step. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha _{t} = 1 - \\beta _{t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the product\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar a_t=\\alpha_1\\cdot\\alpha_2\\cdot\\ldots\\cdot\\alpha_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na noised state can be generated <strong>directly<\/strong> from the\noriginal vector:<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-mathematics-en/media/img/000115_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Forward and reverse processes of a diffusion model\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Forward and reverse processes of a\ndiffusion model<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><msub><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/msqrt><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>ε<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>∼<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nx_t&amp;=\\sqrt{\\bar a_t}\\cdot x_0+\\sqrt{1-\\bar a_t}\\cdot\\varepsilon\\\\\n\\varepsilon&amp;\\sim\\mathcal N(0,I)\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: The input at step t is the original data vector,\nattenuated by the square root of the remaining overall signal fraction,\nplus an independent standard normal noise vector weighted by the square\nroot of the remaining variance fraction. The symbol\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the identity matrix; in the simple model, each component of ε\nhas variance one. The formula describes the <strong>conditional\ndistribution given\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>,\nnot automatically the distribution of all data points.<\/p>\n<p>The corresponding statement is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∣<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msqrt><msub><mover><mi>α<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/msqrt><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>α<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(x_t\\mid x_0)=\\mathcal{N}\\left(\\sqrt{\\bar{\\alpha}_t}\\cdot x_0,\\,(1-\\bar{\\alpha}_t)\\cdot I\\right)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: Given the original, the state at step t is normally\ndistributed, with a mean equal to the square root of alpha bar times the\noriginal and a covariance matrix equal to the remaining noise weight\ntimes the identity matrix. For simplicity, we assume isotropic,\nindependent Gaussian noise.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Complete numerical example of forward\ndiffusion<\/h3>\n<p>We consider just <strong>one<\/strong> numerical component. Let the\noriginal value be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{0} = 2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLet the overall signal fraction at a particular step be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>36<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar{a}_{t} = 0{,}36<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA particular random draw gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon  = -1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msqrt><msub><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/msqrt><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>36<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msqrt><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>64<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{\\bar a_t}&amp;=\\sqrt{0{,}36}=0{,}6\\\\\n\\sqrt{1-\\bar a_t}&amp;=\\sqrt{0{,}64}=0{,}8\\\\\nx_t&amp;=0{,}6\\cdot2+0{,}8\\cdot(-1)\\\\\n&amp;=1{,}2-0{,}8\\\\\n&amp;=0{,}4\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>In words:<\/strong> The original two is retained with a weight\nof zero point six and contributes one point two. The sampled noise,\nminus one, is weighted by zero point eight and subtracts zero point\neight. Zero point four remains. A different random outcome would have\nproduced a different noised value. This sample draw is not a\ndeterministic prediction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>How the model learns to estimate noise<\/h2>\n<p>During training, a neural network is shown a noised state and its\nstep. Since the actual random value drawn during the artificially\ngenerated noise step is known, the network’s estimate can be compared\nwith it:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L(\\theta)=E\\left[\\lVert\\varepsilon-\\varepsilon_{\\theta}(x_t,t)\\rVert^2\\right]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The formula reads as follows: The training loss L for the network\nparameters theta is the expected squared length of the difference\nbetween the actual noise and the noise predicted by the network. A small\nloss improves noise estimation in the training model used. It does\n<strong>not<\/strong> guarantee that every generated object is\nsemantically correct, unaltered or free of problematic training\npatterns.<\/p>\n<p>Generation typically starts with random noise and repeatedly applies\na learned reverse sampler. The specific reverse formula depends on the\nmodel family, time discretisation, choice of variance and sampler. This\nis more than simply “subtract the estimated noise value once”.\nDeterministic or stochastic samplers can be used.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is a score?<\/h2>\n<p>For a <strong>positive, differentiable density<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe score is the gradient of its logarithm:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Score<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Score}(x)=\\nabla_x\\log p(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: Take the natural logarithm of the probability density and\nconsider the direction in which it increases most rapidly. For a\none-dimensional standard normal distribution:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">constant<\/mtext><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Score<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\np(x)&amp;=\\frac1{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}\\cdot\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{x^2}{2}\\right)\\\\\n\\log p(x)&amp;=\\text{constant}-\\frac{x^2}{2}\\\\\n\\operatorname{Score}(x)&amp;=-x\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This follows because the derivative of minus x squared divided by two\nis minus x. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x = 2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe score is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand points towards the high-density centre at zero. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x = -1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand likewise points towards zero. A direction of increasing density is\n<strong>not<\/strong> a guarantee that a unique original training image\nlies there.<\/p>\n<p>Score-based models use relationships between density scales, added\nnoise and reverse dynamics. The noise vector estimated by the training\nobjective and the score are related by scale-dependent factors; the two\nquantities therefore cannot be equated without normalisation.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Cross-check: conditional score and estimated\nnoise<\/h3>\n<p>For the <strong>conditional<\/strong> Gaussian model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(x_{t} | x_{0})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nabove, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0 &lt; \\bar{a}_{t} &lt; 1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ndifferentiating the log-density with respect to the noised state\ngives:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∣<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msqrt><msub><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/msqrt><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mi>ε<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla_{x_t}\\log q(x_t\\mid x_0)\n&amp;=-\\frac{x_t-\\sqrt{\\bar a_t}\\cdot x_0}{1-\\bar a_t}\\\\\n&amp;=-\\frac{\\varepsilon}{\\sqrt{1-\\bar a_t}}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For the sample draw\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>36<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar{a}_{t}=0{,}36<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{0}=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{t}=0{,}4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>64<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\frac{0{,}4-0{,}6\\cdot2}{0{,}64}=+1{,}25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nsimilarly,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon =-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\frac{-1}{0{,}8}=+1{,}25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe calculation refers <strong>to the known\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>.\nThe score of the <strong>marginal<\/strong> distribution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_{t}(x_{t})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nin which we average over unknown original data, is generally not simply\nthe same expression with an arbitrary\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA learned noise prediction is therefore not automatically the exact\nmarginal score.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Optimal transport: moving probability mass at the lowest\npossible cost<\/h2>\n<p>We turn to a <strong>different mathematical problem<\/strong>. There\nare discrete source points\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith masses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_{i}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand target points\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>j<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith masses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b_{j}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn admissible <strong>transport coupling<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi _{ij}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nspecifies how much mass is moved from source point i to target point j.\nAll transported parts must be non-negative; each source and target total\nmust be matched exactly.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">minimise<\/mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">subject to:<\/mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">for each source <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>;<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">for each target <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{minimise}\\quad&amp;\\sum_i\\sum_j\\pi_{ij}\\cdot c_{ij}\\\\\n\\text{subject to:}\\quad&amp;\\pi_{ij}\\ge0;\\\\\n&amp;\\sum_j\\pi_{ij}=a_i\\quad\\text{for each source }i;\\\\\n&amp;\\sum_i\\pi_{ij}=b_j\\quad\\text{for each target }j\\text{.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Read aloud: Among all non-negative allocations of mass, find the one\nthat minimises the sum of transported mass times route cost, while each\nsource and target retains the prescribed total amount. Such a coupling\ndescribes an <strong>optimisation problem<\/strong>, not yet a generative\nreverse diffusion process.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Complete example with two points<\/h3>\n<p>The source distribution has mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe target distribution has mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>3<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe choose squared distance as the cost:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Cost<\/mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Cost}\\quad c(0,1)&amp;={(0-1)}^2=1\\\\\nc(0,3)&amp;={(0-3)}^2=9\\\\\nc(2,1)&amp;={(2-1)}^2=1\\\\\nc(2,3)&amp;={(2-3)}^2=1\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Plan A:<\/strong> The first half of the mass moves from 0 to\n1, the second from 2 to 3. The average transport cost is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}5\\cdot 1 + 0{,}5\\cdot 1 = 1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Plan B:<\/strong> The first half moves from 0 to 3, the second\nfrom 2 to 1. This costs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}5\\cdot 9 + 0{,}5\\cdot 1 = 5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth plans satisfy all mass constraints, but Plan A is considerably\ncheaper. In one dimension with quadratic distance costs, the\n<strong>order-preserving matching of sorted points is optimal<\/strong>:\nthe transport cost\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the minimum here. For the so-called quadratic Wasserstein distance,\nwe therefore have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_{2}^{2} = 1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_{2} = 1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>A single cost comparison describes only these two specified\ndistributions. For continuous distributions, unequal masses and\nhigh-dimensional spaces, the optimisation is more complicated;\nexistence, uniqueness and computational cost depend on the\nassumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Entropically regularised transport and the Sinkhorn\nidea<\/h2>\n<p>To handle large discrete transport problems more efficiently, an\n<strong>entropy term<\/strong> that favours a distributed coupling is\nsometimes added to the linear transport cost. A typical regularised\nexpression is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">minimise<\/mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{minimise}\\quad&amp;\\sum_{i,j}\\pi_{ij}c_{ij}+\n \\tau\\sum_{i,j}\\pi_{ij}(\\log\\pi_{ij}-1)\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(“tau”) is a positive regularisation strength; for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi _{ij} = 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe limiting value of the term is taken to be zero. A larger tau favours\nmore distributed transport plans; a small tau approximates the\nunregularised problem under suitable conditions. <strong>Sinkhorn\niterations<\/strong> repeatedly scale rows and columns of a positive\nmatrix formed from the costs to match the desired marginal totals. This\nis an algorithmic connection to M3-L6 and M3-L7; its numerical stability\nalso depends on scaling and implementation.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Verification example: why regularisation changes\nthe coupling<\/h3>\n<p>In the two-point problem above, <strong>every<\/strong> admissible\ncoupling can be described by a single number\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>u<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0 \\le  u \\le  0{,}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Target 1<\/mtext><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Target 3<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Source 0<\/mtext><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>u<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Source 2<\/mtext><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>u<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Transport cost<\/mtext><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>u<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{array}{c|cc}\n &amp;\\text{Target 1}&amp;\\text{Target 3}\\\\\n\\hline\n\\text{Source 0}&amp;u&amp;0{,}5-u\\\\\n\\text{Source 2}&amp;0{,}5-u&amp;u\n\\end{array}\\\\\n\\text{Transport cost}&amp;=5-8u\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Without regularisation, the minimum is at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=0{,}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith transport cost\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(Plan A). The midpoint\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=0{,}25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndistributes all four masses evenly and costs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>3<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSubstituting the coupling into the entropically regularised expression\ngives the derivative in the interior\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;u&lt;0{,}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msup><mi>J<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mfrac><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msup><mi>J<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>u<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nJ&#39;(u)&amp;=-8+2\\tau\\cdot\\ln\\!\\left[\\frac{u}{0{,}5-u}\\right]\\\\\nJ&#39;&#39;(u)&amp;=2\\tau\\cdot\\left[\\frac1u+\\frac1{0{,}5-u}\\right]&gt;0\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For the deliberately chosen strength\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>4<\/mn><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=\\frac4{\\ln3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit follows from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>J<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J&#39;(u)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{u}{0{,}5-u}=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus we have <strong>uniquely<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>3<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>375<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=\\frac38=0{,}375<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>125<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}5-u=\\frac18=0{,}125<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe pure transport cost of this regularised optimum is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>375<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5-8\\cdot 0{,}375=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhich is higher than the unregularised minimum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHowever, the quantity being minimised was <strong>transport cost plus\nthe entropy term<\/strong>, not transport cost alone. This is not a\ncalculation error but the purpose of the modified objective function.\nThe derivation assumes positive masses, the specified costs and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau &gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to generative AI – and an essential\ndistinction<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Diffusion<\/strong> constructs stochastic modelling and\ngeneration dynamics from noise to a data distribution.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Optimal transport<\/strong> compares distributions in terms\nof the minimum cost of a coupling. It can serve as a training tool or as\na geometric perspective on generative models.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Flow-based methods<\/strong> learn continuous transformations\nof distributions. Some combine ideas from differential equations,\ntransport and generative models; however, not every flow model can be\nequated with unique optimal transport.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Image and audio generation<\/strong> uses data-dependent\ndistributions and neural reverse processes. <strong>Simulation and\nmaterials design<\/strong> can generate possible structures or compare\ndistributions between scenarios. <strong>Optimal transport<\/strong> is\nuseful for distribution matching, image processing, sensor adaptation\nand distance measures between data sets. In <strong>robotic motion\nmodels<\/strong>, the mathematics helps compare spatial probability\ndistributions, but it does not replace collision checking or safe\ntrajectory planning.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>The forward process uses an explicitly defined noise distribution.\nThe noise model is not automatically a realistic model of all recording\nor sensor errors.<\/li>\n<li>The trained reverse process is a statistical approximation. A\nplausible appearance does not guarantee factual, causal or legal\ncorrectness.<\/li>\n<li>Data set biases, copyright issues, reconstruction of sensitive\npatterns and misuse must be addressed independently of mathematical\nquality.<\/li>\n<li>The Wasserstein distance requires a cost or metric structure and\nappropriate finite moments; exact computation can be very expensive for\nlarger or continuous problems.<\/li>\n<li>Entropic regularisation changes the optimisation problem. The\nregularised solution generally has <strong>different<\/strong> costs from\nthe unregularised optimum.<\/li>\n<li>A short scalar calculation conveys the idea, but does not prove a\nconvergence guarantee for a trained diffusion or flow model.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to the next lesson<\/h2>\n<p><strong>M4-L9<\/strong> asks when an AI result can be trusted. For\ngenerative models in particular, it is important to distinguish between\nan attractive representation, modelled probability, actual uncertainty\nand a robustly verified property. In addition, the noise used here is\nnot a differential privacy guarantee of the kind discussed in M4-L7.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source check:<\/strong> 04.11.2026<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ho / Jain / Abbeel, <em>Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic\nModels<\/em>, NeurIPS 2020: <a\nhref=\"https://papers.neurips.cc/paper/2020/hash/4c5bcfec8584af0d967f1ab10179ca4b-Abstract.html\">https://papers.neurips.cc/paper/2020/hash/4c5bcfec8584af0d967f1ab10179ca4b-Abstract.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n(checked 04.11.2026).<\/li>\n<li>Peyré / Cuturi, <em>Computational Optimal Transport<\/em>,\nFoundations and Trends in Machine Learning, 2019: <a\nhref=\"https://research.google/pubs/computational-optimal-transport/\">https://research.google/pubs/computational-optimal-transport/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n(checked 04.11.2026).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_mathematics_en:M4-L9":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L9\">M4-L9 – Compulsory elective: Reliable AI – calibration, robustness and uncertainty<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Choice of study: AI, computer science and specialised fields<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> University studies (elective /\nspecialisation)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L7<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L5<\/a> (probability, samples and\nuncertainty of estimates), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L6<\/a> (optimisation), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L11&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L11<\/a>/<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L1<\/a> (neural networks), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L2<\/a> (causal models), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L8<\/a> (generative methods as an\napplication)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Compulsory elective module in AI, advanced\nBachelor’s to Master’s level.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>An AI model can often solve a task correctly and still be\n<strong>poor at recognising when it is wrong<\/strong>. It can give\nincorrect answers with great confidence, be fooled by small changes to\nits input or fail on unusual data. The mathematics of reliable AI\ntherefore separates several questions: How often is a model correct? Are\nthe probabilities it reports credible? Does its decision remain stable\nunder bounded perturbations? And can it quantify the uncertainty of its\npredictions in a transparent way? No single metric answers all four\nquestions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea: Accuracy is not calibration<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose that for many individual cases a classifier claims to be\ncorrect with a probability of eighty per cent. If it answers correctly\nin only sixty per cent of a sufficiently large group of such cases, it\nis <strong>overconfident<\/strong>. This does not necessarily mean that\nevery individual prediction is wrong; rather, the <strong>reported\nprobability and the observed long-term frequency<\/strong> do not agree\nwell. Another model may make fewer correct decisions but report its own\nuncertainty more realistically.<\/p>\n<p>For a robot, this distinction is particularly important: a good\naverage accuracy on familiar sensor data does not prove that the same\nalgorithm remains safe in snow, under different lighting, with faulty\nsensors or in the presence of deliberate perturbations. The boundary\nbetween statistical reliability and <strong>physical system\nsafety<\/strong> must remain clear.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols – before the first formula<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Read aloud<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Do not confuse with<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_{i}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘p sub i’<\/td>\n<td>probability of the positive class output by the model<\/td>\n<td>guaranteed true probability for an individual case<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_{i}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘y sub i’<\/td>\n<td>observed correct label, here zero or one<\/td>\n<td>model prediction<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{y}_{i}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘y hat sub i’<\/td>\n<td>predicted class or a predicted numerical value – note the\ncontext<\/td>\n<td>actual target value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘n’<\/td>\n<td>number of predictions examined<\/td>\n<td>number of neurons<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘B sub m’<\/td>\n<td>set of predictions in probability bin m<\/td>\n<td>bit value or Brier score<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">acc<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{acc}(B_{m})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘accuracy of group m’<\/td>\n<td>observed accuracy in a group<\/td>\n<td>overall accuracy<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">conf<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{conf}(B_{m})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘confidence of group m’<\/td>\n<td>average reported confidence in this group<\/td>\n<td>confidence interval for an estimate<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ECE<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘Expected Calibration Error’<\/td>\n<td>aggregate discrepancy between group accuracy and confidence<\/td>\n<td>complete proof of perfect calibration<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>BS<\/code><\/td>\n<td>‘Brier Score’<\/td>\n<td>mean squared probability error<\/td>\n<td>classification accuracy<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_{1},z_{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘logits z one and z two’<\/td>\n<td>raw, not yet normalised class scores<\/td>\n<td>directly observed frequencies<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘temperature T’<\/td>\n<td>positive scaling parameter for the logits<\/td>\n<td>physical temperature<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">softmax<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{softmax}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘softmax’<\/td>\n<td>normalisation of positive exponential values to a sum of one<\/td>\n<td>proof of reliable probabilities<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>δ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘perturbation delta’<\/td>\n<td>change to an input vector<\/td>\n<td>DP exception parameter from M4-L7<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">{\\Vert\\delta\\Vert}_\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘maximum norm of delta’<\/td>\n<td>largest absolute change to an input component<\/td>\n<td>Euclidean norm<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w\\cdot x+b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘dot product of w and x plus b’<\/td>\n<td>linear classification score<\/td>\n<td>general proof for a neural network<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Vert w\\Vert _{1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘one-norm of w’<\/td>\n<td>sum of the absolute values of all weight components<\/td>\n<td>Euclidean norm<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_{i}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘residual r sub i’<\/td>\n<td>absolute error of a calibration prediction<\/td>\n<td>reward in reinforcement learning<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘alpha’<\/td>\n<td>here, maximum desired miscoverage rate<\/td>\n<td>learning rate or alpha in diffusion<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⌈<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⌉<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lceil a\\rceil<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘ceiling of a’<\/td>\n<td>smallest integer not less than a<\/td>\n<td>ordinary rounding<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>‘q’<\/td>\n<td>chosen quantile threshold<\/td>\n<td>Q matrix from attention<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Probability calibration<\/h2>\n<p>For case i, a binary model outputs the probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_{i}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthat the positive class applies. In the idealised sense, <strong>perfect\nprobabilistic calibration<\/strong> means that among all cases for which\nthe model reports exactly probability p, the actual proportion of\npositive cases is also p. In finite observed data this statement can\nonly be tested approximately, often by grouping similar predicted\nprobabilities.<\/p>\n<p>A widely used coarse summary is the <strong>Expected Calibration\nError (ECE)<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ECE<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><\/munder><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">acc<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">conf<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{ECE}=\\sum_m\\frac{\\lvert B_m\\rvert}{n}\\cdot\\lvert\\operatorname{acc}(B_m)-\\operatorname{conf}(B_m)\\rvert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In words: divide the predictions into groups. For each group,\ncalculate the absolute difference between the observed accuracy and the\naverage reported confidence. Weight this difference by the proportion of\nall predictions belonging to that group and add the results. <strong>The\nECE depends on the choice of groups<\/strong> and is itself only an\nestimate. A small ECE therefore does not prove that all subgroups or\neach individual probability are calibrated.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Fully worked example with two groups<\/h3>\n<p>We consider <strong>ten binary predictions<\/strong>, divided into two\ngroups of five observations each. Here the reported values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0{,}2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0{,}8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrepresent the probability of the <strong>positive class<\/strong>. Group\nA: of five predictions with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0{,}2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none is actually positive, giving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac15=0{,}2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nGroup B: of five predictions with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0{,}8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthree are actually positive, giving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>3<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac35=0{,}6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Group A: proportion <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>5<\/mn><mn>10<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">; observed positive rate <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">; prediction <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Contribution<\/mtext><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>5<\/mn><mn>10<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Group B: proportion <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>5<\/mn><mn>10<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">; observed positive rate <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">; prediction <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Contribution<\/mtext><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>5<\/mn><mn>10<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Total: <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\text{Group A: proportion }\\tfrac5{10}\\text{; observed positive rate }0{,}2\\text{; prediction }0{,}2\\\\\n&amp;\\quad\\text{Contribution}=\\frac5{10}\\cdot|0{,}2-0{,}2|=0\\\\\n&amp;\\text{Group B: proportion }\\tfrac5{10}\\text{; observed positive rate }0{,}6\\text{; prediction }0{,}8\\\\\n&amp;\\quad\\text{Contribution}=\\frac5{10}\\cdot|0{,}6-0{,}8|=0{,}1\\\\\n&amp;\\text{Total: }\\mathrm{ECE}=0{,}1\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This example uses the <em>calibration error for positive-class\nprobabilities<\/em>. The usual <strong>confidence ECE<\/strong>, in\ncontrast, is based on the probability of the <strong>predicted\nclass<\/strong> and its accuracy. For group A, the predicted class would\nbe negative and the confidence would therefore be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe negative class is correct in four out of five cases. This also gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nGroup B predicts the positive class with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand is correct three times, giving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe numerical ECE value in <strong>this particular dataset<\/strong> is\nalso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTerms and denominators must be clearly defined in real evaluations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The Brier score evaluates each individual prediction<\/h2>\n<p>Another metric uses the squared difference between the predicted\npositive-class probability and the actual label:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">BS<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{BS}=\\frac1n\\sum_i{(p_i-y_i)}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In words: subtract the actual class label, zero or one, from each\npositive-class probability, square each error, add up all the errors and\ndivide by the number of cases. Smaller Brier scores are better; under\nsuitable assumptions the score rewards accurately reported probabilities\nand is a <strong>proper scoring rule<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>For our example, we calculate each group:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">A: one positive observation, four negative; <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Sum of squared errors<\/mtext><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>64<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>04<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">B: three positive, two negative; <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Sum of squared errors<\/mtext><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>04<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>64<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Total: <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>22<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\text{A: one positive observation, four negative; }p=0{,}2\\\\\n&amp;\\quad\\text{Sum of squared errors}=1\\cdot{(0{,}2-1)}^2+4\\cdot{(0{,}2-0)}^2\\\\\n&amp;\\qquad=1\\cdot0{,}64+4\\cdot0{,}04=0{,}80\\\\\n&amp;\\text{B: three positive, two negative; }p=0{,}8\\\\\n&amp;\\quad\\text{Sum of squared errors}=3\\cdot{(0{,}8-1)}^2+2\\cdot{(0{,}8-0)}^2\\\\\n&amp;\\qquad=3\\cdot0{,}04+2\\cdot0{,}64=1{,}40\\\\\n&amp;\\text{Total: }\\mathrm{BS}=\\frac{0{,}80+1{,}40}{10}=0{,}22\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This makes it clear why a model with an ECE of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncannot simply be described as ‘90 per cent reliable’. The number\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis <strong>not<\/strong> the complement of a general probability of\nreliability.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Counterexample: Zero ECE despite substantial\nsubgroup errors<\/h3>\n<p>Suppose that a model outputs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0{,}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the positive class <strong>ten times<\/strong>. In a subgroup A of\nfive cases, all target values are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand in a second subgroup B of five cases they are all\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAcross all ten cases the positive rate is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>5<\/mn><mn>10<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac5{10}=0{,}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nthe error for a single combined ECE bin is therefore\n<strong>zero<\/strong>. Within A, however, the difference is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|1-0{,}5|=0{,}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand within B it is also\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|0-0{,}5|=0{,}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe Brier score is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{10\\cdot{(0{,}5)}^2}{10}=0{,}25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>The example does not prove that every prediction with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0{,}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis incorrect. It shows that <strong>an ECE of zero with coarse binning\ncan conceal substantial subgroup-specific miscalibration.<\/strong>\nSubgroup evaluation, the number of observations and the conditions of\ndeployment must therefore be taken into account when interpreting the\nresults.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Temperature scaling – how confidence scores are\nadjusted<\/h2>\n<p>Many neural classifiers first output <strong>logits<\/strong>. For the\nclass scores\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_{1},z_{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nsoftmax calculates class probabilities by exponentiation and\nnormalisation. In <em>temperature scaling<\/em>, all logits are first\ndivided by a <strong>positive<\/strong> parameter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_1(T)=\\frac{\\exp(z_1/T)}{\\exp(z_1/T)+\\exp(z_2/T)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In words: exponentiate the first logit after dividing it by the\ntemperature, and divide by the sum of the exponentiated logits, scaled\nin the same way. A value of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngreater than one generally makes the distribution flatter, whereas a\nvalue of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nless than one makes it sharper. With a positive temperature, <strong>the\nordering of the logits does not change<\/strong>, but the confidence may\nchange.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Numerical example:<\/strong> Let us take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_{1} = \\ln  4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_{2} = 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T = 1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe probability of the first class is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>4<\/mn><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac4{4+1}=0{,}8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T = 2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msqrt><mn>4<\/mn><\/msqrt><mrow><msqrt><mn>4<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>667<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\sqrt4}{\\sqrt4+1}=\\frac2{2+1}\\approx0{,}667<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe winning class is the same, but the reported confidence decreases.\n<strong>Whether<\/strong> this improves calibration must be determined on\nseparate, representative calibration data. Changing the temperature\nalone proves nothing about data quality, subgroups or robustness.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Robustness: What can small changes to the input do?<\/h2>\n<p>An image may change only slightly while a classifier returns a\ndifferent class. Robustness analysis asks which input perturbations\n<strong>within the defined perturbation model<\/strong> can change the\ndecision. A simple example is a linear classifier with the decision\nscore:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s(x)=w\\cdot x+b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In words: the decision score consists of the dot product of the\nweight vector and the input, plus a threshold value. Values greater than\nzero are assigned to class A and smaller values to class B. For an\nadditive perturbation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>δ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">{\\Vert\\delta\\Vert}_\\infty\\le\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe maximum possible decrease in the score can be bounded:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≥<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s(x+\\delta)\\geq s(x)-\\eta\\cdot\\lVert w\\rVert_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>η<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(‘eta’) is a specified maximum change <strong>per input\ncomponent<\/strong>, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Vert w\\Vert _{1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the sum of the absolute weights. The bound follows because, in the\nworst case, each component of the perturbation vector can change the dot\nproduct by at most the magnitude of its weight multiplied by eta. A\n<strong>positive lower bound<\/strong> proves that the class is preserved\nunder this perturbation model.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Fully worked numerical example of robustness<\/h3>\n<p>We choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w=(2;-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=(1;1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe classification score is therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\cdot 1 + (-1)\\cdot 1 = 1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhich is positive. The one-norm of the weights is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|2| + |-1| = 3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta  = 0{,}2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe guaranteed lower bound is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s(x+\\delta)\\geq1-0{,}2\\cdot3=0{,}4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The decision therefore remains positive <strong>for every<\/strong>\ncomponent-wise perturbation within\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>±<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pm 0{,}2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta =0{,}4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe bound falls to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-1{,}2 = -0{,}2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nthe previous guarantee can <strong>no longer<\/strong> be given. In fact,\nthe permitted perturbation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta=(-0{,}4;+0{,}4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields the score\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s(x+\\delta ) = 2\\cdot 0{,}6 - 1\\cdot 1{,}4 = -0{,}2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nresulting in a change of class. This proof applies to the specific\n<strong>linear model and maximum norm<\/strong>, not automatically to\narbitrary neural networks or physical camera images.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Aleatoric and epistemic uncertainty<\/h2>\n<p>Two fundamental perspectives are distinguished:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aleatoric uncertainty<\/strong> arises from randomness or\nirreducible variability in the observation process. Further model\ntraining cannot always eliminate the nature of this variability.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Epistemic uncertainty<\/strong> describes a lack of knowledge\nabout the model or the data; under suitable assumptions, additional\ninformation can reduce it.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>In practice, the decomposition is not always unambiguous. A Bayesian\nmodel, an ensemble or a sample of several predictions provides\nmeaningful measures of uncertainty only under its assumptions.\n<strong>Out-of-distribution (OOD)<\/strong> means that the data\nencountered in deployment differ substantially from the training\ndistribution. Good average results on test data from the original\ndistribution do not automatically demonstrate good OOD performance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Conformal prediction: Intervals with a precise frequency\nstatement<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Conformal prediction<\/strong> can provide prediction\nintervals or sets with finite-sample, distribution-free <strong>marginal\ncoverage guarantees<\/strong> under the crucial assumption of\n<strong>exchangeability<\/strong> of the calibration cases and the future\ncase. Here, ‘distribution-free’ does not mean that no assumptions at all\nare required.<\/p>\n<p>We consider a fixed, trained regression model that produces a\nprediction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{y}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor an input. On a <strong>separate calibration dataset<\/strong>, the\nabsolute residual error\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_{i} = |y_{i} - \\hat{y}_{i}|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis calculated for each case. Sort these errors in ascending order. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncalibration cases and desired miscoverage\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na common split-conformal construction selects the rank:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>k<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">⌈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">⌉<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>q<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">-th smallest calibration error (if <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Interval for new prediction: <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>;<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nk&amp;=\\left\\lceil(n+1)\\cdot(1-\\alpha)\\right\\rceil\\\\\nq&amp;=k\\text{-th smallest calibration error (if }k\\le n\\text{)}\\\\\n\\text{Interval for new prediction: }\\quad&amp;\n[\\hat y_{\\mathrm{neu}}-q;\\hat y_{\\mathrm{neu}}+q]\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In words: multiply the number of calibration cases plus one by the\ndesired coverage proportion, round up to an integer position, and take\nthe corresponding value of the sorted errors as the interval radius. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k = n+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe threshold is formally infinite in this simple version; small\ncalibration datasets do not permit every desired finite guarantee.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Fully worked example<\/h3>\n<p>We have nine independent, suitably exchangeable calibration cases.\nLet the sorted absolute errors be:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;0{,}1;\\ 0{,}2;\\ 0{,}3;\\ 0{,}4;\\ 0{,}4;\\ 0{,}5;\\ 0{,}6;\\ 0{,}7;\\ 0{,}9\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For the target coverage of 80 per cent, we choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha  = 0{,}2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⌈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⌉<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⌈<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⌉<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=\\lceil(9+1)\\cdot(1-0{,}2)\\rceil=\\lceil8\\rceil=8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe eighth-smallest error is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=0{,}7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the new model prediction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{y}_{\\mathrm{neu}}=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe interval is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>;<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>10<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>;<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>10<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n[10-0{,}7\\,;\\,10+0{,}7]&amp;=[9{,}3\\,;\\,10{,}7]\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Why rank 8 corresponds to the 80 per cent guarantee\nhere:<\/strong> Under exchangeability and with ten\n<strong>distinct<\/strong> residuals (nine calibration errors and one new\nerror), the new error has each of the ten possible ranks with\nprobability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>10<\/mn><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac1{10}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe eight smallest ranks give\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>8<\/mn><mn>10<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac8{10}=0{,}8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhen error values are tied, the usual non-randomised rule is\nconservative; the coverage remains at least as high. This calculation\nconcerns the <strong>marginal<\/strong> probability over random\ncalibration and test data, not a conditional guarantee for a dataset\nheld fixed after the fact or for a particular subgroup.<\/p>\n<p>The statement is that under the <strong>exchangeability\nassumptions<\/strong>, the actual target value of a new, exchangeable\nobservation lies in an interval constructed in this way with at least\nthe nominal marginal coverage of 80 per cent. Another requirement here\nis that the prediction model has been <strong>fixed before evaluating\nthese calibration cases<\/strong>, so that the calibration errors and the\nnew error remain exchangeable. Without separate data, for example in the\ncase of uncontrolled overfitting to the same calibration points, the\nrank argument shown does not automatically apply. The interval does\n<strong>not<\/strong> promise to be correct for each individual object\nwith an exact conditional probability of 80 per cent. Nor does it\nautomatically remain valid if sensors, user populations or data\ncollection change.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>AI classification:<\/strong> evaluate confidence scores,\ncalibration and the costs of errors together instead of reporting\naccuracy alone.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Medical or technical predictions:<\/strong> make uncertainty\ntransparent and, where appropriate, refrain from making an automated\ndecision.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Robotics and image analysis:<\/strong> analyse bounded\nperturbations of sensor data, recognise new deployment environments and\nexamine the consequences of an incorrect signal in advance.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Language models:<\/strong> distinguish model probabilities,\nthe credibility of text, factual correctness and safety policies. A\nfluent response style is not a measure of uncertainty.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Calibration is not the same as truth.<\/strong> Even a\ncalibrated model can make rare and severe errors.<\/li>\n<li><strong>ECE depends on the bins, the amount of data and the\ndefinition of the target.<\/strong> In important applications, also\nreport the Brier score, subgroups, distribution shifts and uncertainty\nintervals for estimates.<\/li>\n<li>Temperature scaling adjusts predicted probabilities but provides no\ngeneral robustness or OOD guarantee.<\/li>\n<li>A robustness bound applies only to the specified input norm, model\ntype and analysed set of attacks. Physical attacks may differ from\nthese.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Conformal prediction requires exchangeability or explicitly\njustified variants of the theory.<\/strong> With temporal drift or\ndeliberate selection, the marginal guarantee may be lost.<\/li>\n<li>Neither good statistics nor a mathematical proof concerning an\nindividual component can replace safe system integration, control of\nfeedback loops or human responsibility.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection to the next lesson<\/h2>\n<p><strong>M4-L10<\/strong> covers distributed systems. A service can\ndocument decisions in a traceable manner only if its states, times and\nfailure scenarios are clearly defined. Further topics are M4-L11 (fusing\nseveral uncertain sensor sources) and M4-L12 (evaluating approximate\nsearch in vector space). Together, M4-L7–M4-L12 provide technical\ncounterparts to the mathematical AI methods.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source check:<\/strong> 04.11.2026<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Guo / Pleiss / Sun / Weinberger, <em>On Calibration of Modern Neural\nNetworks<\/em>, ICML 2017: <a\nhref=\"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v70/guo17a.html\">https://proceedings.mlr.press/v70/guo17a.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n(checked 04.11.2026).<\/li>\n<li>Angelopoulos / Bates, <em>Conformal Prediction: A Gentle\nIntroduction<\/em>, 2023: <a\nhref=\"https://mlanthology.org/ftml/2023/angelopoulos2023ftml-conformal/\">https://mlanthology.org/ftml/2023/angelopoulos2023ftml-conformal/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n(checked 04.11.2026).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_mathematics_en:M4-L10":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L10\">M4-L10 – Compulsory elective: Distributed systems, logical clocks and consensus<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Choice of study: AI, computer science and specialised fields<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> University studies (elective /\nspecialisation)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L13&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L13<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L7<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L4<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Elective module in systems computing,\nadvanced Bachelor’s to Master’s level. Prior knowledge: M2-L1 (graphs),\nM2-L13 (algorithms and logic), M1-L7 (probability fundamentals), M4-L4\n(specifications and invariants)<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>When a service runs on several computers, there is no single central\nclock, and messages arrive with varying delays. One computer may fail\nwhile the others continue working. Nevertheless, database entries,\nmachine states or task lists should appear understandable and as\nreliable as possible. The mathematics involved describes <strong>event\nordering, majorities, consistency rules and conflict\nresolution<\/strong>. Unlike purely parallel programming, we must\nexplicitly take into account that other computers or the network may be\nunreachable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea: a timestamp does not tell the whole story<\/h2>\n<p>Imagine two computers, A and B. A reserves a robotic arm; B reads its\navailability shortly afterwards, but the message has not yet arrived.\nFrom B’s perspective, the arm may appear to be free. The local computer\nclock is therefore insufficient for making the correct decision: we need\na rule specifying <strong>which events depend on one another<\/strong>\nand <strong>when a jointly accepted state becomes binding<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>Three different goals must remain distinct:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Safety (safety of a protocol property):<\/strong> Two\ncontradictory outcomes are never established as valid, provided that the\nmodel assumptions hold.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Liveness (progress):<\/strong> Under suitable additional\nassumptions, the system eventually reaches a decision again.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Availability:<\/strong> A valid client request receives a\nsuccessful response within the definition being used. High availability\nis not the same as a state that is linearizable at all times.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols and terms – a reading guide first<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol / term<\/th>\n<th>Read aloud<\/th>\n<th>Meaning in this lesson<\/th>\n<th>Do not confuse with<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“computer A”, “process P”<\/td>\n<td>separate processes or servers<\/td>\n<td>numerical variables from algebra<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a \\to  b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“event a causally precedes b”<\/td>\n<td><em>happened-before<\/em> relation<\/td>\n<td>ordinary comparison of physical clock times<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a \\Vert  b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“a and b are concurrent”<\/td>\n<td>no causal direction is known<\/td>\n<td>events that necessarily occur simultaneously<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“logical timestamp of event a”<\/td>\n<td>Lamport counter at the event<\/td>\n<td>time in seconds<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\max (x,y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“the greater of x and y”<\/td>\n<td>selection of the greater counter value<\/td>\n<td>sum<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“en”<\/td>\n<td>number of voting servers<\/td>\n<td>sample size in statistics<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“ef”<\/td>\n<td>assumed number of failed servers<\/td>\n<td>function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⌊<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⌋<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lfloor x\\rfloor<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“floor of x”<\/td>\n<td>greatest integer not greater than x<\/td>\n<td>rounding to the nearest number<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“cue”<\/td>\n<td>required majority of votes<\/td>\n<td>query vector in M4-L1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>term<\/code><\/td>\n<td>“term”<\/td>\n<td>Raft election period with an increasing number<\/td>\n<td>algebraic term<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>log<\/code><\/td>\n<td>“sequence of log entries”<\/td>\n<td>ordered collection of replicated commands<\/td>\n<td>natural logarithm<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=(s_{1},\\ldots ,s_{n})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“state vector s”<\/td>\n<td>components of the replicated counter state<\/td>\n<td>position in three-dimensional space<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\max (s,t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“componentwise maximum”<\/td>\n<td>merging two counter states<\/td>\n<td>adding the counter values<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Σ<\/code><\/td>\n<td>“sigma, sum”<\/td>\n<td>addition of all state components<\/td>\n<td>symbol for an arbitrary process<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“vector clock at event a”<\/td>\n<td>counter value for each observed process<\/td>\n<td>a physically synchronised clock<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The notation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a \\to  b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes <em>causal event ordering<\/em> in M4-L10, not necessarily a\nnetwork connection.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a \\Vert  b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans <strong>no known ordering<\/strong>; the two events may have\noccurred at different times in the physical world.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Causal ordering and Lamport clocks<\/h2>\n<p>The relation “a happens before b” is constructed using three\nrules:<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-mathematics-en/media/img/000116_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Three processes with messages and consistent Lamport timestamps\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three processes with messages and\nconsistent Lamport timestamps<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Two events in the same process follow that process’s own execution\norder.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>sending<\/strong> of a message causally precedes its\n<strong>receipt<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>If a precedes b and b precedes c, then a also precedes c; this is\ncalled <em>transitivity<\/em>.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>A <strong>logical Lamport clock<\/strong> is a monotonically\nincreasing integer counter. For the following example, we define this\nspecific convention: before each local event or send event, the counter\nis increased by one. On receiving a message with timestamp m, the new\nlocal counter is set to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\max(C_{\\mathrm{local}},m)+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_{\\mathrm{new}}=\\max(C_{\\mathrm{old}},m)+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> “The new logical timestamp is one\ngreater than the greater of the old local counter and the received\ncounter.” This is an ordering rule, not a measurement of physical\ntime.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Fully worked example: a message from P to Q<\/h3>\n<p>Both counters start at zero.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>A local event takes place on computer P. P sets its counter to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/li>\n<li>P sends a message. Before sending it, P increments its counter to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe message carries the value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/li>\n<li>Computer Q has meanwhile performed a local event of its own and is\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/li>\n<li>Q receives the message. It calculates\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\max (1,2)+1=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe receive event therefore has timestamp\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>3<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/li>\n<li>Q then continues processing the message internally. The next event\nreceives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>4<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>The send causally precedes the receipt, and the counters confirm\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2 &lt; 3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe converse does <strong>not<\/strong> hold:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(a) &lt; C(b)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalone does <strong>not<\/strong> imply that a caused or influenced b. Two\nindependent computers may happen to carry the values 2 and 5 and still\nbe causally unrelated. <strong>Vector clocks<\/strong>, for example, can\nbe used to identify concurrency; their multiple components additionally\nrecord the observed history.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check example: identifying concurrency with vector\nclocks<\/h3>\n<p>With exactly two processes P and Q, let each vector clock have two\ncomponents\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>P<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\mathrm{counter}_P,\\mathrm{counter}_Q)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEach process increments its own component before an event of its own. On\nreceiving a message, Q first takes the <strong>componentwise\nmaximum<\/strong> with the transmitted vector and then increments its own\ncomponent.<\/p>\n<p>Formula, sketch or calculation scheme · Original notation<\/p>\n<pre><code>P after a local event and a send event a: V(a) = (2,0)\nQ after an independent event: V(b) = (0,1)\nComparison: (2,0) ≰ (0,1) and (0,1) ≰ (2,0)\nTherefore a and b are concurrent according to the observed order.\n\nSend event a transmits the state (2,0) to Q.\nQ receives it after event b:\nmax[(0,1),(2,0)] = (2,1)\nReceive event at Q: (2,2)<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The two original vectors are <strong>not comparable\ncomponentwise<\/strong>; the receive vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nby contrast, includes both histories. Unlike a single Lamport counter,\nthe vector clock can distinguish concurrency in this model. This\nstatement assumes that each relevant process component is correctly\nmaintained and transmitted; dynamic membership requires additional\nrules.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Majorities: how many votes are needed?<\/h2>\n<p>A simple majority threshold for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvoting servers is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">⌊<\/mo><mfrac><mi>N<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">⌋<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=\\left\\lfloor\\frac N2\\right\\rfloor+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> “Divide the number of servers by two,\nround down and add one.” For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe calculation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">⌊<\/mo><mfrac><mn>5<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">⌋<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\left\\lfloor\\frac52\\right\\rfloor+1=2+1=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEvery set of three servers overlaps every other such majority of three\nin <strong>at least one<\/strong> server: two disjoint groups of three\nwould require at least six distinct servers. This overlap is an\nimportant building block of consensus protocols, but <strong>not yet a\ncomplete proof of safety<\/strong>. More generally, for two subsets\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_{1},Q_{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\neach containing at least\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmembers from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nservers, we have:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>∩<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>q<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Q_{1}\\cap Q_{2}| \\ge  2q-N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(provided that the right-hand side is positive). For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthere is at least\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nshared server; for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthere are at least\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nshared servers. Nor does this alone guarantee the Raft-specific\nelection, log and commit properties.<\/p>\n<p>With at most\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n<strong>crash failures<\/strong>, a group of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>f<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2f+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvoting servers suffices for a majority decision in the sense that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nreachable servers remain under favourable network conditions. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the majority is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>3<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis statement does <strong>not apply unchanged<\/strong> to maliciously\nmanipulating (<em>Byzantine<\/em>) participants, arbitrarily disrupted\nnetworks or arbitrary consistency requirements.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example of a network partition<\/h3>\n<p>Five Raft replicas are divided into two groups: A and B on the left,\nC, D and E on the right. A disruption prevents the groups from\ncommunicating with one another. The left side has <strong>2 out of 5\nvotes<\/strong>, the right side <strong>3 out of 5<\/strong>. A new leader\nmay emerge only through a proper Raft election with a majority; the\ngroup of three may in principle have enough votes, whereas the group of\ntwo does not. A former leader on the left may <strong>not simply commit\nnew entries<\/strong> just because it was elected previously.<\/p>\n<p>This calculation explains the majority barrier. Whether an election\nsucceeds and which log entries are retained or discarded also depends on\n<strong>election terms, how up to date the logs are, message delays and\nthe complete Raft rules<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Replicated state machines and Raft<\/h2>\n<p>A replicated state machine executes the same ordered stream of\ncommands on several servers. Assuming <strong>deterministic\ntransitions<\/strong> and identical initial states, the same committed\ninputs lead to the same state. A banking or booking system must\ntherefore not only copy data, but above all ensure <strong>the order of\nthe changes<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>Raft separates three groups of problems:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Leader election:<\/strong> A server becomes the leader for an\nelection period (<em>term<\/em>) when it receives the required votes\nunder the protocol conditions.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Log replication:<\/strong> The leader distributes entries in\nan ordered sequence. Entries become committed according to the commit\nrules, not simply because they arrive at some server.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Safety rules:<\/strong> Previously committed entries must not\nbe silently overwritten in a contradictory manner by subsequent leader\nelections. These rules include, for example, checking that logs are\nsufficiently up to date and the Raft-specific commit rule for entries\nfrom the current term.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Consensus is <strong>not a proof that every request will be\nfulfilled<\/strong>: if a majority is unavailable, the safe response may\nbe to withhold acknowledgement of a write operation for the time\nbeing.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Consistency models: what may a client observe?<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Linearizability:<\/strong> A completed operation should appear\nto have taken place at a single point in time between request and\nresponse; the temporal order of non-overlapping operations is preserved.\nThis is stronger than all replicas simply agreeing “at some later\npoint”. <strong>Important for Raft reads:<\/strong> Even a server that\nstill considers itself the leader may be out of date after a network\npartition. A linearizable read response therefore requires a suitable\ncheck of current leadership and the applied commit position, for example\nthrough a quorum-based Read Index procedure or a properly safeguarded\nlease model. Being the “leader” alone does not guarantee an up-to-date\nread.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Eventual consistency:<\/strong> If no further updates occur\nand sufficient communication becomes possible again, replicas may\neventually reach the same state. Without further conflict resolution\nrules, the resulting state may be unexpected when updates compete.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Reading the CAP statement correctly:<\/strong> When a network\npartition prevents two sides from exchanging messages reliably, a system\ncannot both handle all requests successfully <strong>and<\/strong>\nguarantee a linearizable view for every request. It does not mean that\nevery system simply “gets” exactly two out of three freely selectable\nproperties. Latency, the failure model and the consistency definition\nused are decisive.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Alternative without central consensus: monotonically\nincreasing CRDT counters<\/h2>\n<p>For some data types, separate changes can be <strong>merged without\nconflicts<\/strong>, with no need to agree on each individual change in\nadvance. A <em>Grow-only Counter (G-Counter)<\/em> consists of one\ncomponent per replica. Each replica may increase <strong>only its\nown<\/strong> component. The displayed total is the sum of the\ncomponents. When merging, we take <strong>the greater value for each\ncomponent<\/strong>, not the sum of both totals.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">merge<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">value<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{merge}(s,t)_i&amp;=\\max(s_i,t_i)\\\\\n\\operatorname{value}(s)&amp;=\\sum_i s_i\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> “The i-th component of the merged state\nis the maximum of the i-th components of both copies; the counter value\nis the sum of all the components.”<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h3>\n<p>Replicas A, B and C each have their own counter component. One\nnetwork node knows the state\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=(4,1,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nanother knows\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=(3,1,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe second copy has therefore seen A only up to three, but C up to\ntwo.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>s<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>t<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">merge<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Total value<\/mtext><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\ns&amp;=(4,1,0)\\\\\nt&amp;=(3,1,2)\\\\\n\\operatorname{merge}(s,t)&amp;=(\\max(4,3),\\max(1,1),\\max(0,2))\\\\\n&amp;=(4,1,2)\\\\\n\\text{Total value}&amp;=4+1+2=7\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Merging the same state again does not change the seven. This works\nbecause\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\max<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis <strong>commutative, associative and idempotent<\/strong> for each\ncomponent: order and duplicate transmission do not affect the merged\ncounter state. However, a G-Counter <strong>cannot directly\nsubtract<\/strong> and does not replace general booking transactions with\nglobal invariants such as “allocate a ticket only once”.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Retries, failures and limits of safety<\/h2>\n<p>A client may submit a task, the server executes it, but the response\nis lost. Sending the request again risks executing it twice. Systems\ntherefore need <em>idempotent<\/em> operations or stable request\nidentifiers with suitable duplicate detection. Network delivery with “at\nleast once” semantics is not the same as an <strong>end-to-end\nexactly-once business effect<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>With message loss and unbounded delays, a computer cannot reliably\ntell from a local stopwatch whether the other party has crashed or is\nmerely slow. Real consensus protocols therefore base their progress\nproperties on <strong>additional timing, reachability and failure\nassumptions<\/strong>. Safety and progress must be justified\nseparately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>Distributed databases and AI services distribute requests across\ncomputers; replicated configuration data requires consistent decisions.\nSensor networks and fleets of robots must know even when radio coverage\nfails whether a decision is provisionally local or has been confirmed by\nthe whole group. CRDTs, by contrast, can reconcile distributed,\nmonotonic measurement counters once connectivity returns.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The majority example considers <strong>crash failures<\/strong> and a\nfixed group, not arbitrary hostile servers or erroneous administrative\ndecisions.<\/li>\n<li>The Lamport clock rules do not permit exact measurement of physical\ntime; additional methods are required for clock synchronisation.<\/li>\n<li>The G-Counter example requires an identifiable writer for each\ncomponent and monotonic local increments. It does not guarantee\narbitrary transactional consistency.<\/li>\n<li>Raft is a <strong>specific<\/strong> consensus protocol, not a\nsynonym for every kind of distributed coordination; all implementation\nrules must be considered when evaluating safety and availability in an\nindividual case.<\/li>\n<li>A network partition may delay or prevent safe operations. A\nmathematical majority does not replace operational monitoring, safety\nlimits or data backups.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connections and further study<\/h2>\n<p><strong>M2-L1<\/strong> provides the language of graphs,\n<strong>M2-L13<\/strong> covers algorithms and computability, and\n<strong>M4-L4<\/strong> covers program proofs. <strong>M4-L7<\/strong>\ndescribes privacy in distributed learning – such privacy does not follow\nfrom Raft or replication. <strong>M4-L11<\/strong> deals with delayed\nsensor data from multiple sources; the distinction between event time\nand message order is particularly important there.\n<strong>M4-L12<\/strong> uses replicable search indexes that raise their\nown consistency and update questions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Documented source status:<\/strong> 4 November 2026<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Leslie Lamport (1978): <em>Time, Clocks, and the Ordering of Events\nin a Distributed System<\/em>. <a\nhref=\"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/time-clocks-ordering-events-distributed-system/\">https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/time-clocks-ordering-events-distributed-system/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>Diego Ongaro and John Ousterhout (2014): <em>In Search of an\nUnderstandable Consensus Algorithm<\/em> (Extended Version). <a\nhref=\"https://raft.github.io/raft.pdf\">https://raft.github.io/raft.pdf<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The numbers and states used above are <strong>independently\nconstructed learning examples<\/strong>, not results from the\npublications listed. No external sources are loaded when reading this\nlesson.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_mathematics_en:M4-L11":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L11\">M4-L11 – Compulsory elective: Multimodal sensor fusion and probabilistic robotics<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Choice of study: AI, computer science and specialised fields<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> University studies (elective /\nspecialisation)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L11&amp;syslang=de&amp;doclang=de&amp;theme=light\">M2-L11 – School\nmathematics (in German)<\/a>–<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L14&amp;syslang=de&amp;doclang=de&amp;theme=light\">M2-L14 – School\nmathematics (in German)<\/a> (vectors, coordinate systems, rotations), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L7<\/a> (probability), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L5<\/a>–<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L6<\/a> (matrices), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L9&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L9<\/a> (dynamics), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L12&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L12<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L12&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L12<\/a> (robotics and control),\n<a href=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L2<\/a> (Bayes and causal\nmodels)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Master’s-oriented compulsory elective\nmodule in robotics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>A robot does not know its position simply because it receives a\nsingle GPS reading, camera observation or distance measurement. Every\nsensor may be noisy, unavailable, delayed or systematically inaccurate.\nAt the same time, the robot changes between two measurements.\n<strong>Probabilistic robotics<\/strong> therefore combines previous\nestimates, motion models and new observations in a mathematical\nprocedure. For control, the result provides not only a position but also\n<strong>an indication of the remaining uncertainty<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea: predict, observe, correct<\/h2>\n<p>Imagine a mobile robot located either on the left or on the right of\na corridor. Its motor commands suggest a movement, but the wheels may\nslip. A camera detects a red wall but may misinterpret colours. The\nposition we seek is therefore not simply “the latest measurement”.\nInstead, we calculate it in two steps:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Prediction:<\/strong> Which position is likely according to\nthe motion model?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Measurement correction:<\/strong> How likely would the\nobserved sensor information be at each of these positions?<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>This repeated procedure is called a <strong>Bayes filter<\/strong>. It\ngeneralises Bayes’ formula from M1-L7 to state estimation over time.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols – before the formulae<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Read aloud<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Do not confuse with<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“time index t”<\/td>\n<td>Time of an estimate<\/td>\n<td>Physical time without a unit of time<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“state x at time t”<\/td>\n<td>Position, orientation and velocity, for example<\/td>\n<td>Input vector of an AI model<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“control input u at time t”<\/td>\n<td>Applied motion command<\/td>\n<td>Sensor reading<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_{t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“measurement z at time t”<\/td>\n<td>Received camera, IMU or distance measurement<\/td>\n<td>Unknown true state<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">bel<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{bel}_{t}(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“belief at time t for x”<\/td>\n<td>State probability after the measurements<\/td>\n<td>Guaranteed correct position<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">bel<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{bel}_{t}^{-}(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“predicted belief”<\/td>\n<td>Probability before the current measurement correction<\/td>\n<td>Reciprocal of a probability<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(a \\mid  b)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“probability of a given b”<\/td>\n<td>Conditional distribution or density<\/td>\n<td>Established causal effect<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Σ<\/code><\/td>\n<td>“sigma, sum”<\/td>\n<td>Addition over discrete predecessor states<\/td>\n<td>Covariance symbol in other literature<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∝<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\propto<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“proportional to”<\/td>\n<td>Equal up to a positive normalisation factor<\/td>\n<td>Exact equality without normalisation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“mu”<\/td>\n<td>Estimated mean of the state distribution<\/td>\n<td>Measured quantity with zero error<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“P”<\/td>\n<td>Covariance of the state estimate<\/td>\n<td>Transition matrix from M3-L4<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“R”<\/td>\n<td>Covariance of measurement noise<\/td>\n<td>Rotation matrix from M2-L12<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“Q”<\/td>\n<td>Covariance of process noise<\/td>\n<td>Q-value from M4-L6<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“K”<\/td>\n<td>Kalman gain for measurement correction<\/td>\n<td>Number of neighbours in M4-L12<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“F, B, H”<\/td>\n<td>State transition matrix, control matrix and observation matrix<\/td>\n<td>Functions or entropy<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(superscript)<\/td>\n<td>“transpose”<\/td>\n<td>Rows and columns of a matrix interchanged<\/td>\n<td>Temperature T from M4-L9<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“identity matrix”<\/td>\n<td>Neutral element for matrix multiplication<\/td>\n<td>Electric current<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(\\varphi )<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“rotation matrix for angle phi”<\/td>\n<td>Planar coordinate rotation<\/td>\n<td>Measurement noise covariance R<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“map m”<\/td>\n<td>Unknown map of the environment<\/td>\n<td>Received timestamp from M4-L10<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>:<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{0:t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>:<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_{1:t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>:<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{1:t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“sequence from index zero or one to t, respectively”<\/td>\n<td>Entire sequence of states, measurements or control commands<\/td>\n<td>Single measurement<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The superscript <code>⁻<\/code> in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">bel<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msubsup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{bel}_{t}^{-}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans <strong>prediction before the current measurement<\/strong>, not an\ninverse or negative probability. In the covariance matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe variances of the state components are on the diagonal. The\noff-diagonal entries are the covariances between the state\ncomponents.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The Bayes filter in two mathematical steps<\/h2>\n<p>For a <strong>discrete<\/strong> state space, the prediction is\nobtained as follows:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">bel<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/munder><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∣<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">bel<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{bel}_t^{-}(x_t)=\\sum_{x_{t-1}}p(x_t\\mid x_{t-1},u_t)\\cdot\\operatorname{bel}_{t-1}(x_{t-1})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>In words:<\/strong> We consider all possible previous states.\nFor each one, we multiply its previous probability by the probability of\nreaching the new state through the control command. We then add these\ncontributions. For continuous state spaces, an integral replaces the\nsum.<\/p>\n<p>After the measurement, we proceed <strong>under the usual model\nassumption<\/strong> that, given the current state, the current\nmeasurement requires no additional information from previous\nmeasurements or control inputs:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">bel<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>∝<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∣<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><msubsup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">bel<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{bel}_t(x_t)&amp;\\propto p(z_t\\mid x_t)\\cdot\\operatorname{bel}_t^-(x_t)\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>In words:<\/strong> The new state probability is proportional\nto the probability of observing the actual measurement in that state,\nmultiplied by the predicted probability of the state. We then have to\n<strong>normalise<\/strong> over all states so that their probabilities\nadd up to one. The conditional measurement probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(z_{t} | x_{t})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the <strong>likelihood<\/strong>, not already the final posterior.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Fully worked example: left or right?<\/h3>\n<p>Before the movement, the robot is on the left with probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand on the right with probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe control command has the following modelled transitions:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Starting position<\/th>\n<th>To the left<\/th>\n<th>To the right<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Left<\/td>\n<td>0,8<\/td>\n<td>0,2<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Right<\/td>\n<td>0,3<\/td>\n<td>0,7<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Each row adds up to one. First, we calculate the state\nprediction:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">new left<\/mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>48<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">new right<\/mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>28<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nP(\\text{new left})&amp;=0{,}6\\cdot0{,}8+0{,}4\\cdot0{,}3=0{,}48+0{,}12=0{,}60\\\\\nP(\\text{new right})&amp;=0{,}6\\cdot0{,}2+0{,}4\\cdot0{,}7=0{,}12+0{,}28=0{,}40\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The camera now reports the feature “red wall”. Our simplified\nmeasurement model states that if the robot is on the\n<strong>left<\/strong>, this signal occurs with probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nif it is on the <strong>right<\/strong>, with probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe <strong>unnormalised<\/strong> weights are:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Left: <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>54<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Right: <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>08<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Normalisation: <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>54<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>08<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>62<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Left: }\\quad&amp;0{,}9\\cdot0{,}60=0{,}54\\\\\n\\text{Right: }\\quad&amp;0{,}2\\cdot0{,}40=0{,}08\\\\\n\\text{Normalisation: }\\quad&amp;0{,}54+0{,}08=0{,}62\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Dividing both weights by 0,62 gives:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">left<\/mtext><mo>∣<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">red wall<\/mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>54<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>62<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>27<\/mn><mn>31<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8710<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">right<\/mtext><mo>∣<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">red wall<\/mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>08<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>62<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>4<\/mn><mn>31<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1290<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">sum<\/mtext><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>31<\/mn><mn>31<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nP(\\text{left}\\mid\\text{red wall})&amp;=\\frac{0{,}54}{0{,}62}=\\frac{27}{31}\\approx0{,}8710\\\\\nP(\\text{right}\\mid\\text{red wall})&amp;=\\frac{0{,}08}{0{,}62}=\\frac4{31}\\approx0{,}1290\\\\\n\\text{sum}&amp;=\\frac{31}{31}=1\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Interpretation:<\/strong> The measurement makes “left”\nconsiderably more likely. However, it does not prove that the robot is\nactually on the left. The statement\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>87<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">87{,}1\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndepends directly on the assumed transition and sensor probabilities.\nWith a miscalibrated camera model, the confidence calculated from the\nposterior can be misleading.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Kalman filter: uncertainty as variance<\/h2>\n<p>For <strong>linear dynamics and observation with Gaussian\nnoise<\/strong>, a Kalman filter is often used. In a\n<strong>one-dimensional<\/strong> example, the state estimate consists of\na mean\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA new measurement\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas modelled noise variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe Kalman gain is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mrow><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=\\frac{P^{-}}{P^{-}+R}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> “Divide the predicted state variance by\nthe sum of the predicted state variance and the measurement variance.”\nThe corrected estimate is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\mu_{\\mathrm{neu}}&amp;=\\mu^-+K\\cdot(z-\\mu^-)\\\\\nP_{\\mathrm{neu}}&amp;=(1-K)\\cdot P^-\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> “Take the predicted mean and correct it\nby the Kalman gain multiplied by the difference between the measurement\nand the predicted mean.” The second equation gives the corrected\nvariance in this simplified independent scalar case.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Fully worked example: two measurement sources<\/h3>\n<p>A robot initially estimates its position at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith standard deviation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts variance is therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P^-={(2\\,\\mathrm m)}^2=4\\,\\mathrm m^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn additional measuring device reports\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=12\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith standard deviation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=1\\,\\mathrm m^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>K<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>4<\/mn><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>4<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Innovation<\/mtext><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">new standard deviation<\/mtext><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8944<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nK&amp;=\\frac4{4+1}=\\frac45=0{,}8\\\\\n\\text{Innovation}&amp;=z-\\mu^-=12\\,\\mathrm{m}-10\\,\\mathrm{m}=2\\,\\mathrm{m}\\\\\n\\mu_{\\mathrm{neu}}&amp;=10\\,\\mathrm{m}+0{,}8\\cdot2\\,\\mathrm{m}=11{,}6\\,\\mathrm{m}\\\\\nP_{\\mathrm{neu}}&amp;=(1-0{,}8)\\cdot4\\,\\mathrm{m}^2=0{,}8\\,\\mathrm{m}^2\\\\\n\\text{new standard deviation}&amp;=\\sqrt{0{,}8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\approx0{,}8944\\,\\mathrm{m}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Interpretation:<\/strong> The sensor receives a high weight\nhere because its variance is smaller. The result lies between 10 and 12\nmetres. The remaining uncertainty decreases in the model, but neither\nthe noise nor a possible sensor error disappears. Most importantly, two\n<strong>identical copies of the same measurement<\/strong> are not\nindependent measurements and must not be treated as though they\nindependently reduce uncertainty twice.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Countercheck: duplicate sensor data are not two\nindependent measurements<\/h3>\n<p>In the scalar case shown, the Kalman gain assumes that\n<strong>prediction error and measurement noise are independent<\/strong>.\nCounting two copies of the same measurement as two independent sensors\nis particularly problematic. We again use\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu^-=10\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P^-=4\\,\\mathrm m^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=12\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=1\\,\\mathrm m^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>When correctly included once, the measurement gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_{\\mathrm{neu}}=11{,}6\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_{\\mathrm{neu}}=0{,}8\\,\\mathrm m^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTreating the <strong>identical<\/strong> measurement twice as independent\nproduces an artificially smaller calculated variance:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Double counting as independent (incorrect model):<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>4<\/mn><mn>9<\/mn><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4444<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>53<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>9<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>106<\/mn><mn>9<\/mn><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7778<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Correct result for the same single piece of evidence:<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>4<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>58<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\text{Double counting as independent (incorrect model):}\\\\\nP_{\\mathrm{falsch}}&amp;=\\frac1{1/4+1/1+1/1}\\,\\mathrm{m}^2\n=\\frac49\\,\\mathrm{m}^2\\approx0{,}4444\\,\\mathrm{m}^2\\\\\n\\mu_{\\mathrm{falsch}}&amp;=\\frac{10/4+12/1+12/1}{1/4+1+1}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{53/2}{9/4}\\,\\mathrm{m}=\\frac{106}9\\,\\mathrm{m}\\approx11{,}7778\\,\\mathrm{m}\\\\\n&amp;\\text{Correct result for the same single piece of evidence:}\\\\\nP_{\\mathrm{korrekt}}&amp;=\\frac45\\,\\mathrm{m}^2=0{,}8\\,\\mathrm{m}^2\\\\\n\\mu_{\\mathrm{korrekt}}&amp;=\\frac{58}5\\,\\mathrm{m}=11{,}6\\,\\mathrm{m}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The additional apparent precision arises solely from the\n<strong>incorrect independence assumption<\/strong>. Two different\nsensors may also have correlated errors, for example because of shared\npreprocessing or calibration. In that case, simply weighting them using\nseparate standard deviations is insufficient; their cross-covariance\nmust be included in the model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From a single number to state vectors and covariance\nmatrices<\/h2>\n<p>In a realistic model, the state vector may contain position,\norientation and velocity. Under a linear state model, the prediction\nequations are:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msubsup><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msubsup><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msubsup><mi>P<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msubsup><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mi>F<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\mu_t^-&amp;=F\\cdot\\mu_{t-1}+B\\cdot u_t\\\\\nP_t^-&amp;=F\\cdot P_{t-1}\\cdot F^\\top+Q\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntransfers the effect of control commands to the state. <strong>In\nwords:<\/strong> We apply the modelled motion to the previous mean and\npropagate the previous covariance through that motion. Process noise\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis added, representing unmodelled wheel slip, for example.<\/p>\n<p>For the linear observation, the Kalman gain is a matrix:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mi>P<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msubsup><msup><mi>H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><msubsup><mi>P<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msubsup><msup><mi>H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_t=P_t^{-}H^{\\mathrm{T}}\\left(HP_t^{-}H^{\\mathrm{T}}+R\\right)^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The superscript <code>⁻¹<\/code> here denotes the <strong>inverse of\nthe innovation covariance<\/strong>, provided that it exists. Read aloud:\n“Multiply the predicted covariance by the transpose of the observation\nmatrix and then by the inverse of the total predicted measurement\ncovariance.” In numerically stable implementations, the corresponding\nlinear system is often solved instead of explicitly calculating a matrix\ninverse.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Covariance correction and numerical stability<\/h3>\n<p>For the linear measurement step with <strong>uncorrelated prediction\nerror and measurement noise<\/strong>, the updated covariance can also be\nwritten in the so-called <em>Joseph form<\/em>. It uses the same\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P^-<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas above;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the identity matrix:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><msup><mi>K<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P^{+}=(I-KH)P^{-}{(I-KH)}^{\\mathrm{T}}+KRK^{\\mathrm{T}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> “The remaining prediction error and the\nuncertainty introduced through the measurement are added as covariance\ncontributions.” In the one-dimensional example with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>4<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=\\frac45<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P^-=4\\,\\mathrm m^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=1\\,\\mathrm m^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mn>4<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mn>4<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>25<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>16<\/mn><mn>25<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>20<\/mn><mn>25<\/mn><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>4<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nP^+&amp;=\\left(1-\\frac45\\right)^2\\cdot4\\,\\mathrm{m}^2+\\left(\\frac45\\right)^2\\cdot1\\,\\mathrm{m}^2\\\\\n&amp;=\\frac1{25}\\cdot4\\,\\mathrm{m}^2+\\frac{16}{25}\\cdot1\\,\\mathrm{m}^2\\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{20}{25}\\,\\mathrm{m}^2=\\frac45\\,\\mathrm{m}^2=0{,}8\\,\\mathrm{m}^2\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The Joseph form makes both sources of error visible and can help\npreserve symmetry and positive semidefiniteness in finite floating-point\narithmetic. It does <strong>not<\/strong> remedy an incorrect assumption\nof independence between measurements; for correlated noise, the\ncorresponding model assumptions must be changed.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Scope of validity:<\/strong> In the <strong>exact classical\nBayesian Kalman model<\/strong>, linear state and observation equations\napply, together with appropriately modelled independent, normally\ndistributed process and measurement noise with finite covariance; the\ninitial belief is also Gaussian. For non-Gaussian distributions, linear\nestimators may still be useful, but the stated means and covariances\nthen generally do <strong>not<\/strong> describe the complete exact\nposterior distribution. Correlations between noise sources require\nadditional covariance terms or an extended model. Nonlinear models lead\nto approximations such as the <strong>extended Kalman filter\n(EKF)<\/strong> or <strong>unscented Kalman filter (UKF)<\/strong>.\n<strong>Particle filters<\/strong> represent a belief through weighted\nsamples and can also capture several plausible state regions. The\nrespective limitations in accuracy and computation time differ.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Combining coordinates correctly<\/h2>\n<p>A camera measures features in its own coordinate system; a laser\nsensor and a robot arm often use different axes and reference points.\nOnly after a <strong>geometric transformation<\/strong> can measurements\nbe compared meaningfully. In the plane, for a sensor point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe following holds:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>W<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_W=R(\\varphi)p_S+t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>In words:<\/strong> Rotate the sensor coordinate by the angle\nphi, then translate it into world coordinates by the translation vector\nt. An example with a rotation of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>90<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">°<\/mtext><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">90^{\\text{°}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mn>90<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">°<\/mtext><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>t<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mn>90<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">°<\/mtext><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>W<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nR(90^{\\text{°}})&amp;=\\begin{bmatrix}0&amp;-1\\\\1&amp;0\\end{bmatrix}\\\\\np_S&amp;=(1,0)\\\\\nt&amp;=(2,3)\\\\\nR(90^{\\text{°}})\\cdot p_S&amp;=(0,1)\\\\\np_W&amp;=(0,1)+(2,3)=(2,4)\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The sensor point is therefore at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin world coordinates. A forgotten sign or an interchanged coordinate\nsystem can make measurements appear incompatible. Three dimensions\nadditionally involve spatial rotations and often quaternions or rigid\ntransformations from M2-L12 and M3-L9. The <strong>timestamps<\/strong>\nof the measurements must also correspond: a perfectly transformed camera\nposition from one second ago may already be outdated for a fast-moving\nrobot.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sensor fusion is more than averaging<\/h2>\n<p>When combining camera, LiDAR, IMU, wheel-speed sensor and GPS data,\nat least four things must be checked:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Geometry:<\/strong> In which coordinate system is a\nmeasurement expressed, and are the sensors correctly calibrated\nextrinsically?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Time:<\/strong> Which measurements correspond to the same\nstate of motion; which are delayed or missing?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Statistics:<\/strong> What are the variances, outlier rates\nand <strong>correlations<\/strong>? Identical or derived data must not be\ncounted twice as independent evidence.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Association:<\/strong> Do two features actually describe the\nsame object? An incorrectly associated landmark can produce an\napparently very precise but incorrect position.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>A filter may appear numerically precise and nevertheless be\ninconsistent if the noise model is too optimistic. Diagnostic methods\ninclude examining innovation residuals, covariances and real test\nruns.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>SLAM: localising oneself and mapping the world\nsimultaneously<\/h2>\n<p><strong>SLAM<\/strong> stands for <em>Simultaneous Localization and\nMapping<\/em>: position and the map of the environment are estimated\n<strong>jointly<\/strong>. This is necessary because the map is initially\nunknown, while the robot’s own position depends on recognising\npreviously mapped features. The relationship sought is expressed as a\njoint probability distribution:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>:<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>:<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>:<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;p(x_{0:t},m\\mid z_{1:t},u_{1:t})\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> “The probability distribution of the\nentire state trajectory from the beginning to t and the map m, given all\nmeasurements and control inputs up to t.” The expression makes clear why\nmap errors and position errors are <strong>correlated<\/strong>. A\nwell-known event is <strong>loop closure<\/strong>: the robot recognises\na previously visited place and can correct accumulated position errors\nover several sections of its journey. However,\n<strong>incorrect<\/strong> recognition can displace large areas of a\nmap.<\/p>\n<p>Many SLAM methods formulate the problem as <strong>factor graph\noptimisation<\/strong>; others use EKFs or particle filters. These\nmethods have different requirements for memory, real-time capability,\nloop closure detection and modelling.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Countercheck: without a reference, the absolute\nSLAM position is not unique<\/h3>\n<p>If a simple one-dimensional robot model observes only the\n<strong>relative distance<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=m-x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbetween robot position\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand landmark\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na measurement\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=3\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncannot determine the absolute position. For example, both of the\nfollowing states produce the same measurement:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Model A: <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>⟹<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Model B: <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>13<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>⟹<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">In general: <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mrow><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\"> gives for every <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mrow><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\"> the same measurement.<\/mtext><\/mrow><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Model A: }\\quad x=2\\,\\mathrm m,\\quad m=5\\,\\mathrm m\n &amp;\\implies z=m-x=3\\,\\mathrm m\\\\\n\\text{Model B: }\\quad x=10\\,\\mathrm m,\\quad m=13\\,\\mathrm m\n &amp;\\implies z=m-x=3\\,\\mathrm m\\\\\n\\text{In general: }\\quad (x+c,m+c)&amp;\\text{ gives for every }c\\text{ the same measurement.}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This degree of freedom is called <em>gauge freedom<\/em>, or a lack of\nabsolute anchoring. A map may be geometrically consistent internally\neven though its common origin has shifted. Additional information is\nrequired to establish a global reference system, such as an anchored\ninitial position or a suitable external reference. In two or three\ndimensions, global rotations may also belong to these unobservable\ndegrees of freedom. The example is explicitly a <strong>simplified\nrelative observation model<\/strong>, not a claim that every real SLAM\nsetup is fundamentally unidentifiable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>Multimodal sensor fusion contributes to position estimation for\ndelivery robots, warehouse vehicles and autonomous platforms.\nCombinations of camera images, inertial data and laser scans can be\nuseful even under changing sensor conditions, provided their\nuncertainties are modelled honestly. For AI agents with physical\neffects, the belief provides a basis for <strong>explicitly\nincorporating uncertainty into planning<\/strong>, rather than treating a\nsingle apparently certain value as the truth.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Bayes filters rely on <strong>Markov modelling<\/strong>;\nunaccounted-for long-term influences violate this assumption.<\/li>\n<li>Classical Kalman formulae require well-defined dynamics,\nobservations, covariances and appropriate distributional assumptions.\nNonlinear and multimodal uncertainties require other methods or\napproximations.<\/li>\n<li>Sensors are often <strong>dependent<\/strong> in time or through\nshared preprocessing. Ignoring correlation leads to artificially small\nvariance.<\/li>\n<li>A low estimated variance is <strong>not proof of safety<\/strong>.\nSensor blinding, incorrect associations, environmental changes and\nmechanical limits must be checked separately.<\/li>\n<li>SLAM may remain ambiguous in repetitive, symmetrical or dynamic\nscenes; sensing alone does not solve every observability problem.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connections to existing and subsequent topics<\/h2>\n<p><strong>School M2-L11–School M2-L14<\/strong> explain spatial\ngeometry, <strong>M1-L6<\/strong> matrices,\n<strong>M2-L12/M3-L12<\/strong> robotics and control,\n<strong>M4-L2<\/strong> conditional probability, and\n<strong>M4-L9<\/strong> the reliability of predictions.\n<strong>M4-L10<\/strong> describes the distribution and causal ordering\nof messages between robots. <strong>M4-L12<\/strong> uses similarity\nsearch, for example in recognising images or landmarks; however, a\nsimilar feature vector is not yet a geometrically validated location.\nThe internal section review of both compulsory elective lessons is now\ncomplete; external subject review, source and rights checks, and checks\non actual devices and screen readers are still pending before public\nrelease.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Documented source status:<\/strong> 04.11.2026<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sebastian Thrun, Wolfram Burgard and Dieter Fox: <em>Probabilistic\nRobotics<\/em>. MIT Press, 2005, chapters on recursive estimation,\nGaussian filters and SLAM. <a\nhref=\"https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262201629/probabilistic-robotics/\">https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262201629/probabilistic-robotics/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>Hugh Durrant-Whyte and Tim Bailey (2006): <em>Simultaneous\nLocalization and Mapping: Part I<\/em>. IEEE Robotics &amp; Automation\nMagazine. <a\nhref=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/MRA.2006.1638022\">https://doi.org/10.1109/MRA.2006.1638022<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The numerical examples were constructed specifically for this course.\nExternal sources are <strong>not a runtime dependency<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_mathematics_en:M4-L12":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L12\">M4-L12 – Compulsory elective: Vector search, database indexes and approximate nearest neighbours<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Choice of study: AI, computer science and specialised fields<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> University studies (elective /\nspecialisation)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L11&amp;syslang=de&amp;doclang=de&amp;theme=light\">M2-L11 – School\nMathematics (in German)<\/a> (dot product, angles), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L5<\/a>–<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L8<\/a> (vectors, matrices and\nnumerical methods), <a href=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a>\n(graphs), <a href=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L11&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L11<\/a>\n(embeddings), <a href=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L1<\/a> (language\nmodels) and <a href=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L3<\/a> (graph\nmodels)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Compulsory elective module in AI\ninfrastructure, advanced Bachelor’s to Master’s level.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is this about?<\/h2>\n<p>An AI system needs to find the most suitable entries among a very\nlarge collection of text passages, images or measurement series. A\nmodern approach converts such data into <strong>numerical\nvectors<\/strong>. It then searches for the stored vectors that are\nclosest to the query according to a chosen <strong>similarity\nmeasure<\/strong>. With millions of entries, comparing the query directly\nwith every vector can become expensive. Specialised <strong>database\nindexes<\/strong> can therefore find suitable candidates much faster, but\nsometimes miss a vector that is actually closer. Mathematics helps us\nunderstand this trade-off between <strong>retrieval quality, running\ntime and memory requirements<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Basic idea: Similarity is a decision made by the model<\/h2>\n<p>A language model or a separate encoder generates a vector from a\npassage of text. This is called an <em>embedding<\/em>. With a suitable\nmodel, two similar texts may be assigned vectors that point in similar\ndirections. This is a <strong>learned, measurable\nrepresentation<\/strong>, not a guarantee that both texts mean the same\nthing, are true or make the same legal statement.<\/p>\n<p>A search therefore involves two distinct steps:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Represent:<\/strong> Map the text, image or data object to a\nvector.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Compare and retrieve:<\/strong> A distance or similarity\nvalue ranks stored vectors relative to the query.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>If the representation is poorly chosen, even the most mathematically\naccurate nearest-neighbour algorithm cannot conjure up good results in\nterms of content.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>New symbols – before the formulae<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol / term<\/th>\n<th>Read aloud<\/th>\n<th>Meaning here<\/th>\n<th>Do not confuse with<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“cue, query vector”<\/td>\n<td>Vector of the search query<\/td>\n<td>Q-value from M4-L6 or attention Q from M4-L1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{i}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“x sub i”<\/td>\n<td>Stored vector i<\/td>\n<td>Spatial position or state in M4-L11<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“dee”<\/td>\n<td>Number of components of a vector<\/td>\n<td>Distance symbol when written as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(x,y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>j<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“index i and index j”<\/td>\n<td>i numbers stored vectors; j numbers their individual components<\/td>\n<td>Time variable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“en”<\/td>\n<td>Number of stored vectors<\/td>\n<td>Number of servers in M4-L10<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“kay”<\/td>\n<td>Number of neighbours requested<\/td>\n<td>Kalman gain K in M4-L11<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Vert x\\Vert _{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“two-norm of x”<\/td>\n<td>Euclidean length of x<\/td>\n<td>Absolute value of a scalar<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\cdot y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“x dot y”<\/td>\n<td>Sum of component-wise products<\/td>\n<td>Logical AND<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sim<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{sim}_{\\cos}(q,x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“cosine similarity of q and x”<\/td>\n<td>Alignment of directions after normalisation<\/td>\n<td>Probability calibration<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">dist<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{dist}_2(q,x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“Euclidean distance from q to x”<\/td>\n<td>Length of the difference vector<\/td>\n<td>Same direction with different lengths<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg min<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname*{arg\\,min}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>“argument of the minimum”<\/td>\n<td>Index of an entry with minimum distance<\/td>\n<td>Numerical value of the minimum itself<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>top-k<\/code><\/td>\n<td>“the top kay results”<\/td>\n<td>k highest-ranked search results<\/td>\n<td>All relevant entries<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ANN<\/code><\/td>\n<td>“Approximate Nearest Neighbours”<\/td>\n<td>Approximate nearest neighbours<\/td>\n<td>Exact k-NN search<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>HNSW<\/code><\/td>\n<td>“Hierarchical Navigable Small World”<\/td>\n<td>Navigable multi-level neighbourhood graph<\/td>\n<td>Guaranteed exact search<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IVF<\/code><\/td>\n<td>“Inverted File Index”<\/td>\n<td>Group-based vector index<\/td>\n<td>Inverted word index in ordinary full-text search<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>PQ<\/code><\/td>\n<td>“Product Quantization”<\/td>\n<td>Compressed representation of subvectors<\/td>\n<td>Probability measure<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Recall@k<\/code><\/td>\n<td>“recall at kay”<\/td>\n<td>Proportion of exact top-k neighbours found by approximate\nsearch<\/td>\n<td>Semantic correctness of all answers<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>p95<\/code><\/td>\n<td>“95th percentile”<\/td>\n<td>Value below which 95% of observed response times lie<\/td>\n<td>Guaranteed maximum duration<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>GiB<\/code><\/td>\n<td>“gibibyte”<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>30<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">bytes<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^{30}\\,\\text{bytes}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Decimal gigabyte:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>9<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">bytes<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^9\\,\\text{bytes}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>Terminological pitfall:<\/strong> In some technical texts, the\nnumber of vectors is denoted by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrather than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes a distance function. The definitions in the table apply\nthroughout this lesson. Cosine similarity is <strong>not a\nprobability<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three different similarity measures<\/h2>\n<p>For two non-zero real vectors q and x, the <strong>cosine\nsimilarity<\/strong> is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sim_cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{sim\\_cos}(q,x)=\\frac{q\\cdot x}{\\lVert q\\rVert_2\\cdot\\lVert x\\rVert_2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>In words:<\/strong> Divide the dot product of the two vectors\nby the product of their lengths. A value of one means the same\ndirection, minus one means opposite directions, and zero means a right\nangle for non-zero vectors. The sign and range of values described here\napply to <strong>real<\/strong> vectors with a Euclidean inner\nproduct.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>Euclidean distance<\/strong>, by contrast, is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">dist<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{dist}_2(q,x)=\\lVert q-x\\rVert_2=\\sqrt{\\sum_j{(q_j-x_j)}^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>In words:<\/strong> Subtract each component of x from the\ncorresponding component of the query, square the differences, add them\nand take the square root. Unlike cosine similarity, this distance also\ntakes the <strong>length<\/strong> of a vector into account.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>dot product<\/strong> itself can also serve as a ranking\nscore, for example if a model has been trained specifically for this\npurpose. However, for similar directions, it often favours vectors with\na larger norm. The training objective of the particular embedding model\nis therefore relevant when choosing the metric.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Fully worked example: Cosine similarity and\ndistance rank differently<\/h3>\n<p>The query is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=(1,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThree stored vectors are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=(2,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=(1,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=(-1,-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-mathematics-en/media/img/000045_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Query q=(1,1) and data points A=(2,2), B=(1,0), C=(-1,-1). Cosine similarity ranks A before B before C; Euclidean distance ranks B before A before C.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Query q=(1,1) and data points A=(2,2),\nB=(1,0), C=(-1,-1). Cosine similarity ranks A before B before C;\nEuclidean distance ranks B before A before C.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The same vector example produces different rankings: cosine\nsimilarity A–B–C, Euclidean distance B–A–C.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Step 1: Lengths and dot products.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>8<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>q<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>q<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>q<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\lVert q\\rVert_2&amp;=\\sqrt{1^2+1^2}=\\sqrt2\\\\\n\\lVert A\\rVert_2&amp;=\\sqrt{2^2+2^2}=\\sqrt8=2\\sqrt2\\\\\n\\lVert B\\rVert_2&amp;=1\\\\\n\\lVert C\\rVert_2&amp;=\\sqrt2\\\\\nq\\cdot A&amp;=1\\cdot2+1\\cdot2=4\\\\\nq\\cdot B&amp;=1\\cdot1+1\\cdot0=1\\\\\nq\\cdot C&amp;=1\\cdot(-1)+1\\cdot(-1)=-2\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: Cosine similarities.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sim<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>4<\/mn><mrow><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>4<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sim<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7071<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sim<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>⋅<\/mo><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{sim}_{\\cos}(q,A)&amp;=\\frac4{\\sqrt2\\cdot2\\sqrt2}=\\frac44=1\\\\\n\\operatorname{sim}_{\\cos}(q,B)&amp;=\\frac1{\\sqrt2\\cdot1}=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\approx0{,}7071\\\\\n\\operatorname{sim}_{\\cos}(q,C)&amp;=\\frac{-2}{\\sqrt2\\cdot\\sqrt2}=-1\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The <strong>cosine similarity ranking<\/strong> is therefore\n<code>A before B before C<\/code>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Step 3: Euclidean distances.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">dist<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4142<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">dist<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">dist<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>8<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8284<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{dist}_2(q,A)&amp;=\\sqrt{{(1-2)}^2+{(1-2)}^2}=\\sqrt2\\approx1{,}4142\\\\\n\\operatorname{dist}_2(q,B)&amp;=\\sqrt{{(1-1)}^2+{(1-0)}^2}=1\\\\\n\\operatorname{dist}_2(q,C)&amp;=\\sqrt{{(1+1)}^2+{(1+1)}^2}=\\sqrt8\\approx2{,}8284\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The <strong>distance ranking<\/strong>, by contrast, is\n<code>B before A before C<\/code>. Both rankings are mathematically\ncorrect – they answer <strong>different questions<\/strong>. For\n<strong>normalised<\/strong> vectors of length one, the following useful\nrelationship holds:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sim_cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">if <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">‖<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lVert q-x\\rVert_2^2=2-2\\cdot\\operatorname{sim\\_cos}(q,x),\\quad\\text{if }\\lVert q\\rVert_2=\\lVert x\\rVert_2=1\\text{.}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> “For unit vectors, the squared Euclidean\ndistance equals two minus twice the cosine similarity.” The two measures\ntherefore give the same ranking <strong>only under this normalisation\nassumption<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exact k-nearest-neighbour search<\/h2>\n<p>For each query, we can calculate the distance to <strong>all\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvectors<\/strong>, then sort them and take the best\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor d-dimensional vectors, a naive pass requires approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N\\cdot d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\narithmetic operations on the components to calculate the distances,\nexcluding the cost of selection. This linear full scan is simple, easy\nto verify and may be appropriate for small data collections or highly\nefficient hardware.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Exact search<\/strong> means that the actual best\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nentries according to the <strong>specified mathematical measure<\/strong>\nare found, provided the implemented numerical comparisons and filters\nare correct. With an <strong>access or metadata filter<\/strong>, the\nreference set is explicitly restricted to <strong>permitted<\/strong>\nentries; the result must provide the best k entries <strong>within this\nset<\/strong>. A correct search ranking must not first retrieve\nprohibited results and then filter them out in a way that causes\nauthorised top-k results to be missed. Exact metric search does\n<strong>not<\/strong> mean that the vectors found are the most relevant\nin the ordinary linguistic sense.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Counterexample: Why access filters matter before\ntop-k selection<\/h3>\n<p>Consider a one-dimensional query\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand three data points; the measure is Euclidean distance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|q-x|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe want <strong>one<\/strong> result\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>),\nwhich must come only from authorised entries:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Entry<\/th>\n<th>Position x<\/th>\n<th>Distance to q<\/th>\n<th>Authorised?<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>no<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>yes<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C<\/td>\n<td>2<\/td>\n<td>2<\/td>\n<td>yes<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Formula, sketch or calculation scheme · Original notation<\/p>\n<pre><code>Unfiltered top-1: A  → after access filter: no results\nCorrect filtered top-1 over {B,C}: B with distance 1<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The example does not prove that a particular index filter always\nworks incorrectly. It shows that a query which <strong>first forms an\noverly small, unauthorised candidate set<\/strong> and then removes\nprohibited entries may return an empty result even though authorised\nresults exist. A correct system must enforce access rights\n<strong>and<\/strong> evaluate the ranking against the permitted\nreference set. Depending on the implementation, ANN filters may require\na different search or post-filtering method to achieve this.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Approximate Nearest Neighbours (ANN): Speed versus\nguaranteed completeness<\/h2>\n<p>ANN indexes examine only part of the search space thoroughly. This\noften saves work, but may miss a mathematically closer vector. Two\nimportant families are:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>HNSW:<\/strong> Connects vectors in neighbourhood graphs at\nseveral levels. The search starts with a coarse overview at an upper\nlevel and navigates down to lower levels. A suitable search parameter\ncan improve recall, but usually at the cost of more work. The method\noffers no universal guarantee that it will <strong>always<\/strong>\nreturn the exact k neighbours.<\/li>\n<li><strong>IVF and product quantisation:<\/strong> Clustering divides\nthe space into search groups. Only selected groups are examined;\nquantisation can represent vectors using short codes. This saves memory\nand distance calculations, but introduces approximation errors. Whether\nIVF, PQ or their combination is suitable depends on the data structure\nand search requirements.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>An index is <strong>not a magical mathematical substitute<\/strong>\nfor the meaning of a vector. When embeddings change, the database must\nbe adapted to the new representation or re-indexed. Metadata filters,\ndeletions, updates and data access rights also affect practical search\nquality.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Recall@k: How many true neighbours were found?<\/h2>\n<p>For evaluation, we often begin by constructing an <strong>exact\nreference search<\/strong> for a test set. The comparison is not made\nagainst a subjective answer rating, but against the k mathematically\nnearest data points under the same definition of distance.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Recall@k<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">_<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">_<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>∩<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">_<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">_<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>k<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Recall@k}=\\frac{\\lvert\\mathrm{ANN\\_top\\_k}\\cap\\mathrm{exakt\\_top\\_k}\\rvert}{k}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Read aloud:<\/strong> “Count how many entries occur in both\ntop-k sets and divide by k.” This measure assumes unique entries and a\ndefined way of handling distance ties.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Fully worked example: One result is missing<\/h3>\n<p>For the query in the previous example, the exact top-2 entries by\n<strong>cosine similarity ranking<\/strong> are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSuppose an approximate index incorrectly returns\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">_<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">_<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">_<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">_<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Intersection<\/mtext><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Recall@2<\/mtext><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm{exakt\\_top\\_2}&amp;=\\{A,B\\}\\\\\n\\mathrm{ANN\\_top\\_2}&amp;=\\{B,C\\}\\\\\n\\text{Intersection}&amp;=\\{B\\}\\\\\n\\text{Recall@2}&amp;=\\frac12=0{,}5=50\\,\\%\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In this single test, the ANN search found <strong>only half<\/strong>\nof the two exact best vectors. This tells us neither whether\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nprovides the correct answer to a real question nor how fast the search\nwas. A robust benchmark tests many queries, data distributions, filters\nand index configurations. Important measures include, for example, the\nmean, <strong>p95 latency<\/strong>, memory, throughput and update\ncosts.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"abschnitt-11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>With equal distances, the reference is not\nautomatically unique<\/h3>\n<p>The quotient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>∩<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>k<\/mi><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{|\\mathrm{ANN}_{\\mathrm{top}\\,k}\\cap\\mathrm{exakt}_{\\mathrm{top}\\,k}|}{k}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nneeds a clearly defined exact reference. If data points have\n<strong>identical distances<\/strong>, several correct top-k sets may\nexist. Example:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nboth points have distance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>If the reference arbitrarily chooses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbut the ANN index returns\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nsimple set-based recall appears to be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<strong>even though B is just as close<\/strong>. A benchmark must\ntherefore resolve ties deterministically, for example using a fixed\nordering of IDs, or use a suitable tie-aware quality measure. The\nconvention must be documented identically for the exact search and ANN\ncomparison.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>How much memory does a vector database require?<\/h2>\n<p>Consider one million vectors, each with 768 dimensions. If each\ncomponent is stored as a 32-bit floating-point number, it requires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">bytes<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\text{bytes}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe space occupied by the numerical data alone is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>N<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">vectors<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>d<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>768<\/mn><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">values per vector<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Memory<\/mtext><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">bytes<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>768<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">bytes<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>072<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">bytes<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>072<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/mrow><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(decimal gigabytes)<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>861<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/mrow><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(gibibytes)<\/mtext><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nN&amp;=1\\,000\\,000\\quad\\text{vectors}\\\\\nd&amp;=768\\quad\\text{values per vector}\\\\\n\\text{Memory}&amp;=N\\cdot d\\cdot4\\,\\text{bytes}\\\\\n&amp;=1\\,000\\,000\\cdot768\\cdot4\\,\\text{bytes}\\\\\n&amp;=3\\,072\\,000\\,000\\,\\text{bytes}\\\\\n&amp;=3{,}072\\,\\mathrm{GB}\\quad\\text{(decimal gigabytes)}\\\\\n&amp;\\approx2{,}861\\,\\mathrm{GiB}\\quad\\text{(gibibytes)}\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The <strong>actual database<\/strong> also needs IDs, documents or\nreferences, metadata, index graphs, mappings and administrative data. An\nideal encoding with exactly <strong>one byte per component<\/strong>\nwould require\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>768<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">bytes<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">768\\,000\\,000\\,\\text{bytes}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the vector codes alone, but would involve quantisation errors and\nfurther overhead. Product quantisation can encode vectors in very\ndifferent ways; the one-byte calculation is only a <strong>simple model\nfor comparing storage requirements<\/strong>, not an automatically valid\nPQ result.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The complete AI retrieval process<\/h2>\n<p>An AI system using document-supported generation\n(<em>Retrieval-Augmented Generation<\/em>, RAG) can work as follows:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Prepare documents:<\/strong> Divide texts into meaningful,\nuniquely identified sections. Rights and source information remain\nattributable to the corresponding material.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Calculate embeddings:<\/strong> A suitable encoder converts\neach section into a vector.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Build the index:<\/strong> Vectors and metadata are assigned\na search index (possibly ANN).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Map the query:<\/strong> Embed the search text using a\ncompatible encoder.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Search for and filter candidates:<\/strong> The database\nreturns suitable documents that the user is <strong>authorised<\/strong>\nto access. Access rights must not be bypassed through simplifications in\nANN search or filtering.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Reorder if necessary:<\/strong> A more powerful model\nevaluates the candidates in relation to the original query. This stage\nis called <em>reranking<\/em>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Generate and substantiate the answer:<\/strong> The language\nmodel may cite sources only for claims that they actually support. High\nvector similarity alone is <strong>not evidence of truth<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Retrieval quality and answer quality should be measured\n<strong>separately<\/strong>. Perfect recall in the index says nothing\nabout whether the answer is summarised correctly. Conversely, a language\nmodel can produce convincing text even though the search has failed to\nsupply crucial counterevidence.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>High-dimensional spaces and practical pitfalls<\/h2>\n<p>In high dimensions, differences between distances become less\ninformative for certain data distributions; this is <strong>not a\nuniversal claim<\/strong> that vector search in high dimensions is\nfundamentally impossible. Actual quality depends on training,\ndistribution, normalisation, the measure, the intrinsic properties of\nthe data and the structure of the index.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Normalisation:<\/strong> In cosine search, zero vectors must\nnot be divided by their norm. Unit normalisation affects the dot product\nand Euclidean distance.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Filters:<\/strong> When filtering by customer affiliation or\nrecency, an unsuitable filtering or indexing method can miss relevant\nresults or increase running times.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Access rights:<\/strong> Tenant isolation and deletion\nrequirements are security requirements. A stored vector may itself still\nallow sensitive information to be inferred.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Changes:<\/strong> A new encoder can alter the geometric\nspace; values from different model versions cannot be compared without\nverification.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Benchmarks:<\/strong> An index may be fast on average and\nstill have problematic extreme cases. In particular, p95/p99 and recall\nafter filtering should be evaluated.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Comparison measure:<\/strong> Recall@k measures agreement\nwith <strong>exact metric search<\/strong>, not semantic relevance as\njudged by humans.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is it useful for?<\/h2>\n<p>Vector search supports local knowledge libraries, image and audio\nsearch, code research, recommendation methods, searches for similar\nsensor patterns and language model retrieval. For\n<strong>M4-L11<\/strong>, similarity indexes can speed up the search for\ncandidate visual landmarks; <strong>geometric and probabilistic\nverification<\/strong> of the matches remains necessary. For\n<strong>M4-L1<\/strong> and <strong>M2-L11<\/strong>, the lesson bridges\nthe gap between a trained embedding model and its reliable use in a\ndatabase system.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>An embedding models similarity only according to the data, training\nand chosen distance measure. It is not general proof of truth, security\nor identity.<\/li>\n<li>ANN methods can miss mathematically better candidates. Index names\nsuch as HNSW or IVF alone offer <strong>no guaranteed latency or recall\nrate<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>All memory figures refer to explicitly stated data types and do\n<strong>not<\/strong> include the complete storage requirements of a\ndatabase.<\/li>\n<li>The equivalence of cosine and Euclidean rankings in the form used\nabove holds <strong>only for unit-normalised vectors<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>A RAG system additionally needs critical evaluation of sources,\naccess control, up-to-date data and end-to-end tests. A correct search\nresult does not prevent hallucinations.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connections and conclusion of the F series<\/h2>\n<p><strong>School Mathematics M2-L11<\/strong> explains the dot product;\n<strong>M1-L5<\/strong> linear algebra; <strong>M1-L8<\/strong> numerical\nlimitations; <strong>M2-L11<\/strong> embeddings; <strong>M4-L1<\/strong>\nlanguage processing; <strong>M4-L3<\/strong> graph models.\n<strong>M4-L10<\/strong> shows how distributed nodes can keep data and\nindexes consistent; <strong>M4-L11<\/strong> explains how to verify\ngeometric candidates. M4-L12 concludes the first series of elective\nlessons on AI and computer science topics. The additional specialist\napplications M4-L13 (architecture and construction) and M4-L14\n(aerospace) can be read independently as elective topics. They are not\nofficial compulsory university modules.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Documented source status:<\/strong> 4 November 2026<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Yu. A. Malkov and D. A. Yashunin: <em>Efficient and Robust\nApproximate Nearest Neighbor Search Using Hierarchical Navigable Small\nWorld Graphs<\/em>. IEEE TPAMI 42(4), 2020; DOI\n10.1109/TPAMI.2018.2889473. <a\nhref=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TPAMI.2018.2889473\">https://doi.org/10.1109/TPAMI.2018.2889473<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>Jeff Johnson, Matthijs Douze and Hervé Jégou: <em>Billion-scale\nsimilarity search with GPUs<\/em> (2017). <a\nhref=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.08734\">https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.08734<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The calculations are <strong>independent teaching examples<\/strong>,\nnot measurements taken from the publications. The websites are not\nloaded during offline use.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_mathematics_en:M4-L13":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L13\">M4-L13 – Mathematics for architecture, building construction and engineering<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Choice of study: AI, computer science and specialised fields<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Not specified<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> An elective and applied field for\nprospective architecture and civil engineering students; not a complete\ncourse in structural mechanics or building physics, nor a basis for\ncalculations suitable for building control purposes.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Guiding question:<\/strong> How does a drawing become a\nspatial idea, a structural component become a mechanical model, and a\ndesign become a justified estimate of quantities?<\/p>\n<p>Architecture combines spatial design with technical constraints. A\nfloor plan must translate into actual dimensions in a building. A floor\nslab must transfer forces to load-bearing elements. A window affects not\nonly daylight but also the thermal balance. For all of this, students do\nnot need entirely new mathematics, but several familiar mathematical\ntools in a common context.<\/p>\n<p>TU Dresden’s university modules <strong>Descriptive Geometry and\nCAD<\/strong>, <strong>Fundamentals of Structural Design<\/strong> and\n<strong>Building Climatology<\/strong> show why these applications\nalready occur in architecture degree programmes. The examples below,\nhowever, are <strong>original didactic model cases<\/strong>, not\nmaterial taken from module descriptions or templates for structural\ndesign calculations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Drawing means establishing a scale<\/h2>\n<p>A plan at a scale of <strong>1:100<\/strong> reduces a real length by\na factor of 100. A four-metre-long wall corresponds to four centimetres\non the plan. In other words, the number on paper is not the actual size;\nthe corresponding unit and scale are part of the information.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Actual length<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Representation at 1:100<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Representation at 1:50<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>4.00 m<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4.0 cm<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">8.0 cm<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0.80 m<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">8.0 mm<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">16.0 mm<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0.25 m<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2.5 mm<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5.0 mm<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The simple formula is <strong>plan length = actual length / scale\ndenominator<\/strong>, provided that both lengths are first converted to\nthe same unit. Anyone working in millimetres in a CAD file must still\ncheck which unit the result actually uses when exporting and\nprinting.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Important:<\/strong> Lengths reduced to scale change linearly;\nareas change by the square of the factor, and volumes by its cube. A\nsquare with a side length of one metre has a side length of one\ncentimetre on a 1:100 plan, and therefore a plan area of only one square\ncentimetre. Areas must not be converted back using the length factor\nalone.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From floor area to a spatial model<\/h2>\n<p>A rectangular room measuring\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm m\\times6\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>\nhas a floor area of\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>24<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">24\\,\\mathrm m^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>.\nWith a clear height of <strong>2.8 m<\/strong>, the idealised cuboid has\na volume of\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>67<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">67{,}2\\,\\mathrm m^3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>.\nThe model is useful, for example, for an initial understanding of room\nsizes and volumetric flow rates, but it accounts for neither wall\nthicknesses nor sloping ceilings.<\/p>\n<p>For more complicated floor plans, areas are subdivided: an L-shaped\nroom can be calculated as the sum of two non-overlapping rectangles or\nas one large rectangle minus a cut-out. Descriptive geometry adds\nanother consideration: a three-dimensional object provides different\ninformation in plan, elevation and side view. A single viewing angle is\nnot always sufficient to determine the spatial construction\nunambiguously.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Forces are vectors, not just numbers<\/h2>\n<p>Consider a horizontal beam bridge supported at both ends. A uniformly\ndistributed load is described by the <strong>line load q<\/strong> in\nkilonewtons per metre. Its total resultant force is the product of the\nload intensity and the length.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original worked example:<\/strong> An idealised beam is\n<strong>4 m<\/strong> long; a downward load of <strong>3 kN/m<\/strong>\nacts along its entire length. The resultant force is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=q\\cdot L=3\\,\\mathrm{kN/m}\\cdot4\\,\\mathrm m=12\\,\\mathrm{kN}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>With symmetrical loading and two idealised supports, each carries\n<strong>6 kN<\/strong>. This follows from the condition that the sum of\nall vertical forces must be zero in a system at rest. For asymmetrical\nloading, equilibrium of moments would also be required.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-mathematics-en/media/img/000083_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Sketch of a beam supported at both ends with a uniformly distributed load and two support reactions\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Sketch of a beam supported at both ends\nwith a uniformly distributed load and two support reactions<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Independently constructed teaching sketch; not a real structural\ndesign calculation.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The bending moment makes internal loading visible<\/h2>\n<p>The support reaction is not the whole story. Within a structural\ncomponent, too, the forces acting on it must remain in equilibrium. For\na simply supported beam with a constant line load, this leads to a\nmaximum bending moment at the centre:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mn>8<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M_{\\max}=\\frac{q\\cdot L^2}{8}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>Using the example values gives\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>4<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mn>8<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M_{\\max}=\\frac{3\\cdot4^2}{8}=6\\,\\mathrm{kN\\,m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>.\nBecause the length enters the equation quadratically, a slightly greater\nspan can cause a considerably greater bending moment. If only L is\ndoubled while q remains unchanged, M_max increases by a factor of\nfour.<\/p>\n<p>The stress in an idealised elastic cross-section also depends on its\nsection modulus. For a rectangular cross-section bending about its\nstrong axis, the simplified model gives:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mn>6<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=\\frac{b\\cdot h^2}{6}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mi>W<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_{\\max}=\\frac{M_{\\max}}{W}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>If, solely for practice, we take\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=0{,}20\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>\nand\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=0{,}40\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>,\nthen\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>00533<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W\\approx0{,}00533\\,\\mathrm m^3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>\nand\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>13<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_{\\max}\\approx1{,}13\\,\\mathrm{MPa}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>.\nThis is only a mathematical illustrative example for a homogeneous beam\nunder the modelling assumptions of elementary bending theory. It makes\n<strong>no<\/strong> statement about the load-bearing capacity of\nreinforced concrete, timber or an actual structure. Material behaviour,\nsafety factors, shear, deflection, connections, stability and standards\nare not included here.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why the depth of a beam can be so important<\/h2>\n<p>In the example, the section modulus increases in proportion to the\nsquare of the cross-sectional depth. The second moment of area of a\nrectangle, for the corresponding choice of axis, is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mn>12<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=\\frac{b\\cdot h^3}{12}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand depends on the third power of the depth. This reveals an important\ndesign principle: the spatial distribution of material changes the\nmechanical effect. Saying that a component contains ‘more material’ is\nnot a sufficient description – <strong>where<\/strong> the material is\nlocated is also decisive.<\/p>\n<p>In real buildings, load cases, cross-sectional shape, self-weight,\ndeformation, connecting elements and regulations also determine the\nassessment. A quick mathematical comparison can explain a structural\nidea, but can never replace professional structural design.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mathematics for architecture also includes building\nphysics<\/h2>\n<p>For an initial model of steady-state heat transfer, the heat flow\nrate through a homogeneous building surface can be estimated using the\n<strong>U-value<\/strong>, the area and the temperature difference:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=U\\cdot A\\cdot\\Delta T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>Suppose an idealised surface has\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=80\\,\\mathrm m^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>,\nits U-value is\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}30\\,\\mathrm W/(\\mathrm m^2\\cdot\\mathrm K)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>,\nand the temperature difference between indoors and outdoors is\n<strong>20 K<\/strong>. The calculation then gives\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>480<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=480\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>.\nThis means an instantaneous rate of heat loss in the simple model,\n<strong>not<\/strong> automatically a daily or annual energy consumption.\nDetermining energy demand also requires the evolution over time,\nventilation, thermal bridges, solar gains, internal heat sources and\nfurther conditions.<\/p>\n<p>Here in particular, the interaction between mathematics and\narchitecture becomes apparent: geometry provides areas, physics provides\nmodels, and analysis describes how quantities can change over time.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From the beam model to simulation<\/h2>\n<p>For complex building structures, a single formula is often\ninsufficient. The structure can be divided into many smaller sections,\nand approximate solutions calculated for them. An important approach is\nthe <strong>finite element method (FEM)<\/strong>, introduced in\nMathematics M3-L3. In civil engineering it combines geometry, material\nmodels and boundary conditions.<\/p>\n<p>The quality of FEM output depends on more than its computational\nprecision. A beam with incorrectly modelled supports can produce a\nmisleading solution even with a very fine mesh. Therefore,\n<strong>modelling assumptions, units and plausibility checks<\/strong>\nmust come before any potential design decision.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What else belongs in an actual architecture or civil\nengineering degree programme?<\/h2>\n<p>This chapter provides an initial orientation, not a substitute for\nthe courses themselves. Depending on the degree programme, further\nsubjects include building materials, construction, building climatology,\nsurveying, construction informatics, fire protection, cost planning and\ndesign studios. Architects also need design and communication skills\nthat cannot be reduced to a formula. Structural design, in turn,\nrequires specific engineering knowledge and application of the relevant\nrules.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Further learning path:<\/strong> School Mathematics (German)\n<a href=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L11&amp;syslang=de&amp;doclang=de&amp;theme=light\">M1-L11<\/a>\n(similarity/scale), School Mathematics (German) <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L21&amp;syslang=de&amp;doclang=de&amp;theme=light\">M1-L21<\/a> (vectors),\nSchool Mathematics (German) <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L11&amp;syslang=de&amp;doclang=de&amp;theme=light\">M2-L11<\/a> (dot\nproduct), School Mathematics (German) <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L12&amp;syslang=de&amp;doclang=de&amp;theme=light\">M2-L12<\/a> (planes), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L4<\/a> (gradient) and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L3<\/a> (finite elements). The new\nsection can be read as an <strong>optional specialist\napplication<\/strong> after these fundamentals.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Comprehension questions for self-assessment<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>How large does a 2.50 m wide opening appear on a plan at 1:50?\n<strong>Result:<\/strong> 5.0 cm.<\/li>\n<li>With the same q, the span increases from 4 to 6 m. By what factor\ndoes the simplified M_max change? <strong>Result:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mn>6<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">{\\left(\\frac64\\right)}^2=2{,}25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/li>\n<li>What quantity is missing if we want to convert 480 W into energy\nconsumption in kWh? <strong>Answer:<\/strong> at least a duration; in\nreal buildings, additional dynamic influences as well.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The beam formulas apply to a <strong>statically determinate,\nstraight, simply supported beam with a constant line load<\/strong> under\nthe relevant assumptions of elementary beam theory. The building physics\nformula applies as a steady-state approximation. Design in accordance\nwith standards, safety verification and building energy planning are\nseparate specialist tasks and require qualified professionals.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Documented source status:<\/strong> 10 November 2026<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://tu-dresden.de/bu/architektur-landschaft/studium/module\">TU\nDresden – Module handbooks for architecture and landscape\narchitecture<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>: Academic context for descriptive geometry, structural\ndesign, building climatology and construction.<\/li>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://www.cee.ed.tum.de/bm/lehre/interactive-apps/\">Technical\nUniversity of Munich – Teaching examples from the Chair of Structural\nMechanics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>: Examples of beam bending and fundamental mechanical\nmodels.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><em>The sources provide subject-specific orientation. The structure,\nnumerical values and explanations were newly developed for this learning\ncourse; no illustration was reproduced from the sources.<\/em><\/p>\n<\/article>","university_mathematics_en:M4-L14":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L14\">M4-L14 – Mathematics for aviation, spaceflight and navigation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Choice of study: AI, computer science and specialised fields<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Not specified<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> An elective and applied field at the\nintersection of applied mathematics, physics and aerospace engineering.\nThis text introduces mathematical models; it is not a guide to actual\nflight operations, navigation or mission planning.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Guiding question:<\/strong> Why does an aircraft need lift,\nwhy does a satellite not simply fall to Earth, and why does a spacecraft\nneed a carefully planned change in velocity to transfer to another\norbit?<\/p>\n<p>Aerospace engineering problems bring together several mathematical\nlanguages: vectors describe position and velocity, differential\nequations describe motion, trigonometry describes angles, probability\ndescribes measurement uncertainty, and optimisation deals with scarce\nresources such as fuel.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Velocity is a vector<\/h2>\n<p>An aircraft may travel north while the wind blows eastwards. Its\nvelocity relative to the ground is the <strong>vector sum<\/strong> of\nits velocity relative to the air and the wind velocity. For a teaching\nmodel, we assume:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Airspeed: <strong>200 km/h northwards<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Wind: <strong>30 km/h eastwards<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>In a two-dimensional coordinate system, the ground velocity vector is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=(30,200)\\,\\mathrm{km/h}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts magnitude is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mn>200<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>202<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{30^2+200^2}\\approx202{,}2\\,\\mathrm{km/h}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe direction of travel relative to the ground deviates eastwards by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arctan<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mn>30<\/mn><mn>200<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mn>5<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\arctan\\left(\\frac{30}{200}\\right)\\approx8{,}5^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>This result already shows that a speed without a specified direction\ncan be insufficient in navigation problems. The example uses constant\nvectors and neglects actual flight control, time-varying winds and\ngeodetic effects.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Lift as a model of physical quantities<\/h2>\n<p>A commonly used idealised lift equation is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=0{,}5\\cdot\\rho\\cdot v^2\\cdot S\\cdot C_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>Here,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ρ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes air density,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe speed relative to the air,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\na reference area and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\na dimensionless lift coefficient. For\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=1{,}2\\,\\mathrm{kg/m}^3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>,\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=50\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>,\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=10\\,\\mathrm m^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>\nand\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_L=0{,}8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>,\nwe obtain <strong>12,000 N<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>This is a worked example, not a statement about a particular\naircraft. The lift coefficient is not constant; it depends on factors\nsuch as geometry, angle of attack and flow conditions. The presence of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v^{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the equation therefore does not mean that lift in reality always\nincreases by exactly a factor of four when the speed doubles.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Gravity has not disappeared in orbit<\/h2>\n<p>A satellite in low Earth orbit keeps moving while Earth’s\ngravitational attraction curves its trajectory. In the idealised\ntwo-body model for a circular orbit, gravity provides exactly the\nrequired centripetal acceleration. It follows that:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mfrac><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm{kreis}}=\\sqrt{\\frac\\mu r}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the distance from the <strong>centre of Earth<\/strong>, not the\naltitude above Earth’s surface.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrepresents the gravitational effect of the central body.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original worked example:<\/strong> We choose the rounded value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>398<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_{\\mathrm{erde}}=398\\,600\\,\\mathrm{km}^3/\\mathrm{s}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand an orbital radius of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>778<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=6\\,778\\,\\mathrm{km}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(approximately 400 km above the Earth model used here). The idealised\ncircular orbital speed is then approximately <strong>7.67 km/s<\/strong>.\nSuch a large value illustrates why spaceflight operates on different\nscales of speed and energy from ordinary travel on the ground.<\/p>\n<p>The orbital period in the circular model can be estimated using\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msqrt><mfrac><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mi>μ<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=2\\pi\\cdot\\sqrt{\\frac{r^3}{\\mu}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the example orbit, this gives approximately <strong>92.6\nminutes<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-mathematics-en/media/img/000084_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Two circular orbits and an elliptical transfer orbit in an idealised two-body model\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two circular orbits and an elliptical\ntransfer orbit in an idealised two-body model<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Independently constructed schematic illustration. Radii are\nschematic; not a real mission plan.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The vis-viva equation relates position and orbital\nspeed<\/h2>\n<p>A body does not travel at the same speed everywhere along an\nelliptical orbit. Under the assumptions of the two-body model, this can\nbe described using the <strong>vis-viva equation<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mn>2<\/mn><mi>r<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>a<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v^2=\\mu\\cdot\\left(\\frac2r-\\frac1a\\right)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>Here,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the semi-major axis of the ellipse. When a spacecraft is closer to\nthe central body, its orbital speed on the same bound ellipse is greater\nthan at a more distant point. This is not a coincidence; it is related\nto the conservation of mechanical energy.<\/p>\n<p>For a circular orbit,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a = r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe equation reduces to the expression used earlier,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v^2=\\frac\\mu r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>An orbital transfer requires Δv<\/h2>\n<p>In the simplified case, a <strong>Hohmann transfer<\/strong> is an\nelliptical transfer orbit between two circular, coplanar orbits. In the\nidealised model, the spacecraft briefly changes its velocity at two\npoints. The first change takes it from the inner circular orbit onto the\nellipse; the second adjusts its velocity to match the outer circular\norbit.<\/p>\n<p>For the radii\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe semi-major axis of the transfer ellipse is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t=\\frac{r_1+r_2}{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>The orbital speeds in the initial and target circular orbits are\nobtained using\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mfrac><mi>μ<\/mi><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{\\frac\\mu{r_1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mfrac><mi>μ<\/mi><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{\\frac\\mu{r_2}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe speeds on the ellipse are calculated at the two points of tangency\nusing the vis-viva equation. The differences are the two <strong>Δv\nmanoeuvres<\/strong>. In our teaching example, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>778<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_1=6\\,778\\,\\mathrm{km}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>378<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_2=7\\,378\\,\\mathrm{km}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nboth steps can be calculated. The total ideal change in velocity in this\nmodel is approximately <strong>0.32 km/s<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Caution:<\/strong> This is a highly simplified transfer\nbetween two circular orbits in the same plane, not a generally optimal\nflight trajectory. Perturbations, orbital inclination, engine\ncharacteristics, trajectory tracking and actual propulsion phases alter\nthe real requirements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why the choice of departure time matters<\/h2>\n<p>When a spacecraft arrives, two planets or satellites are no longer\nwhere they were at departure. <strong>Travel time and relative\npositions<\/strong> are therefore key quantities. In its introduction to\nspaceflight, NASA explains that transfer orbits and encounter geometry\nmust be planned together. In practical mission analysis, trajectories\nare therefore predicted and suitable time windows identified.<\/p>\n<p>Mathematically, this takes us from simple Keplerian orbits to the\nnumerical integration of differential equations. In a more complex\ngravitational field or under continuous thrust, the flight can no longer\nbe fully described by a single elementary formula.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Measurement errors and navigation<\/h2>\n<p>A position measurement is not an absolute truth. It may contain\nsystematic deviations and random noise. Imagine two sensors estimating\nthe same distance:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sensor A reports <strong>100.0 m<\/strong>, with an assumed standard\ndeviation of <strong>2 m<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Sensor B reports <strong>104.0 m<\/strong>, with an assumed standard\ndeviation of <strong>4 m<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Under the explicitly simplified assumption of <strong>independent,\nunbiased, normally distributed measurement errors<\/strong>, an\ninverse-variance-weighted mean is appropriate:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mfrac><\/mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mfrac><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat x=\\frac{\\frac{x_A}{\\sigma_A^2}+\\frac{x_B}{\\sigma_B^2}}{\\frac1{\\sigma_A^2}+\\frac1{\\sigma_B^2}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>For the example values, this gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat x=100{,}8\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe more precise sensor receives a greater weight because its variance\nis smaller. In actual navigation, it is also necessary to check whether\nmeasurement errors are correlated, whether the distribution is\nappropriate and whether both sensors are measuring the same physical\nquantity at all.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What mathematical models can and cannot do<\/h2>\n<p>A model can explain an order of magnitude, a possible cause or a\ndesign alternative. However, its conclusions cannot go beyond what its\nassumptions allow. A numerically precise value for a satellite orbit is\nnot an actual flight plan; a simplified lift value says nothing about\naerodynamic stall, manoeuvring limits or structural margins.<\/p>\n<p>Practical specialist fields include flight mechanics, fluid\nmechanics, navigation, control engineering, multibody dynamics,\nnumerical optimisation and mission analysis. The presentation here\ndeliberately connects only a few central ideas.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Learning path and brief self-assessment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> School mathematics M1-L20–School\nM1-L21 (trigonometry and vectors), School M2-L11–School M2-L14 (vector\nproducts and spatial geometry), M1-L9 (differential equations), M2-L12\n(mathematics for robotics) and, depending on your interests, M2-L9\n(Fourier analysis).<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Why must the distance to the <strong>centre<\/strong> be used in the\nsatellite equation? Because the central gravitational model refers to\nthe distance between the centres of mass involved.<\/li>\n<li>Why does an orbital manoeuvre change the subsequent orbit rather\nthan simply the current position? Because velocity and position together\ndetermine the future state of motion.<\/li>\n<li>What happens in the inverse-variance-weighted mean if one sensor\nbecomes very uncertain? Its weight decreases.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Documented source status:<\/strong> 10 November 2026<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://science.nasa.gov/learn/basics-of-space-flight/chapter3-3/\">NASA\n– Basics of Space Flight, Chapter 3: Gravity &amp; Mechanics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>:\nKeplerian orbits as a fundamental physical model.<\/li>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://science.nasa.gov/learn/basics-of-space-flight/chapter4-1/\">NASA\n– Basics of Space Flight, Chapter 4: Trajectories<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>: An accessible\nintroduction to transfer orbits.<\/li>\n<li><a href=\"https://elib.dlr.de/143300/\">DLR – Fundamentals of orbital\nmechanics, bibliographic entry<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>: Overview of spaceflight mechanics\nand mission applications; the actual publisher’s text was not\nreproduced.<\/li>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://www.dlr.de/de/irs/ueber-uns/abteilungen/systemanalyse-raumsegment/abteilungsaktivitaeten\">DLR\n– Mission analysis<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>: Context for mission design, transfer orbits and\nfurther constraints.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><em>The sources provide orientation on specialist fields and\nequations. The structure and worked examples were developed\nindependently.<\/em><\/p>\n<\/article>"};
